\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Gamblers Sit Out India Budget-HEARD ON THE STREET\b0\par\par\uc2 1,634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2010\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] Budget time in India usually a season of tall wagers, Tooth Fairy-like hopes. Not this time - Sensex +1% over past two weeks; daily trading volume on BSE has been nearly half its recent averages in run-up to Friday's budget. There are some good reasons for investors to hold back in 2010; they were burned during both budget announcements in 2009 - stocks tumbled as New Delhi first fell short of expectations for big stimulus plan, then did little to advance widely expected economic reforms. Moreover, past expectations centered on handouts, more government spending. This time, forecasters looking for New Delhi to roll back stimulus, focus on narrowing massive fiscal deficit -- 6.8% of GDP in FY10. But even short-sellers have reason to be wary; fiscal discipline rhetoric a rarity in India, where governments usually loath to pull back spending. If Delhi surprises again it'll be with less of a pullback than currently expected - a boon to stocks. That makes this budget a riskier gamble than ever. See: "=HEARD ON THE STREET: Gamblers Sit Out India's Budget Season." (HAR)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +101.4% at HK$2.453 billion at interval, 7.1% of market total, vs 5.0% Monday's half-day, suggests HSI may give back gains near-term after its 3.7% gain over past 1.5 sessions. China Life (2628.HK) leads with HK$301.20 million, then A50 (2823.HK) with HK$280.31 million, Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$215.51 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$105.86 million and \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$100.68 million. (SUT)\par \par \uc2 0601 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  expects government debt to cheapen next FY (10-year yield may go up to 8.5%) as bond supply jacks up, with demand hit in absence of RBI buying and likely tightening of monetary policy. Estimates effective net supply of central and state government bonds will be up between 3%-10% on an on-year basis. "With banks unlikely to be big demanders except to maintain a certain minimum excess stock of SLR as insurance for borrowing liquidity from RBI via its repo window; the key swing factor will be demand from insurers and other long term investors." Tips potential excess supply of between INR500 billion to INR1.2 trillion under various scenarios, given pattern of historical 'normal' demand from insurers and long term investors. (SDS)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.5% at 7597.44, marking 2nd-straight session of gains after week long Lunar New Year holiday, highest since Jan. 29, following Taiwan posts positive economic data announced yesterday. Taiwan 4Q GDP growth hit 5.5-year high at 9.22%, January jobless rate at 1-year low 5.68%, while government also revises 2010 GDP view to +4.72% vs +4.39% previous forecast. Still, volume thin at NT$91.29 billion, as investors stay on sidelines ahead of US Fed Chairman Bernanke's semi-annual testimony on Wednesday, says Taiwan International Securities assistant VP Andrew Teng. Tips Taiex in 7500-7670 band tomorrow; volume tipped to remain thin throughout this week before investors getting details of any U.S. monetary policy shifts. \uc2 Evergreen Marine\uc2  (2603.TW) +6.8% at NT$18.90 after Economic Daily News today reports company will raise freight charges on Europe bound shipping services from March 1. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) down 2.7% at NT$34.60. (WUM)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF surges to 1.4684 from 1.4630 earlier in session; 2 dealers in Tokyo say short-term speculators' buying on vague talk SNB is in market again. "I haven't seen the name on the screen myself, but there are many investors who are talking about (SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) now," says senior dealer at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank; pair last at 1.4667; tipped in 1.4640-1.4690 range for now. (TMO)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.4% 715.31 at midday pause, with across-the-board gains; optimism on economic outlook after robust GDP numbers yesterday still aiding sentiment. "It looks like the market is coming back a bit, there are some good valued companies out there so some think it's time to step in," says senior trader with local brokerage. Whether upside is sustainable remains to be seen ahead of Friday's Thaksin court case. Resistance at 718, support at 710. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +2.2% at THB232.00, \uc2 PTT Exploration and Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.5% at THB137.50, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +0.8% at THB534.00, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +3.0% at THB77.25, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) +3.4% at THB12.30. (LHM)\par \par \uc2 0537 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,116.40/oz, up $3.80 since New York close; now in consolidation phase ahead of Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's biannual testimony before Congress, which begins Wednesday. "The base has been built so all it needs now is Bernanke to dampen rate expectations and we can look to move higher," says Singapore based trader. Adds, no direction from \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so gold just trading in tight range. ScotiaMocatta technical analysis puts short term support at $1,098, upside resistance at $1,143. (JAC)\par \par \uc2 0537 GMT [Dow Jones] India overnight call rates tad up, likely steady as not many borrowers in market, says state-run bank dealer; call rates at 3.35% vs 3.30% Monday close on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's electronic trading platform; collateralized borrowing and lending obligation (CBLO) last at 3.05%. "The mutual funds are in the CBLO market but it seems banks are covered for cash. CBLO rates should start drifting down later in the day," says dealer. Tips call rates in 3.30%-3.35% range while CBLO to slip to 2.50%-2.75% in session. \uc2 Kotak Mahindra Bank\uc2  says liquidity likely to be surplus in week with about INR237.33 billion of outflows against likely inflows of INR694.26 billion. Outflows to include about INR120 billion through second round of 25 bps cash reserve ratio hike, to take effect February 26, says house. (NAC)\par \par \uc2 0534 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2  says, based on QUICK consensus estimates, FY 2010 recurring profit for TSE 1st tier companies (excluding financials) is projected to jump 52.7% on-year. Says manufacturers look especially strong (recurring profit to soar 90.1%), as basic materials and processing/assembly sectors will likely lead the way with robust growth. Adds, non-manufacturers should also fare well (recurring profit projected to climb 21.5%), with many sectors expected to enjoy double-digit profit gains. Says with exception of pharmaceuticals, air transports, all 33 TSE sectors should see recurring profit gains or move into the black. Projections may bode well for fundamentalist approach to Japan stock investing, as market has been buffeted thus far in 2010 by powerful external factors including yen strengthening, US economic recovery doubts, European debt worries, and Chinese credit tightening. (BMF)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] Weaker-than-expected Singapore January CPI shows price pressures remain relatively benign, provide no immediate cause for any sharp policy shifts, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Robert Prior-Wandesforde says. "I don't think there's anything to keep MAS officials awake at night in terms of reflationary pressures." But notes probably too early to see pick up in economic demand feed through to prices. "It normally takes a good 12 to 18 months or even more before a strong pick up in demand feeds through to core inflation." That said, MAS April meeting at this point remains "close call" given the pressures for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act pre-emptively in its policy in face of accelerating regional demand. (SRH)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.1% at 2561.923 midday in light volume, off earlier low of 2539.385, as selling in select bank and automotive blue chips keeps index in red despite bargain buying by foreign funds. "Foreign funds have almost turned to be net buyers after being net sellers most of the morning session, lifting the main index from its low," says trader at Reliance Securities; expects bargain buying later in session to drive index to positive territory. Foreigners still net sellers of IDR890 million vs IDR40 billion earlier session. Bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 1.8% at IDR8,350, off IDR8,200-low, while Bank Danamon (BDMN.JK) down 1.9% at IDR5,100, rebounding from IDR4,975-low. Consumer goods producer \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UNVR.JK) +3.1% at IDR11,550 on bargain buying. (EDH)\par \par \uc2 0508 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2  says short-term Japanese stock market likely to see buyers and sellers playing tug-of-war, with signs of monetary tightening by the US and China sparking investor concerns, and prospects for weaker yen heightening expectations for a rapid earnings recovery (earnings upgrades) by Japanese firms. "Although short-term catalysts imply range-bound trading for the time being, we believe buying interest triggered by brighter prospects for earnings recovery will gradually outweigh selling pressure over the long term." Expects Japanese stocks to become increasingly attractive compared to stocks in other major markets, with firms positioned to benefit from consumer spending in Asia gaining particular attention. Adds, key Japanese economic indicators slated for announcement in coming week include Jan. balance of trade and exports on Feb. 24, consumer price index (Feb. for Tokyo, Jan. for nationwide), Jan. industrial production and housing starts (Feb. 26) [ 02-23-10 0107ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100223e62n0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
