\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HK Short Sales +55.5% Midday; China Life Leads\b0\par\par\uc2 1,512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2010\par 07:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +55.5% at HK$4.571 billion at interval, 8.2% of market total vs 6.8% Thursday's half-day, as sentiment more bearish after index falls below 20,500 (down 2.5% at 20,332.51 midday), while next support of 20,000 eyed near-term. China Life (2628.HK) leads with HK$267.55 million, then HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$263.40 million, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0005.HK) with HK$232.81 million, H-share index ETF (2828.HK) with HK$231.99 million and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0939.HK) with HK$217.05 million. (SUT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals contracts on Shanghai Futures Exchange sharply lower amid worries over possible further tightening of monetary policy by China, says Yong An Futures analyst Wu Jianjian; adds, President Obama's proposal to limit bank sizes, risks they can take also dents market sentiment. Says, "concerns over Beijing's tightening measures have been plaguing players since Wednesday, and very likely base metals will suffer (from that) for some time." Tips nearby support for copper at CNY55,000/ton on chart-based cues. Benchmark 2010 May copper contract now down 3% at CNY58,960/ton, benchmark 2010 May zinc contract now down 4.1% at CNY19,890/ton. (LIY)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bond auction likely to go through smoothly with cutoffs expected at near-market levels, say traders; 2020 floating rate bond cutoff yield likely at 4.73% according to median estimate of 5 dealers in Dow Jones poll. Cutoff yield for 7.32% 2014 bond forecast at 7.13%, while cutoff yield for 8.28% 2032 bond estimated at 8.29%, according to poll. Dealers say this time no surprises expected on cutoff of floating rate bond as participants comfort level with bond grows; strong probability of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity tightening at policy meet Jan 29 should ensure strong demand for instrument free of interest rate risk. Private bank dealer says upside surprise in price at auction, if any, would come in floating rate bond. RBI to sell INR70 billion of government bonds later in session.(BIJ)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI expected to hike cash reserve ratio by 50 bps to drain excess liquidity, but unlikely to change key policy rates as core inflation, private investment remain weak, says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Economy.com; says raising rates would have "unwelcome consequences at this stage of the (economic) recovery." Notes, biggest argument against monetary tightening is sluggish investment, which is essential to remove infrastructure bottlenecks that constrain economic growth, contribute to persistently high inflation. Adds, higher interest rates would have minimal impact on supply-driven food price inflation. Instead, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, administrative measures better tools to control current inflation. RBI to review monetary policy Jan. 29. (AVK)\par \par \uc2 0559 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY now under 90.00, but any type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for time being despite Finance Minister Naoto Kan's remark favoring a weak JPY at first press conference as he tones down afterward, says Ross Schaap, senior analyst at Euroasia Group. "A replay of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pre-March 2004 massive yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not in the cards," as "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the potential to spark political conflict with the U.S. over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation." Adds, Japan firms now make goods overseas, meaning they are less sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  so they won't pressure on government to push up USD/JPY. "Kan is now on record stating that markets should determine the exchange rate, and he will very likely continue to hold this line." USD/JPY earlier hit 1-month low of 89.78.(TMO)\par \par \uc2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end mixed; most-active 5-year bond higher on Taiex's 2.5% decline as money flows to safer havens, but gains limited on banks' profit-taking, says local brokerage trader. Still, 2-year bond, where foreign investors typically park their short-term funds, lower; yield surges 5.6 bps to end at 0.3010% on foreign fund outflows. 5-year yield ends at 0.8985% vs 0.9013% yesterday, lowest since Dec. 23, tipped in 0.8800%-0.9100% band Monday. In when-issued trade, 20-year bond ends flat at yesterday's 2.1020% level in thin volume, as traders stay on sidelines ahead of 30-year bonds auction Monday, which tipped to be auctioned in 2.3800%-2.4100% band vs 2.3760% in when-issued trade late Friday; "20-year bond may rise after 30-year bond auction, as gap between 30-year yield and 20-year yield is very close," expected to cause worse-than-expected 30-year bond auction, he says. (WUM)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] India overnight call rates flat on ample liquidity, banks borrowing in CBLO at lower rates, says dealer with state-run bank; overnight call rates at 3.30% unchanged from Thursday's close, CBLO now at 3.12%. "Banks are holding ample government securities and therefore borrowing in CBLO at attractive lower rates," says dealer. Tips call rates to move in 3.25%-3.30% range, CBLO to slip to 2.90%-2.80% levels later in day. (NAC)\par \par \uc2 0544 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 2.5% at 7927.31, lowest since Dec. 23, marking 5th straight session of losses, biggest daily point-loss since Nov. 27, tracking Asian bourses, Wall Street, says Michael Lin, trader at Fubon Securities. Tips index biased up next week after week-long correction if U.S. shares rebound. "The correction (in Taiex) may come to an end Monday on short covering," Lin says. Adds, economy on track to recovery, optimistic sentiment about better-than-expected 1Q guidance by major techs next week remains unchanged. Losses across board but led by electronics (down 2.7%), financials (down 2.5%). Chip designer MediaTek (2454.TW) fell 4.9% at NT$523 after downgrade by \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  to Equalweight from Overweight. Cathay (2882.TW) down 2.7% at NT$56.80 as trade pact talk with China shows little progress so far. Tips Taiex in 7900-8000 band Monday. (CHY)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares down 1.4% at 708.94 at midday pause as falls across regional bourses weigh on sentiment. Crude price weakness, together with political uncertainty ahead of planned anti-government protest also hurting, says dealer with foreign brokerage. "But overall it's holding up pretty well really, particularly when you look at the region." Support at 702, resistance at 715. Among most active, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 3.4% at THB570.00, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) down 2.4% at THB24.90, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) down 3.7% at THB1.30, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) down 2.5% at THB11.90, \uc2 Thai Airways\uc2  (THAI.TH) down 2.2% at THB18.00. (LHM)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY to remain under downward pressure as long as recent trend of non-Japanese investors' buying of Japanese shares continues, says Kimihiko Tomita, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at State Street. Says share trade itself doesn't have much direct impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors also sell some JPY to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, yet volume of trade large enough to attract JPY buying from short-term speculators, he says. Adds, "trade is especially active when the Nikkei 225 falls as they often buy on dips," he says. Nikkei 225 is now down more than 2%, USD/JPY at 89.97 vs 90.33 in NY trade Thursday. (TMO)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1092.90/oz, down 20 cents, silver flat at $17.37/oz but platinum at $1,562/oz, down $34. "Platinum is getting sold on Tocom. I think some of the longs are taking profit ahead of the weekend as no one knows what the U.S. will trade like tonight after the Obama speech," says HK based metal trader. President Obama indicated Thursday that actions would be taken to curb proprietary trading at commercial banks in the U.S. Tocom December 2010 platinum at Y4,519/gram, down Y255. Spot platinum now down 5.5% from Wednesday's intraday and 17-month high of $1,652.50. (JAC)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei March Futures down 2.3% at 10,590 after hitting intraday low of 10,510; Japan futures market impacted by overnight U.S. stock market fall, dragged down by U.S. Obama administration's plan to put new limits on size of banks/bar commercial banks that take deposits from customers from proprietary trading, says Tamotsu Numazaki, manager at Traders Securities. While Japan market has been rising on foreign buying recently, "it's not like this trend is ending because of the U.S. banking plan, but it is triggering profit-taking for now," he says. Adds futures may trade between 10,500-10,800 next week. (AYT) [ 01-22-10 0119ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100122e61m00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
