\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: Yen Will Fall Without Kan's Help\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2010\par 09:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Kan can get a weaker yen without market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In fact, he is already doing that.\par \par \uc2 See the yen's weakness against the dollar here\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3250"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3250}}}\par \par \uc2 Since his appointment two days ago, Japan's new finance minister has already paved the way for further losses in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not only through his comments supporting a weaker yen but also through increased talk of new fiscal and monetary measures that will make the yen even less attractive.\par \par \uc2 Kan, the country's deputy prime minister who was swiftly appointed to replace the retiring Hirohisa Fujii, initially took the market by surprise as he laid out his thoughts on the yen at his first press conference on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Not only did he say it would "nice" for the yen to weaken further but he noted that many Japanese companies think the dollar should be around Y95 and that he will cooperate with the Bank of Japan to achieve this.\par \par \uc2 This prompted immediate speculation that Kan will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lifted the dollar even further from the 15-year low it hit a month ago under Y85.\par \par \uc2 However, despite the bluntness of Kan's remarks, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to change the non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy that has prevailed since its last such exercise in March 2004.\par \par \uc2 As Tohru Sasaki, an analyst with \uc2 JP Morgan Chase Bank\uc2  in Tokyo, pointed out: "The reason why the Japanese government was forced to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a structural one, not based on individuals or parties' thoughts [or] policy."\par \par \uc2 In other words, the previous LDP government didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar fell as far as Y87, so why should the current DPJ administration do it when the dollar is over Y90?\par \par \uc2 Kan has since been forced to tone down his remarks after Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama said the government shouldn't comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even so, Kan should be able to achieve the weaker yen he seeks without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His appointment has coincided with more talk from Tokyo of government spending to help avoid a double-dip downturn in the economy. If that is the case, then Japan's continued fiscal profligacy--which will push its fiscal deficit even higher at a time when most other governments are addressing a reduction in deficit ratios--will play into the hands of yen sellers.\par \par \uc2 Kan's pledge to work with the Bank of Japan may also hint at further pressure on the bank's officials to provide additional market liquidity, as opposed to being a direction from him for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Such a move would probably drive Japanese yields, which are already down at their 2005 lows, even lower and reduce support for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even further.\par \par \uc2 According to Nicole Elliott, technical analyst at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in London, Kan could also find technicals on his side. She reckons that a dollar close above Y95 for the week could indicate the end of a "bear mode."\par \par \uc2 Couple this with the potential wedge-formation that has been forming since April last year and a massive short-covering could take the dollar back up to Y110, Elliott forecast.\par \par \uc2 Early Friday in Europe, most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were largely unchanged as they waited for the new U.S. non-farm payrolls at 1330 GMT, hoping for the first positive showing in nearly two years. The consensus is for a fall of 10,000 last month after a decline of 11,000 in November.\par \par \uc2 Despite Kan's softening of his call for a weaker yen, the dollar traded flat at Y93.28 at 0745 GMT compared with late Thursday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The euro was up at $1.4332 from $1.4320, reflecting a slight decline in risk appetite after China tightened its monetary policy--indicating a shift in focus from growth to inflation concerns.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also up at Y133.74 from Y133.57.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right n