\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(1/8) Kan Era In Japan Begins With Jolt\b0\par\par\uc2 919 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2010\par 20:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -- Japan's new finance minister began his job with a jolt Thursday, swaying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and debt markets with proclamations that he favored a weaker yen and more government spending to keep Japan's weak economy from sliding back into recession.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Naoto Kan's comments at his first news conference in his new job were unusually blunt, stretching the boundaries of what is often considered appropriate for a top finance official.\par \par \uc2 He also continued his campaign to pressure the Bank of Japan to keep monetary policy loose -- comments that have sometimes been seen as challenging the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence. "With help from monetary-policy steps, I'd like to avoid the economy from falling into a double-dip recession," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kan, 63 years old, was tapped this week to succeed the 77-year-old Hirohisa Fujii, who resigned suddenly, four months into his job, citing high blood pressure and fatigue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kan has long been known as an iconoclastic leader willing to test political conventions. He follows a finance minister who came into office supporting the market's strong valuation of the yen and trying to impose some limits on government borrowing.\par \par \uc2 Finance officials generally avoid detailed discussions of exchange rates -- and in particular try to avoid endorsing specific exchange values. But Mr. Kan suggested to reporters he favored an exchange rate of about 95 yen to the dollar, a weakening of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below the current rate around 92 yen.\par \par \uc2 "There are a lot of voices in the business world saying that [the dollar] around 95 yen is appropriate in terms of trade," he said. "From that perspective, I'll first examine various impacts that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rate movements could have on the Japanese economy, and in cooperation with the Bank of Japan, I will make efforts to deal with individual cases in order to bring the exchange rate to appropriate levels."\par \par \uc2 While noting that the yen had already weakened in recent weeks, he added: "I'm hoping the correction will make a bit more progress, making the yen weaker."\par \par \uc2 Following Mr. Kan's remarks Thursday, the dollar rose a touch more than a yen from its level late Wednesday in New York, hitting a four-month high of 93.40 yen.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen hurts Japan's best-known exporters, by making their products more expensive abroad and reducing overseas profits in yen terms. \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 ., for one, predicts its operating profit will fall by 30 billion yen a year for every one-yen appreciation against the dollar. A strong yen also can worsen deflation by pushing down import prices.\par \par \uc2 On fiscal policy, Mr. Kan said his priority is stimulating growth, not reining in Japan's public debt level, despite the fact that public borrowing is the highest of the industrialized nations at 180% of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 "Looking at economic conditions" after the collapse of U.S. investment bank \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in September 2008, "I've never thought that tightening fiscal policy should be allowed," Mr. Kan said.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year Japanese government bond yield rose to 1.345% following Mr. Kan's comments, its highest mark since Nov. 16. The level compares with 1.330% at the start of Thursday's trading. Bond yields rise as prices fall.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kan's exchange-rate remarks fueled speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time since 2004. "It's a strong message, particularly for overseas players," said Hideaki Inoue, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Trust & Banking Corp\uc2 . "Speculation is growing that the government will be more likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market" if the yen rises sharply.\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Kan's words had a noticeable impact on his first day in office, it is unclear he will be able to have any lasting impact on exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 "Talk is cheap, and I think in the end the financial markets will find it difficult to believe in [Kan] as a driving force behind any depreciation in the yen," said Yunosuke Ikeda, a senior yen strategist at Nomura in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 With Japanese interest rates already very low, there is little scope for further monetary easing -- an important component of a successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, Mr. Ikeda said.\par \par \uc2 What's more, he said, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would almost certainly be unable to clinch the international support it would need to take such a step. "The U.S. is very unlikely to accept a real \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially as the U.S. dollar is now recovering and as the political relationship between the U.S. and Japan is worsening," he said. He said he expected the dollar to trade around a range of 85 yen to 90 yen throughout the year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kan's comments mark a change of tune from those of Mr. Fujii, who said at the start of his tenure that a weak yen is undesirable. They also highlight the difficulty the Japanese government faces in trying to improve the economy in the face of a strong yen that weighs on exports. The yen rose as high as 84.82 to the dollar in November, a 14-year high. It has weakened since then, but remains at a high level compared with recent years.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Katie Martin in London contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 07, 2010 14:28 ET (19:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100107e617004qy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
