\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 7-New Japan finmin Kan seeks weaker yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2010\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 7 (Reuters) - Japan's new Finance Minister, Naoto Kan, called on Thursday for a weaker yen and said he would work with the Bank of Japan to get it to an appropriate level.\par \par \uc2 The yen slid after his comments, which were seen as unusually specific for a finance minister, despite Kan's past comments that had been seen as less hawkish on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than his predecessor. ****************************************************************\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2 -- "It would be nice if the yen weakened a bit more," Kan, who is also deputy prime minister, told his first news conference after his appointment, adding an appropriate level was needed given the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's impact on the economy.\par \par \uc2 -- Kan says many Japanese firms in favour of dollar/yen around 95 yen\par \par \uc2 -- Japan must avoid a second recession with help of monetary policy, Kan says.\par \par \uc2 -- Kan says he wasn't in favour of fiscal austerity in the just completed 2010/11 annual budget. In the future, the need for expansionary budgets will depend on how many goals are met in the government's long-term growth strategy, he says.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2 YASUO YAMAMOTO, SENIOR ECONOMIST, MIZUHO RESEARCH INSTITUTE\par \par \uc2 "Kan has been a proponent of weak yen, though I think he is not trying to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the market may be cautious that he emphasised the difference between him and Fujii and that a \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take place once the yen becomes stronger... The market psychology may be such that it would become more difficult to continue purchasing the yen."\par \par \uc2 GARETH BERRY, \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, \uc2 UBS\uc2 , SINGAPORE:\par \par \uc2 "USD/JPY jumped in response to his comment that he wants the yen to weaken more, suggesting 95 as an appropriate USDJPY level. Kan also suggested that a review of the budget would be appropriate, which raises the risk that JGB issuance could exceed the previous 44 trln yen ceiling.\par \par \uc2 "We believe ... political considerations played a part in his appointment, and Prime Minister Hatoyama himself acknowledged that the appointment was made so that Japan's budget policies will be able to continue unhindered, as Kan was 'closest' to budget policy formation. We continue to see USD/JPY at 95 in 1 month."\par \par \uc2 ROB SUBBARAMAN, ECONOMIST, NOMURA INTERNATIONAL, HONG KONG:\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese economy is in pretty weak shape at the moment. It has been hit harder than the other major economies even though it did not have a financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "So it does show you Japan is struggling -- it's not just growth that is a problem, it is back in deflation. There is a growing need for looser policies hence the statements from the new finance minister."\par \par \uc2 "The economy had a bit of support from fiscal policies in the second half of last year but we see that fading and the economy falling into a relapse in the first half of this year. We see growth slowing again and it will impel the BOJ to be innovative and there will be a step up in quantitative easing (QE).\par \par \uc2 "Japan was hit hard because its domestic industry and demand have been weak and it has become more dependent on exports. The country's policy response has not been as effective as elsewhere -- interest rates were very low, QE was not as big as elsewhere and the yen went the wrong way, it appreciated." [20100107 080919 GMT]\par \par \uc2 TIM CONDON, HEAD OF ASIA RESEARCH, ING, SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ policy is basically to have zero price inflation and they have managed to deliver that. I don't think the BOJ sees the need for any further policy accommodation on their side. So I am sceptical of any change in monetary policy that would necessitate the weakening of the yen."\par \par \uc2 TETSUYA MIURA, CHIEF MARKET ANALYST, MIZUHO SECURITIES, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "There are two implications for JGBs coming from Kan's comments. That he wants to work with the Bank of Japan is positive for the bond market. But on the other hand, he is blunt about wanting a weaker yen and this could put upward pressure on long-term yields. All in all his stance could add to the steepening pressure on the yield curve."\par \par \uc2 AZUSA KATO, ECONOMIST, BNP PARIBAS, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "The markets saw the previous finance minister Fujii as prepared to put up with a stronger yen. But I think the Democratic Party knew all along that a strong yen was not good for the economy at this point. A change of finance minister was a good opportunity to make known their view that they would prefer a weaker yen.\par \par \uc2 "On fiscal policy, nothing seems to have changed. Kan has an image as being somewhat looser on this, which might make a difference if the budget was still being compiled. But that is over now, so it is more a question of how they fight with the Liberal Democratic Party."\par \par \uc2 SATORU OGASAWARA, ECONOMIST, CREDIT SUISSE, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "As Kan takes a slightly different approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , investors may be discouraged from pushing the yen higher. But this does not mean that the market trend will be changed completely as it is decided by economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 "Kan is known to make sharp comments when it comes to parliamentary debate but it is uncertain whether he is good at communicating with markets and it may take time for markets to fully understand his ability.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ will clearly be prone to more political pressure and Kan is likely to increase pressure for more action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen resumes its rise and heads towards 85 yen to the dollar again."\par \par \uc2 OSAMU TAKASHIMA, CHIEF \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  ANALYST, BANK OF TOKYO-MITSUBISHI UFJ, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "Given Japan's economic condition, it's good Kan made clear he wants the yen to weaken more. But given Japan's relations with other countries, it would be hard for Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "I won't be surprised, under the current trend in the market, if the dollar rises to around 94 to 95 yen."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KATZ, ECONOMIST AT FORECAST, SINGAPORE:\par \par \uc2 "It appears that he's influenced by Ozawa re fiscal policy, and certainly his rhetoric is aggressively expansionary compared to Fujii.\par \par \uc2 "His last comment that he never believed fiscal austerity is good is particularly interesting for the yen and bonds. It appears that he may drop Fujii's insistance on keeping FY10 issuance under the self imposed 44 trillion yen cap.\par \par \uc2 "This suggests to me that he will not be afraid to increase JGB issuance and then push the BoJ into quantitative easing to keep yields from spiking, so I'd say that combined with his comment that he'd prefer dollar/yen at 95 it is pretty obviously to going to weigh on yen.\par \par \uc2 "It may also add to selling pressures seen in JGBs but may keep the Nikkei supported through softening the yen and boosting stimulus expectations.\par \par \uc2 GLENN MAGUIRE, ASIA CHIEF ECONOMIST, \uc2 SOCIETE GENERALE\uc2 , HONG KONG:\par \par \uc2 "I don't think he is talking about reactivating an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It clearly would be helpful if the yen weakened a little bit so the Japanese can share in some of the robust growth that some of its trade competitors like South Korea and Taiwan have enjoyed...There is clearly a change of tone that reflects the deterioration of the economic environment since the DPJ was swept to power. They were swept to power on a pledge to cut public spending and maintain fiscal discipline but that has been overtaken by events.\par \par \uc2 "There's little doubt Japan will have to do more on fiscal policy. It will have to spend more, it will have to issue more JGBs. To finance that, the Bank of Japan will have increase its purchases of those and these are conditions that would necessarily suggest a weaker yen."\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROBINSON, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ANALYST, SAXO CAPITAL MARKETS, SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "The comments were rather explicit, especially for a first-time news conference.\par \par \uc2 "While the market was anticipating that this was the kind of theme he would adopt, I think the absolute tone has and will continue to force USD/JPY higher and drag other yen crosses along with it.\par \par \uc2 "Bearing in mind that the market was generally looking at a weaker yen, the move was sharp and expect it to continue at least for the rest of the day."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 -- The dollar surged to the day's high of 92.87 yen <JPY=>, up from around 92.20 yen just before the comments. <FXNEWS> [FRX/]\par \par \uc2 -- March 10-year JGB futures <1JGBv1> traded at 138.82, extending losses in the evening session. [JP/] <FINEWS>\par \par \uc2 -- For stocks click [.T], for the Nikkei share average click <.N225>\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2 -- Kan was named on Wednesday to replace 77-year-old Hirohisa Fujii, who stepped down for health reasons. He is a founder, along with Hatoyama, of the ruling Democratic Party (DPJ) and is known as a champion of reducing the policy clout of bureaucrats but he lacks experience in budget and tax issues.\par \par \uc2 -- Kan, 63, is among the most vocal critics in the cabinet of the Bank of Japan. He has criticised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic view as too rosy and pressured it to do more to support growth.\par \par \uc2 -- Kan remains economics minister but gave up his portfolio as national strategy minister.\par \par \uc2 -- A former finance ministry bureaucrat, Fujii often served as the voice of fiscal restraint and had spoken of the need to eventually ease Japan off its dependence on exports and let the yen rise as the economy became more domestically focused. [20100107 080946 GMT]\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/KAN (INSTANT VIEW 7)|LANGEN|Table|J|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|UKI|JAPAN-ECONOMY/KAN (INSTANT VIEW 7)=2|LANGEN|Table|J|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|UKI|chained   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100107e617000fe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
