\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  between Nov 27-Dec 28 -MOF\b0\par\par\uc2 159 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2009\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 30 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from Nov. 27 to Dec. 28.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have stayed out of the market for more than five years. The last time they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen was in March 2004, when they conducted yen-selling, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a 14-year low of 84.82 yen <JPY=> on Nov. 27 and market sources said on that day that Japanese authorities had called banks to check dollar/yen rates, a move which could have been aimed at sending a warning against sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has risen about 8 percent to above 92.00 yen since hitting that low. (Reporting by Satomi Noguchi; Editing by Charlotte Cooper)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|J|ABN|M|D|NAT|E|RBN|U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100102e5cu00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
