\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Five political risks to watch in Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chisa Fujioka   \par \uc2 1,114 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2009\par 03:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 15 (Reuters) - Japanese Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama, his popularity slipping after nearly three months in office, faces headaches as he tries to keep the economy from tipping back into recession while cutting huge public debt.\par \par \uc2 He is also under pressure to smooth policy rifts in a coalition with two small but vocal parties and to ease voter worries over a dispute with Washington about a U.S. military base before an election for parliament's upper house in mid-2010.\par \par \uc2 Following is a summary of key political risks to watch:\par \par \uc2 * FISCAL DILEMMA\par \par \uc2 The new government is caught between the need to keep stimulating the economy to avoid slipping back into recession and the need to address concern among investors and voters about Japan's ballooning public debt, approaching 200 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Bond yields are rising as sliding tax revenues mean government income now covers less than half of spending. Efforts to cut budget waste to find funds for new programmes such as childcare benefits have fallen short of target, while spending requests from cabinet ministers have been rising, making it hard for Hatoyama to limit new bond issuance in 2010/11 to below the 44 trillion yen earmarked for this year.\par \par \uc2 Investor worries over the Democrats' ability to manage the economy have been one factor behind the underperformance of Japanese shares compared to U.S. and European shares.\par \par \uc2 Longer term, Japan faces the mounting welfare costs of the world's fastest-ageing population. Hatoyama has promised to keep Japan's broad sales tax at 5 percent for the next four years.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- The budget for 2010/11 is due by the end of this year and the focus is on how the government will balance campaign pledges to put money in the hands of consumers with tackling falling tax revenues and the bulging public debt.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say major policy changes may risk disappointing voters ahead of the upper house election if they are not explained well, although a recent poll by the Yomiuri newspaper showed only 18 percent of voters insist on pledges being kept.\par \par \uc2 * GOVERNANCE, COALITION HEADACHES\par \par \uc2 The Democrats' drive to give politicians more control over policy-making than bureaucrats has exposed rifts among cabinet ministers and confusion over who makes decisions.\par \par \uc2 Hatoyama also faces policy gaps with two tiny parties whose backing he needs in the upper house to enact laws smoothly.\par \par \uc2 People's New Party chief and Financial Services Minister Shizuka Kamei, an outspoken proponent of big spending, is already pressing for a beefed up 2010/11 budget.\par \par \uc2 The pacifist Social Democratic Party has threatened to leave the coalition if its views are ignored in resolving the dispute between Japan and top security ally the United States over a U.S. military base on Okinawa island.\par \par \uc2 Washington wants to go ahead with a 2006 plan to move a Marine air base on Okinawa, but Hatoyama may delay a decision.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- The Democrats will need to win an outright majority in next year's upper house election to ditch their two coalition partners and focus on policy priorities of their own.\par \par \uc2 -- With support low for the main opposition Liberal Democratic Party, the Democrats look strong heading into the election, but Hatoyama's public support has been slipping from highs of more than 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 -- Delay in resolving the Futenma row could sour U.S. ties and disappoint voters who want Hatoyama to show leadership.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  INDEPENDENCE?\par \par \uc2 The government, wary of big fiscal spending in the face of huge debt, has put pressure on the Bank of Japan for a stronger policy response to deflation and the risk of a new recession. The BOJ began withdrawing from credit markets in October but caved into government pressure this month by offering banks more short-term funds, leaving the door open to yet more demands.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is independent by law but is required to communicate closely with the government to align policies.\par \par \uc2 Government comments also tend to sway market expectations for future monetary policy moves, making it hard for the BOJ to act against those expectations.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Persistent deflation could put the BOJ under pressure to buy more government bonds or revert to quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 -- A big output gap, falls in planned capital spending and the probability of prolonged deflation mean the BOJ will probably keep interest rates ultra-low for several years.\par \par \uc2 * YEN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should not be manipulated out of line with economic fundamentals, and has made comments favouring a strong yen that echo the government's desire to wean Japan from dependence on exports for growth.\par \par \uc2 Still, the government appears reluctant to see the yen soar too quickly and endanger \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fragile, export-led recovery. Fujii has said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an option if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves too far, too fast.\par \par \uc2 Much of the yen strength relates to a sagging U.S. dollar against a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the yen rises with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that may give the government pause but a big yen move on its own, perhaps because of the unwinding of risky investments elsewhere that are funded in yen, could prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Attention will be on comments by government officials regarding possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Picking a level that would trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tricky.\par \par \uc2 -- The market is unlikely to get too worried about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless it becomes more volatile and the yen nears its all-time high of 79.75 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 * FUNDING SCANDALS\par \par \uc2 Political funding scandals have cast a cloud over Hatoyama and his predecessor Ichiro Ozawa, now Democratic Party secretary-general.\par \par \uc2 A recent poll by the Yomiuri newspaper showed 85 percent of voters were unhappy with Hatoyama's explanations over misreported funds, although 54 percent said Hatoyama need not resign even if his former secretary is charged in the affair. Japanese media have said prosecutors would likely decide soon whether to charge the former secretary, who was in charge of record-keeping.\par \par \uc2 Political analysts say the scandal's impact depends on whether more damaging details emerge from prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- The opposition Liberal Democratic Party could badger Hatoyama on the scandal in parliament next year, but its own lawmakers are involved in funds scandals too.\par \par \uc2 -- A big fall in support ahead of the upper house election could put pressure on Hatoyama to resign, since polls show voters back the Democrats for policies rather than for his popularity. (Editing by Andrew Marshall)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA000002