\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Kospi Down 0.1%; Off Lows On Solid Techs, Autos\b0\par\par\uc2 1,651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2009\par 05:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi down 0.1% at 1625.94 in thin trade led by financial, commodity stocks; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Woori Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2  (053000.SE) off 2.0% at KRW15,000, Posco (005490.SE) down 0.9% at KRW562,000. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ssangyong Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (003620.SE) down 8.4% at KRW3,290 after news overseas creditors still object to Ssangyong's revised turnaround plan, dampening hopes for court's approval of plan Friday, say analysts. But most bluechip techs, autos remain solid, preventing sharp Kospi correction despite bearish mood in regional markets; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  (005930.SE) +0.1% at KRW773,000, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (005380.SE) +1.4% at KRW106,500. Among gainers, Daewoo Shipbuilding (042660.SE) +4.7% at KRW16,700, Kumho Industrial (002990.SE) +2.3% at KRW11,050. Kospi likely to drift around current level, supported by program buying. (SKS)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan core machinery orders, regarded as leading capex indicator, likely fell 4.2% on month in October, according to poll of economists by Dow Jones, Nikkei. Such a result would be worst since 9.3% drop in July. Despite jumping 10.5% in September, orders likely still struggling to regain surer footing as many firms reluctant to pour money into plant and equipment with much excess capacity, uncertainty over final global, domestic demand remaining, analysts say. Also likely to continue dragging on orders ahead is deflation, which increases real interest rates, making firms' capex-related borrowing costs more expensive. Data earlier Wednesday showed weaker than expected July-September capex behind a sharp downward revision to that quarter's GDP, which government now says expanded mere 0.3% on quarter, much lower than preliminary estimate of 1.2% expansion. October machinery orders data due at 2350 GMT. (ATM)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex opens lower tracking negative global cues; index down 0.4% at 17,162.31 with 21 of 30 issues down. Losses led by metals and banks; most autos also trading lower. "We maintain our stance to lighten long positions and increase cash levels as stocks and indexes find it hard to make new highs," says \uc2 Ashok Jainani\uc2 , vice-president, \uc2 Khandwala Securities\uc2 ; tips support for index at 17,049. \uc2 Sterlite Industries\uc2  (500900.BY) down 1.7% at INR848, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) down 0.8% at INR865.55, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) down 0.7% at INR1,073. S&P CNX Nifty down 0.4% at 5,128.35. (SHB)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] Keen interest in S-chips making up for slack in Singapore bourse, with China plays accounting for much of market's overall volume of 1.17 billion shares by midday. "It's an S-chip day today. Potential dual listing candidates are seeing strong interest," says chartist at foreign brokerage. FTSE ST China Index +0.2% while STI off 0.2% at 2801.05 midday. But having bounced off morning low of 2784.64 (down 0.7%), STI's downside for rest of session may be limited, although any upside may be small given lack of positive leads, pullback in most other Asian markets. "Often at this time of the year, many investors wonder whether to neutralize or close down positions for the year. But many are also worried that if they do, they may miss a year-end rally," says Macquarie. Following \uc2 China XLX Fertiliser\uc2 's (B9R.SG) strong debut in Hong Kong yesterday, investors plowing into other S-chips on hopes these companies will also pursue dual listing. Most active stocks include \uc2 Ying Li International\uc2  (5DM.SG) +4.7% at S$0.675 on 71.4 million shares, \uc2 China Milk Products\uc2  Group (G86.SG) +7.8% at S$0.345 on 57.7 million, China XLX off 1.3% at S$0.76 on 50.5 million, Sinotel (D3W.SG) +2.4% at S$0.635 on 40.0 million, \uc2 China Hongxing Sports\uc2  (BR9.SG) +2.6% at S$0.20 on 31.9 million. (FKH)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] Macquarie trims 12-month Topix target to 1,050 from 1,140, reflecting accumulating risks flowing from policy passivity. Also says market has yet to discount ongoing recovery in corporate earnings. Says while previous global recoveries have been associated with TOPIX rising around 40% on-year, "this time it appears the rally in Topix has been cut short," citing proximate explanations such as policy inactivity in face of deflation/yen strength, deflation-corroding earnings revisions, issuance indigestion, abusive issuance (highlighting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's corporate governance issues). Adds, aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen/fight deflation should lead to decline in the cost of Japan government bonds' CDS (a measure of sovereign risk), thus positively impact fair value of Topix. (BMF)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT [Dow Jones] A second major gas find in the South China Sea by Husky Energy (HSE.T) is likely to be met by cheering in the boardrooms of other companies prospecting there just as the area was beginning to lose its luster, with efforts there largely disappointing. Other companies prospecting there include \uc2 Anadarko Petroleum\uc2  (APC) and \uc2 BG Group\uc2  (BG.LN), which plan to drill deepwater wells there in 2010; and also at \uc2 Devon Energy\uc2  (DVN), which now has more leverage in its planned sale of three blocks. See: CHINA ENERGY WATCH: New Husky Gas Find To Set Pulses Racing (SGH)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares tad lower, tracking losses in regional markets while concerns of unrest at large anti-corruption protests in Jakarta hurting sentiment; main index off 0.2% at 2478.264 vs 2470.853 session low. Traders tip 2,430-3,500 band. Consumer names lead decline with carmaker \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) down 1.3% at IDR33,400, bellwether Telkom off 1.1% at IDR9,380, \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UNVR.JK) slips 0.9% to IDR11,200. Still, Buying in resource, energy plays offering support; Astra Agro (AALI.JK) +1.1% at IDR23,600 on report 11M09 CPO output +10%; Bumi Resources (BUMI.JK) +1.0% at IDR2,500 on report of coal marketing tie-up with unlisted Berau Coal; Bakrie Sumatera (UNSP.JK) at IDR700, unmoved by news of plan to take over unlisted Domas Agrointi; "there are so many reports on Bakrie-related companies and the market is getting fatigued; they'll wait to see if it really happens," Sucorinvest director says. Tanker operator Berlian Laju (BLTA.JK) yet to react to statement will form offshore oil/gas services venture, stock last steady at IDR680. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds steady; dealer says bonds moving not more than 2 bps either way in quiet trade. "Players are starting to wind down for the year with the last auction of the year already announced (yesterday)," says dealer. Adds, auction results of February 2012 MGS due Monday. Yields for February 2015 MGS +1 bp at 3.75%, May 2013 MGS flat at 3.22%, November 2019 down 1 bp at 4.24%. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 32.288 midday vs 32.350 yesterday on export settlements, rebound in local shares (Taiex turns higher, last +0.1%), says local bank trader. Still, expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend 32.250 support; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still prefers a weak local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to support exporters, he says. Tips USD/TWD in 32.260-32.300 band for rest of session but notes pair biased down this week following strong November export/import data Monday. (CHY)\par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares still lower midday, tracking Wall Street losses. Shanghai Composite Index down 1.6% at 3243.21, tipped to hover around current level rest of session. "Investors are so cautious after recent gains that they sell stocks when there is any bad news. Today it's the weakness in the overseas markets," says Simon Wang at \uc2 Guoyuan Securities\uc2 . Metal, financial stocks lead losses with Yunnan Copper (000878.SZ) down 2.0% at CNY31.54, Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) down 1.3% at CNY82.79, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) down 2.7% at CNY17.34, China Life Insurance (601628.SH) down 2.2% at CNY31.97. Shenzhen index down 1.3% at 1213.32.(WYL)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds open higher as finance minister quashing of concerns that government may up borrowing for fiscal Tuesday propelling market rally, says state-run bank dealer; 6.90% 2019 bond at 96.49 vs 96.42 last close. "The benchmark note, however, may underperform as traders dump it on concerns that it may lose that 10-year tag once the new calendar year begins," says dealer; tips yield on both 6.35% 2020 bond (now 6.50%), 2019 bond (now 6.41%) to target 6.40% over session as traders shift into 2020 note as they dump current 10-year benchmark. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 0512 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 1.5% at 9989.08, fresh intraday low, after declines in Chinese stock markets sour sentiment, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  market analyst Seiichi Suzuki. Says Nikkei may fall more for rest of session, possibly to around 9900, with some traders taking cautious stance ahead of Bank of Japan tankan survey due next Monday. Still, "Short-term players are moving the market now, so data on fundamentals don't really trigger big stock movements," he says; notes impact of weaker-than-expected Japan revised 3Q GDP limited. 31/33 Topix subindexes lower, with financials weak, brokerages (down 4.0%) by far worst performer on board; \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO) down 4.6% at Y663. Daiwa Securities Group (8601.TO) down 3.1% at Y472. \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group (8411.TO) \uc2 down 3.6% at Y160. MUFG (8306.TO) off 4.8%. (JUO) [ 12-08-09 2350ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091209e5c90005v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
