\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: US Treasurys Higher As Equities Weaken\b0\par\par\uc2 1,492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2009\par 11:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1008 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys move higher in London Monday amid weaker European equity markets following Friday's sharp sell-off in sovereign debt markets after the much better than expected nonfarm payrolls report. Monday's data include Conference Board employment trends index due at 1500 GMT but the main event will be Fed chairman Bernanke's speech at 1745 GMT. Also, the Fed's Dudley speaks at 2245 GMT. No supply is due Monday but heavy supply hits the markets later in the week with three, ten and 30-year US Treasury issuance scheduled. The Treasury yield curve flattens, with the two-year/ten-year benchmark spread narrowing to +261.7 bps from Friday's +263.8 bps. The two-year note is up +/32 at 99 27+/32, yielding 0.822%, the ten-year up 8/32 at 99 14/32, yielding 3.442%, with the 30-year bond up 3+/32 at 99 24+/32, yielding 4.388%. Bunds more higher while gilts are unchanged with March bunds up 0.19 at 122.72 and March gilts at 116.83. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite recent hints \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has more to lose than to gain by any such move, \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ strategist Naomi Fink writes. Not only are some finally realizing "the protective effects of yen strength in an environment of liquidity-fueled commodity gains," but greater reliance since the Lehman shock on short-term debt, which has outpaced gains in yen value of Japan's foreign reserves, "exert(s) upward pressure on the marginal rate of interest on every additional unit of foreign reserves...In short, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is much costlier" than it would have been previously. Adds that weakening yen to boost Japan exporters may be risky strategy in new economic landscape where strong revival of U.S.-led demand isn't assured: "Even if U.S. orders do eventually rise," Fink writes, "if they do not rise predictably, the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Japanese exporter revenues could be comparable to shooting in the dark." (MSA)\par \par \uc2 0951 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian forint weakens slightly as European stock markets turn negative. The EUR/HUF is at 269.40 after opening close to the 268.15 level seen Friday afternoon. It's hard to see which direction the HUF is taking, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. One thing is for sure: that the regular rally seen around Dec. 5 is canceled, it adds. Reflecting the uncertainty, Raiffeisen Bank says that the EUR/HUF could dip below 266 later this week if global sentiment returns to positive. (MAF)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's "better-than-expected (November) exports will strengthen the local economy," says Industrial Bank of Taiwan economist Yi Chia. Tips island's 4Q GDP to return to on-year growth. November exports rise 19.4% on-year to US$20.02 billion on-year vs October's 4.7% drop, first on-year growth since August 2008, on surge in shipments to China, stronger demand from U.S. Growth larger than median 16.2% gain forecast by 9 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. Taiwan November imports also post first on-year growth since Sep 2008, +17.9% on-year to US$17.90 billion, beating survey's forecast of 10.7% gain. (WUM)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] Swiss retail sales have been surprisingly robust, \uc2 Bank Sarasin\uc2  economists Alessandro Bee says, referring to yearly 3.1% increase in real terms. However, rising Swiss unemployment will likely keep the lid in any further recovery of consumption in the next months, he notes. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist Fabian Heller agrees the data are relatively strong, also points to recent 3Q GDP data confirming the resilience in private consumption. However, the yearly change is very volatile and subject to statistical adjustments, he adds. (MGE)\par \par \uc2 0938 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premium little changed on narrow spot move; 12-month premium at 1.21 vs 1.22 Friday. "Given the stable money market rates, premium would continue to track the spot moves within 46.00-46.75 (while watching 12 month forward dollar within 47.50-48.00)," says \uc2 Moses Harding\uc2 , head of global markets group at \uc2 IndusInd Bank\uc2 . Notes, with immediate direction of rupee move into 46.50-46.75 range, premium expected to move into lower end of 2.25%-2.50% range for 3 months, 2.65%-2.90% for 6 months and 2.50%-2.75% for 12 month contracts. Tips exporters can await 12 month forward dollar move into 47.85-48.00 while importers should not chase current weakness having hedged 1 to 3 month payables on spot move into 46.05-46.20. USD/INR spot rate at 46.57 vs 46.29 late Friday. (AVK)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] Wednesday's pre-budget report (PBR) is one of the key events of the week, with the UK likely to come out with the largest budget deficit, as a percentage of GDP, of all the G20 in 2010, says Jim Reid, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  strategist. "The Chancellor will need to persuade the market and the rating agencies that there is a credible plan to significantly reduce the deficit in the years ahead." Furthermore, the report will be an indication of how other countries will aim to tax financial institutions in the years ahead. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0933 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's November CPI shows there's still strong deflation pressure, "so the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely to hike the (key) interest rate" in a hurry, says Yi Chia, economist at Industrial Bank of Taiwan; tips \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike in mid-2010. November CPI posts 1.59% on-year drop, marking 10th straight month of drop due to falling food prices; decline also much larger than 1.0% drop predicted by 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Still, drop smaller than October's revised 1.87% decline on higher transportation costs. November's WPI +0.84% on-year vs October's revised 6.21% drop vs expected 0.1% drop on higher prices of chemical materials, crude oil, coal products. (WUM)\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/GBP is on the slide again and testing the key 0.9030 support area. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says trade below that point would signal a deeper pullback toward 0.8980 is underway, while topside, only a move above 0.9155 would trigger a fresh bullish signal. EUR/GBP now at 0.9035. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end down 1.1% at 2483.758 in light volume, with selling by local funds in commodity-related stocks after recent gains; index still up 83% since start of 2009. "It's a healthy correction but continued buying by foreign funds could (indicate) the general trend remains bullish until the end of the year," says trader at Valbury Securities; expects shares to continue to fall tomorrow but puts support at 2470. Foreigners net buyers of IDR285 billion in shares. Among decliners, gold, nickel miner Antam (ANTM.JK) off 5.3% at IDR2,250, coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) off 2.0% at IDR2,500, Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) off 3.7% at IDR3,950.(EDH)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI ends down 0.4% at 1265.36, dragged lower in last minutes of trade after trading in tight 1267-1275 range for most of day. "The benchmark closing below support at 1267 (38.2% Fibonacci retracement of 1233-1288 rally) is significant as it suggests greater underlying weakness. The benchmark may head towards support at 1252 (50-DMA) later this week if selling momentum accelerates," says one dealer. "The bearish divergence on (KLCI's) MACD remains a concern. (If) the 30-DMA is violated, and its 50-DMA is (breached), next downside target is at 1233," says CIMB. To the upside, 1270 support turns resistance. KLCI December Futures also trading at 9.0 point discount at 1258.0, underscoring bearish sentiment. Among decliners, \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) down 0.6% at MYR8.95, CIMB (1023.KU) down 0.5% at MYR12.82, AMMB (1015.KU) down 2.2% at MYR4.92. On upside, Plus (5052.KU) +1.6% at MYR3.27, Genting (3182.KU) +1.1% at MYR7.38. (VGB)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT [Dow Jones] Yen selling has further to run, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . USD/JPY has already bounced off 85 - a level that tempts corporates to buy USD. Capital outflows could also accelerate, given the chances of divergence in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy rates and Nikkei underperformance. "Over time, we believe it is increasingly likely that Japan will once again wrestle the title of world funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the USD," bank says. USD/JPY now at 89.99. (KJM) [ 12-07-09 0508ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091207e5c70006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
