\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Good Opportunities In Selling Yen - Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,099 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2009\par 10:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0919 GMT [Dow Jones] Yen selling has further to run, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 . USD/JPY has already bounced off 85 - a level that tempts corporates to buy USD. Capital outflows could also accelerate, given the chances of divergence in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy rates and Nikkei underperformance. "Over time, we believe it is increasingly likely that Japan will once again wrestle the title of world funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the USD," bank says. USD/JPY now at 89.99. (KJM)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades higher at 25.726-25.70 late Friday as euro recoups some of last week's losses. EUR/CZK tracks moves on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region and ignores better-than-expected Czech October trade surplus. "The koruna is expected to stay in its current range Monday and track moves elsewhere (in the) region," a local trader says, adding that investors are seen waiting for Nov. CPI data due Wednesday. (LEO)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar has pushed broadly higher in European trading, extending the gains it made Friday after a stronger-than-expected employment reading, pushing the euro under $1.48 for the first time in a month. Sterling is also lower, trading below $1.64. The buck is also higher against Canadian, Australian and New Zealand dollars. However, further gains against the yen are proving tough to make. Exporter selling in USD/JPY is offering a near-term barrier around Y90, where the dollar is now trading. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty edges down after touching 4.0355 against the euro Friday, a level not seen since January, a dealer says. "We'll need to stay in this new area for a bit," he says, adding the EUR/PLN looks set to trade in a tight 4.05-4.08 range Monday. EUR/PLN at 4.0770 from 4.0555 late Friday; USD/PLN at 2.7596 from 2.7130. Average yield on 2-year Treasury bonds now at 4.88% from 4.87% Friday; 5-year unchanged at 5.63%; 10-year at 6.17% from 6.18%. (MKR)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] The decline in Hungary's industrial output is expected to have slowed in October to 12% year-on-year from September's 15% fall, according to a poll of 10 economists by Dow Jones Newswires. The range of forecasts calls for a decline of between 7.4% and 14%. Export output will be eyed but any improvement would be seen as fragile. The recovery of Germany, Hungary's key export market, will remain vital given that domestic consumption is in the doldrums. Data are due at Tuesday at 0800 GMT. (MAF)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher late but off peak of 9,460 tapped early as suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  curtails upswing; Bank Indonesia spotted selling small amount, likely $25 million-$50 million, between 9,460-9,465, dealers say; rebound in some rivals vs USD buoys IDR but profit-taking sets in late trade amid lack of domestic catalysts. Dealers tip 9,420-9,480 range tomorrow, last 9,450 vs 9,415 late Friday. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY's strong close Friday signals a base is now in and technically gains to 92.32 can be expected says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . The spot did run into profit taking and exporter offers in Asia Monday however with Japan's officials now sheltering downside risks the bank looks for it to edge higher and has revised its year end target to 90.00. USD/JPY now trades at 89.95. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0856 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM data show that short USD positions in the week to Dec. 1 were at their highest level since March 08, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . That means the market is "vulnerable to a corrective rally in the USD." Net long JPY was the most popular position in G10, having overtaken AUD longs on Nov 24. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far, but with the Nikkei and government approval both waning, a dip back to 85 could push the authorities into action, bank says. GBP remains the only short position vs USD in G10, bank says. Adds that it sees downside risks to GBP this week. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/NZD could be poised to touch 1.30 for the first time since late 2000, according to \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Bank expects the RBA to maintain its hawkish tone when governor Stevens speaks Tuesday, and sees an upside surprise to employment data Thursday. RBNZ, by contrast, is expected to keep rates on hold Thursday, and it could even talk down rate hike expectations. Cross trades at 1.2793. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0853 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF has defended key support at 1.5000 and 1.5070 and is expected to move in a tight range between 1.5070 and 1.5250, Sarasin says. A break below 1.5070 would signal a test of a key support 1.5000. Alternatively, a break above 1.5250 would change trend to positive. Stands at 1.5108. (MGE)\par \par \uc2 0853 GMT [Dow Jones] USD is gaining ground pretty much across the board Monday, however, it's struggling against the JPY. USD/JPY printed a one-month high of 90.78 after Friday's US payrolls and peaked at 90.45 in Asia trade Monday. However, so far in Europe there seems to be plenty of offers, some reportedly from Japanese accounts ahead of 90.00. USD/JPY at 89.95. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts edge lower Monday after Friday's sharp fall following US nonfarm payrolls. The gilt market awaits this week's PBR, two BOE buybacks and the BOE meeting plus the positive jobs data from the US is expected to keep influencing the markets. Monday's data is light, with only BOE narrow money due in the UK at 0930 GMT. In Europe, ECB president Trichet speaks and US Fed chairman speaks at 1745 GMT. The BOE is scheduled to buy GBP1.7 billion gilts in the March '2020 to September 2034. The gilt yield curve flattens marginally, with the 2-year/10-year benchmark spread tightening to +252.6 bps from Friday's +253.3 bps. The March gilt futures contract is currently down 0.05 on the day at 116.78. The 10-year benchmark gilt is down 0.02 at 106.165, yielding 3.705%. (NKA) [ 12-07-09 0419ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091207e5c700060\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
