\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,170 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2009\par 12:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY, SPAIN COMPLETE GOVT BOND ISSUANCE;EYES ON ECB, 2010 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Next week, Germany and Spain will join those countries in the euro zone that have already completed their scheduled bond issuance for this year. (published 1134 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN LOWER HOUSE APPROVES 2010 EUR8.5B FISCAL STIMULUS PLAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's lower house of parliament Friday approved a bill that seeks to pump an additional EUR8.5 billion of fiscal stimulus into the economy next year. (published 1048 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BUBA LIFTS 2009,2010 GDP FORECAST;CAUTIOUS ABOUT 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The German Bundesbank Friday revised up its forecasts for economic activity and inflation in 2009 and 2010, but remains less optimistic than private-sector economists about 2011 growth. (published 1029 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA: TO KEEP WAY OF MANAGING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RESERVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China on Friday sought to reassure the outside world it is taking a conservative approach to managing its massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, with the government saying it hasn't made big changes to its strategy and the U.S. dollar remains a central asset. (published 1034 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN 2009 BUDGET GAP SEEN 3% OF GDP, 6% 2010-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany is expected to record a budget deficit of 3% of gross domestic product in 2009 and 6% in 2010, Handelsblatt reports Friday, citing documents prepared for a budget committee meeting next week which were seen by the newspaper. (published 0938 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN POSTPONES UNVEILING OF STIMULUS PLAN TO NEXT WEEK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government Friday postponed to early next week its planned announcement of a fresh economic stimulus package as ruling coalition lawmakers squabbled over how much should be spent in the latest effort to bolster the slumping economy. (published 0923 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S DEPUTY PRIME MINISTER CALLS FOR WEAKER YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's deputy prime minister said Friday he wants the yen to weaken "a bit more", a highly unusual comment that underscores how concerned policymakers are about a potential rebound in the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0319 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRESH STIMULUS IN JAPAN WORTH AROUND Y24 TRILLION IN EFFECTS -KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's fresh economic stimulus package for the current fiscal year will likely have an effect worth around Y24.3 trillion, Kyodo News reported Friday, citing government sources. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPEC LIKELY TO KEEP CURRENT OIL OUTPUT UNCHANGED-GULF OFFICIAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ministers from the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries will stress greater compliance when they meet in Angola later this month, but the oil-producer group will likely keep output unchanged for now, a senior Gulf official said Friday. (published 1009 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA REGULATOR: ASKED BIG BANKS TO UP CAPITAL RATIO TO 11% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has asked big banks to raise their minimum capital adequacy ratio to 11% in an effort to lower the risk of an increase in bad loans after a lending binge in the first half of the year. (published 1127 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RUSSIA FUND FLOWS QUINTUPLE IN WEEK TO DEC. 2 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Flows into Russian funds increased more than five-fold in the week to Dec. 2 from the previous week, leading other BRIC countries in terms of capital inflow for the period, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  reported Tuesday, citing data from Emerging Portfolio Fund Research. (published 0944 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPANISH OCT INDUSTRIAL PRODUCTION -9.2% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish industrial production showed further signs of stabilization in October after falling sharply in the first half of the year. (published 0800 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALY 2010 CAR SALES SEEN FLAT WITH INCENTIVES -STUDY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian new car sales could remain flat next year at 2,160,000 units if the government extends a package of incentives for the purchase of new, less polluting cars, according to an influential think tank. (published 0946 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STRONGER-THAN-EXPECTED KOREA GDP PROMPTS RATE DEBATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's economy grew at a faster pace than initially thought in the third quarter, data issued Friday showed, rekindling debate on when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should raise rates. (published 0335 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PROBLEM UK COMMERCIAL REAL-ESTATE DEBT DOUBLES - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of U.K. commercial real-estate debt in default or in breach of key lending agreements more than doubled to about GBP30 billion in the first six months of the year, the Financial Times reports Friday, citing a study. (published 0719 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN CREDIT DEFAULT SWAPS FALL ON WEAKER YEN, MAY EXTEND DECLINES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's benchmark index for credit default swaps fell sharply this week as a weaker yen alleviated fears about the outlook for the export-driven economy and damped demand for protection against bankruptcy risks. (published 0907 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA PM ADVISER: EASY POLICY MAY HAVE TO BE WITHDRAWN SLOWLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India may have to withdraw its accommodative monetary policy slowly, a top policy adviser to the government said Friday. (published 0551 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA GOVERNMENT: TO INITIATE REFORMS TO SHRINK FISCAL GAP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's federal government will initiate reforms to shrink a yawning fiscal deficit and bring it under control, the Finance Ministry has said. (published 0449 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR MIXED; SENTIMENT UNCERTAIN AHEAD OF DATA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is mixed and the euro is weaker in Europe Friday as the market waits for the latest U.S. non-farm payrolls. (published 1030 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/USD STILL HEADS TO 1.5145 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD falters just shy of the 2009 high at 1.5145 following the cap at 1.5142 Thursday. Dips need to be kept above 1.5027 in order to prevent leaving 1.5142 as a bull failure, so while 1.5027 remains intact, there is room for upside pressure on the 1.5097/1.5102 lower intraday highs. (published 0805 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: EURIBOR BASES AT 98.740 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Consolidation of the recent strong 120.66/124.06 advance touched 122.92 Thursday before staging a good recovery. The failure to even test the projected 122.76 level highlights the lack of bear momentum, and regaining ground above 123.44 would bring the focus back to the upside. (published 0837 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS SIGNAL EURO MAY REVERSE YEAR'S RALLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro has been on a seemingly relentless climb against the dollar most of the year, but the options market is now signaling a pullback is increasingly likely. (published 0701 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: WHAT IF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WERE TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ON THE YEN? \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With all the "will they, won't they" talk of late about whether Japanese authorities will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell the yen, it's worth considering whether any market action would have long-lasting impact. (published 0406 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS MAY FALL ON US JOBS DATA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options could edge down early next week if a key U.S. employment report shows that fewer jobs were lost in November, prompting players to further trim their dollar downside hedges. (published 0338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: HEARD ON THE STREET: THE OTHER STEEPENING CURVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For government-bond traders, this year has been all about the steep yield curve, which has provided booming profits. (published 0023 GMT) [ 04-12-09 1155GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091204e5c4000vt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
