\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan Bonds End Down; Caution On Local Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 1,750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2009\par 07:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0617 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan bonds end slightly down in light volume, tracking weak U.S. Treasuries, also on investor caution before local government elections Saturday, says local brokerage trader. 5-year yield closes at 0.8930% vs 0.8905% last close, erasing earlier gains from banks' buying; key reopened 10-year yield at 1.4522% vs 1.4436% last close. "Investors will remain cautious this week before the election results come out," he says. Tips tight range movements tomorrow with 5-year yield in 0.8800%-0.9000% band, reopened 10-year yield in 1.4400%-1.4600% band. (CYH)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2  says margin trading patterns currently indicate a clear lack of USD buying interest. Says over past month, open buying interest has grown, particularly in Aussie dollar and euro, whereas interest in the U.S. dollar has fallen sharply. And that despite fact that USD has not only not gained against the yen but has also actually lost ground in line with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the context of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  margin trading, says such movements look to be signaling growing belief that USD "is entering a period of increasing weakness." Notes, overall \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  margin market unit price has fallen by around 8% from its Sept. high but moves to cut rapidly rising exchange losses have not as yet increased to point where non-yen weakening against the yen and establishment of "short non-yen/long yen" positions, based on the reversal of existing positions, is fueling multiplier effect. Thus far, JPY strengthening, especially vis-a-vis USD, cited as major catalyst in Japan stock falls, albeit without prolific mention of USD weakness as result of outright dearth of USD buying interest. Such condition may be harder to correct, even given unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government-sponsored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  programs aimed at helping prop up \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market. USD/JPY currently trading 87.85/87.87 vs high of 87.93, well off recent low of 84.82 seen several days ago. Nikkei closed up 3.8% at 9977.67, best percent and point gains since May 7. (BMF)\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia keeps key rate unchanged at 6.50% as expected; accompanying policy statement doesn't break any new ground, simply rehashing previous statements that rate at current level consistent with growth, inflation targets going forward. Analysts expect rate hikes to start early next year as oil, commodity prices rise while domestic economy rebounds; mild November CPI print gives BI more leg room in deciding when to hike, with analysts mostly predicting first rise in late 1Q or early 2Q; "another reason for steady policy (today and going forward) may be that BI wants to give bank lending rates a little more time to fall further," as average commercial lending rates down only 130 bps in current cycle in which BI chopped 300 bps from key rate, DBS says; tips \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on hold until end-2Q before lifting rate. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0603 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares finish virtually unchanged at 7684.67, as investors less eager to chase stocks higher or cashing out before Saturday's elections of local city and county chiefs. "The index has recovered more than 200 points from its week low, and pretty much every sector has benefited from this, so some investors are paring holdings, for profit-taking or simply out of caution ahead of the elections," says Dennes Chang, vice president at \uc2 Jih Sun Securities\uc2  Investment Trust, tipping 7600-7700 range for tomorrow. Among sectors, financials gain most with 1.0% rally, led by Cathay's (2882.TW) 3.0% rise to NT$58.80 after Reuters reports, citing unnamed sources, that company talking to China's ICBC for stake sale, which also lifts other bank names; Chinatrust (2891.TW) +3.1% to NT$19.85. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) finishes unchanged at NT$34.55 after hitting intraday high of NT$34.60. (PEL)\par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1% midday at 700.81 in moderate trade. SET rebounds after 2.3% fall yesterday following disappointment over court ruling on suspended industrial projects. "There are still many unclear issues regarding the projects that remain suspended. So, I don't think the SET could stand well above 700 level be the end of the day," says Aphisit Limsupanark, strategist at BFIT Securities. Court yesterday lifts suspension on only 11 of 76 projects at Map Ta Phut industrial estate. Support for day pegged at 690, resistance at 705. Among most active, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.9% at THB227, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.1% at THB133.50, IRPC (IRPC.TH) +3.5% at THB4.14. \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) down 0.9% at THB221. (PPP)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.5% midday at 2,483.781 in light volume, led by gains in commodity and consumer-related stocks; resistance remains at 2,500. Still, "in the morning trade, the main index has been moving in a very limited range ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meeting on interest rates," says trader at \uc2 Kresna Securities\uc2 ; expects main index to stay in positive territory after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to key leave interest rate unchanged at 6.5%, as expected. Among gainers, gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) +3.3% at IDR3,925, coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK) +2.3% at IDR17,600, while consumer goods producer \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UNVR.JK) +2.2% at IDR11,550. (EDH)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Nomura International\uc2  says JPY likely to remain under upward pressure in 2010, with USD/JPY possibly falling to 83 level, driven by year-end repatriation flows and decline in global risk appetite. Says this will leave the Japanese authorities with little option but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "We believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have some success in pushing back USD/JPY to around 85 and defending that level." Recommends buying JPY as part of diversified basket (of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) against USD. Adds dollar will likely remain weak in 2010 owing to persistently weak capital inflows and extraordinarily stimulative monetary policy. Recommends shorting USD exposure versus diversified global basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : JPY, EUR, KRW, CLP and RUB, weighted 15%-25% each. Implications for Japan stocks--particularly high USD-sales exposed firms such as major automakers and electronics makers dependent on holiday gift-giving season--stark given further unabated yen strengthening. (BMF)\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate trading soft on low demand from banks a day before they report cash reserve levels to RBI; overnight call rate at 3.15% vs 3.26% Wednesday's close, INR34.29 billion traded so far, well below recent daily average. "Demand is low because banks are well covered towards the end of fortnight. CBLO is also low and it won't be a surprise if it falls to as low as 1% later," says dealer with state-run bank. CBLO presently around 2.50%. Under RBI rules, banks need to set aside 5% of deposits in cash, report reserve levels to RBI every alternate Friday. Most borrow early to buffer last minute liquidity shocks, demand low today with next reporting due Friday. (AVK)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  unlikely to raise interest rates in near term; DBS says Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may only start raising policy rate from 3Q10 as domestic demand continues to be constrained by political risk even as government pushes for greater public investment spending. "From this perspective, it makes sense for the BoT to wait for the Fed and/or other Asian central banks to tighten before it makes its first move." BOT left benchmark rate unchanged at 1.25% yesterday while didn't make major alterations to its policy statement. (PPP)\par \par \uc2 0517 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals are mostly higher in Asia Thursday although copper has failed to test its overnight and 14-month high of $7,170/ton with volume again fairly thin. Prices likely to remain well supported at these levels in 2010, says Dominic Schnider, head of commodities research at \uc2 UBS\uc2  Wealth Management. "Inventories (of copper) have increased but you have to look where we are in the cycle. At this stage they could be double these levels," he says. Adds, in 1H10 OECD demand likely to turn from neutral factor into a positive factor for base metals demand and offset weaker China import demand. However he says he does not anticipate significantly more upside in 2010 and copper prices above $7,500/ton should be taken as a signal to exit positions. LME 3-month copper at $7,145/ton, +$20 since yesterday's kerb with 564 lots done. (JAC)\par \par \uc2 0508 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower with PSEi down 0.9% at 3090.91 in moderate volume. "We made a big advance in the first two days of December so this is basically profit taking," says Jun Calaycay, Accord Capital Equities trader; adds market likely range-bound Friday in 3050-3070 band with market awaiting November inflation Friday. Among decliners, Century Peak Metal Holdings (CPM.PH) off 5.7% at PHP6.60; \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH) off 8.9% to PHP17.75 on news Hongkong's First Pacific (0142.HK) has raised stake in company to 40.7% from 31.5% previously, eliminating hopes for alternate tender offer for company. \uc2 Vista Land & Lifescapes\uc2  (VLL.PH) off 1.0% at PHP1.96.(RSA)\par \par \uc2 0505 GMT [Dow Jones] Indications that BOK would be willing to use \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to lean into higher imported inflation pressures, coupled also with a robust balance of payments picture, USD/KRW may fall at a more rapid pace than previously forecast, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in report. "We believe that central bankers are slowly coming to terms with the risks posed by higher food prices - not its direct impact, but in its ability to push up general price expectations." Thus, trade-dependent and food-importing countries may opt to facilitating faster \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, it adds. Also, "we believe it (KRW) is still fundamentally undervalued on a trade-weighted basis." Puts 1-month, 3-month, and 12-month USD/KRW forecast at 1,135, 1,100, and 1,050, respectively, vs previous forecast at 1,150, 1,135, and 1,100, respectively. (MJL) [ 12-03-09 0117ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091203e5c30004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
