\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Options Down As Spot Rises -Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2009\par 04:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] 1-month ATM implied volatilities down at 11.65%/12.35% vs 12.30%/13.00% in New York Wednesday, as solid spot market prompts players to unload downside hedges. With USD/JPY's upside potential appearing bright in near-term amid rising share markets, some players bought short-dated contracts including overnight, 3-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts with 89.00 strike price, options trader at major Tokyo bank says; face amounts of such contracts unknown. (MIN)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, despite USD/JPY rise overnight on such speculation, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Yuji Saito. Notes, Tokyo market generally dubious about chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as USD/JPY back up (last at 87.86) from 14-year low of 84.82 Friday on receding Dubai shock worries, recovering share markets; also, BOJ's new fund-provision step announced Tuesday to keep down JPY; and, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need U.S.'s agreement to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/JPY, but U.S. appears happy with current USD weakness. "If the dollar-yen plunges below 85.00 again, stepped up warnings could be possible," Saito says, but adds such a drop looks unlikely as Dubai jitters receding, and after BOJ move. (MIN, KDM)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving higher in narrow range, tracking movement of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in absence of major news; pair last up at 46.58 vs 46.51 late yesterday in subdued trade; local bank trader tips stronger bias for pair rest of session. "We do not expect any sharp movement, we will just follow the lead of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says, adding pair likely to trade within 46.50-46.60 range. (RSA)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' new 2010 outlook more upbeat about growth in most countries; "In Asia, we have framed our forecasts around a strong sequential recovery and inventory adjustment including in the more export-orientated countries." Expects moderate U.S. slowdown in 2H10, slowing but still-strong China. For 2010 and 2011, forecasts 11.4% and 10.0% growth for China, 5.1% and 4.8% for ASEAN-5, 8.8% and 8.2% for Asia ex-Japan. Believes inflationary pressures in region will stay muted, except in India, Vietnam; Indonesian inflation expected to head higher, but within \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort zone, expects it to head down in 2011 on credible, effective policy tightening. "We continue to look for Asia to be in the forefront of policy tightening." (CNG)\par \par \uc2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD gaining on rising global risk appetite following announcement today \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  plans to repay US$45 billion of government emergency funding, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sue Trinh. "Stocks are up and gold is making new record highs and the Kiwi is being dragged along for the ride." On local scene, strong housing, commodities data will also underpin NZD. ANZ Commodity price gained 10.5% in November on-month in second highest rise in 23-year history of series. "Stronger commodity prices and strong house price inflation are likely to force the RBNZ's hand sooner than they would like, and ultimately that rate backdrop will be supportive for the Kiwi." RBNZ expected to leave rates unchanged at next Thursday's review, but may take more hawkish stance than in September, October. Trinh tips immediate support at 0.7250, resistance at 0.7300 vs last 0.7262. (SML)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.4% at 2482.251 in light volume, helped by steady IDR, gains in several Asian markets; resistance at 2500. "Foreign funds have so far dominated buying, driving the main index higher," says trader at First Asia Capital. Foreigners net buyers of IDR45 billion worth of shares. Among gainers: gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) +2.0% at IDR3,875, coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK) +2.6% at IDR17,650, while bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) +1.1% at IDR9,100. (EDH)\par \par \uc2 0251 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 near flat at 4765.0, consolidating within 4754.4-4787.0, with materials sector underpinning the market in line with offshore moves. Fresh record high of US$1,225 in gold the main feature, leaving Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 0.6%. BHP (BHP.AU) up 0.4% after offshore gains, although Rio (RIO.AU) down 0.2%. \uc2 Incitec Pivot\uc2  (IPL.AU) up 6.5% on North American peer strength, attributed to bullish comments on fertilizer demand from Potash CEO. Tonight's U.S. weekly jobless claims data and Friday's non-farm payrolls awaited, although traders say the market is winding down before year end. "Some of the bigger funds seem to be pulling down the shutters and are just a little averse to making major changes to their portfolios heading into the holiday season," says BBY Senior Institutional Trader Peter Copeland. He says market looks positive overall. "I guess what's behind the current strength is that the worry about China not being able to manage its growth seems to be abating. There's also a little bit more comfort that the U.S. will avoid a double dip recession." Financials mixed, with CBA (CBA.AU) down 0.8% after outperforming yesterday, Suncorp (SUN.AU) up 3.2% after CFO appointment. (DWR)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW higher at 1,156.4 vs 1,154.0 late yesterday in Seoul; players cautious over possible BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after traders say authorities suspected to have bought USD around 1,154 yesterday. Kospi losing steam (last +0.3% vs +0.7% earlier), also weighing on KRW. "I expect some tussle around the 1,155 level," with exporters poised to sell above 1,156 while importers waiting to buy on dips near 1,154, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,153-1,158 range for rest of session. (MJL)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares heading to flat close as lack of drivers curb activity; "It's been a quiet day today...It looks like we are going to end about even, I can't see what's going to trigger life into the market," says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke. Adds investors will continue to watch offshore markets for direction but doesn't expect much action until there's some local corporate news. NZX-50 last +0.1% at 3151.46. Says investors cheered by news class action in U.S against \uc2 PGG Wrightson\uc2 's (PGW.NZ) 20% owner, \uc2 Agria Corp\uc2 ., dismissed. PGW.NZ up 6.9% at NZ$0.62, PGW rights (PGWRA.NZ) +27.9% at NZ$0.156. Very little action anywhere else in market given dearth of drivers; Telecom (TEL.NZ) flat at NZ$2.46, Restaurant Brands down 1.2% at NZ$1.60 on some profit-taking after strong run. (RSH)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.2% at 1594.67 in light volume, off early high of 1605.40 as investors lock in profits after 3-day gains, says Lee Sun-yup at Shinhan Investment Corporation; "There's no upward momentum to lift the Kospi further sharply. Also investors are cautious before the release of the U.S. non-farm payroll data." But doesn't expect any drastic pullback today as no new negatives have emerged, either. Kospi likely to stay near last close of 1591.63 today. Car makers turn lower on profit-taking; "also a recent fall in the Japanese yen (vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) seem to be giving investors an excuse to take profits as Korean cars compete more with Japanese cars" in global markets, says Lee. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.5% at KRW101,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) down 1.4% at KRW17,400. But most techs still higher, \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.7% at KRW752,000, \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) +4.8% at KRW109,000. (SKS)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last down 1.2%, after ranging between 489-501, with locals selling on concerns that many investors will have to cash out to pay back state subsidized loans after government said yesterday subsidized lending program will end by late December, instead of end-March. "Weak sentiment is understandable amid rising concerns that businesses may have to cope with higher \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs," as they seek other sources of financing, says analyst with BIDV Securities. Volume moderate at 20 million shares valued at VND800 billion. Among decliners, Pha Lai Power (PPC.VH) down 4.8% at VND18,100, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) down 1.5% at VND46,300, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) down 2.0% at VND29,400. Immediate support pegged at 488. (HAA)\par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Linkage between crude oil market, dollar, equities to be further challenged by next week's EIA statistics--likely to reveal another sizable increase in Cushing crude supplies of at least 3%, says Jim Ritterbusch of Ritterbusch and Associates; predicts "small upswing in refinery activity that should further allay any concerns over product supply availability for months to come." Hedge funds still supporting long side of complex as long as dollar remains weak, equities rise; however, "we will continue to cite the continued weakness in the crude curve and the lack of any reduction in the product surpluses as important bearish influences with which the trading community will ultimately be forced to contend," Ritterbusch says. January Nymex crude recently trading 16 cents higher at $76.76/bbl on Globex. (FLO) [ 12-02-09 2202ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091203e5c30002i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
