\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Seen Up Before ECB Meets\b0\par\par\uc2 3,020 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2009\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen up; government debt little changed; majors gain on yen; oil, gold up\par \par \uc2 -WSJ: Former Ford Execs' Bid For Volvo Includes Participation Of Swedish Investors\par \par \uc2 -German Finance Min: No Broad Credit Crunch\par \par \uc2 -RBS Most Exposed UK Bank To Dubai World - Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Siemens\uc2  Plans Currently No Sale Of Hearing Aid Unit - Sources\par \par \uc2 -Thomas Cook Seeks To Buy Oeger Tours - Report\par \par \uc2 -WSJ: Mitsubishi In Talks With \uc2 Peugeot\uc2  On Link\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Bank of America\uc2  To Repay $45B In TARP Aid\par \par \uc2 -Fed's Beige Book: Economic Conditions Continue To Improve\par \par \uc2 -RBS Faces Govt Veto On Bonus Payout, Fears Impact\par \par \uc2 -At Least 5,000 UK Bankers Seen Earning GBP1M -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Barclays\uc2  Sets Pay Raises To Beat UK Clampdown -Report\par \par \uc2 -Ex-BoE Policymaker Blasts Governor's Aid Secrecy -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Comcast\uc2 , GE Ready to Announce Deal For NBC Universal\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0900 GMT E.U. Nov Eurozone Comp PMI; 0930 GMT U.K. Nov CIPS Svcs PMI; 1000 GMT E.U. 3Q GDP, 1st release; 1245 GMT ECB interest rate decision; 1330 GMT ECB Governing Council meeting press conference\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Investors seem willing to give stock markets another go on Thursday, but buying will be more selective as some of the big funds curtail activity as the year winds down. European bourses are seen up slightly ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary meeting, with investors looking for a sign of coming tightening. Government debt is seen little changed to start. Major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , oil and gold are higher.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European stock markets may start slightly higher on Thursday, as investors tempt fate to bid the year's rally a bit higher before the holidays.\par \par \uc2 For Thursday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE up 9 at 5336, the DAX up 22 at 5804 and the CAC up 5 at 3801.\par \par \uc2 Armando Guglielmetti, senior strategist at InvestNews.ch, looks for prices to remain mostly flat ahead of the ECB meeting, with the best gainers likely to be the fast-growing names.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the bigger funds seem to be pulling down the shutters and are just a little averse to making major changes to their portfolios heading into the holiday season," said BBY Senior Institutional Trader Peter Copeland.\par \par \uc2 The Stoxx 600 index is showing a gain of 24.2% year-to-date and can continue to advance from these levels, according to strategists at ING who raised their 2010 target to 310 on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 However, they do expect a change in sector performance and said "we believe that the first stage of the rally, characterized by the outperformance of risky, volatile stocks, is now over," they said.\par \par \uc2 Many investors are bracing for key economic releases due out Thursday and Friday. On Thursday, purchasing managers data are released in the U.K. and the euro zone. Also in the euro zone, third-quarter gross domestic product figures and retail sales data are due. In the U.S., weekly jobless claims and the ISM services index will be released.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, strategists at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in London offered an upbeat view on 2010. "We are bullish on growth for next year and as long as there are worries on the outlook, the market can go a little bit higher," said Teun Draaisma, European equity strategist. "We believe these little blips are dips to buy--if you are nimble enough to get out at the right time."\par \par \uc2 That rosy view contrasted with rising energy inventories in America, an indication that growth may not be taking hold in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "We can talk a lot about China and its strong manufacturing data but the U.S. is still the world's largest energy consumer and it looks like it's having a hard time sucking down oil right now," Phil Flynn of PFGBest said.\par \par \uc2 Equity strategists at ING said that investors should start to take profits in high-volatility sectors to concentrate on value. "We have downgraded all of the financial sectors and are now underweight banks and insurance," the broker said.\par \par \uc2 "We believe banks are still short of capital and that institutional appetite for equities will remain subdued, as will M&A activity. Non-market risks include the possibility of a hung parliament following the next U.K. general election," the broker said.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, it upgraded many of the sectors that are less sensitive to economic trends and said it's now overweight food and beverage, health care, telecoms and personal and household goods.\par \par \uc2 For the U.K., \uc2 UBS\uc2  said, "Our biggest overweight is pharma, given the stage of the economic cycle. Valuations relative to the market are at a 20-year low, and investors are no longer aggressively overweight the sector."\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are higher on Thursday, after analysts' bearish comments weighed on \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and other financials Wednesday, though another round of buying in \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  and other materials stocks left overall indices little changed.\par \par \uc2 "Financials have been the weak link of the market of late," said Howard Ward, chief investment officer for GAMCO Growth Fund. "I've lightened up in financials of late. If a crack shows in this recovery, it's going to be seen in financial stocks first."\par \par \uc2 If Friday's jobs report is a disappointment, \uc2 Fred Dickson\uc2 , chief market strategist at \uc2 Davidson Cos\uc2 ., says he expects selling across the board.\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets are mostly higher Thursday, with \uc2 Mitsubishi Motors Corp\uc2 . surging on news the Japanese carmaker was in talks with France's \uc2 PSA Peugeot-Citroen\uc2  about a capital tie-up.\par \par \uc2 Exporters were also rising in Tokyo as worries over the strong yen eased somewhat, although fresh data showed that Japanese companies curbed investment in the latest quarter.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  is forecasting an upbeat year ahead for China, where the world's fastest-growing major economy will be underpinned by the "tail winds" of aggressive monetary and fiscal policy stimulus and rebounding corporate earnings.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The yen continues to lose ground, but the euro, pound and Swiss franc are advancing against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are jittery about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. While we downplay that risk, we do think that easing of concerns about Dubai should see the yen soften further," \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  strategists said in a report.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. Treasury spokesman confirmed that Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat met with Treasury officials this week, a move that could signal \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as it seeks to tame the yen.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is getting serious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kathy Lien, a director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Investors will also be eyeing the ECB's decision on its benchmark interest rate scheduled for Thursday, as well as comments by its president, Jean-Claude Trichet, following the rate-setting meeting.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the ECB to signal a definite intention to head for the exit," James Nixon, an ECB Watcher at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in London, wrote in a note to clients. "Market stands to be surprised by the ECB's determination to reduce excess liquidity."\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 With the ECB meeting dominating events Thursday, prices of government bonds in the euro zone and the U.K. may start little changed, amid supply from France with EUR4.2 billion of issuance and some important data.\par \par \uc2 Gilt markets will watch the ECB meeting for signs of tightening of euro-zone liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England's chief economist Spencer Dale spoke optimistically about the U.K. economy, saying it has turned a corner on the road to recovery, though difficulties lie ahead. He added that the depreciation of the sterling would help to stimulate economic activity by helping to improve the competitiveness of U.K. companies.\par \par \uc2 On the U.K., \uc2 UBS\uc2  said that "in terms of monetary policy, we expect the Bank of England to leave rates on hold for some time yet, and only start to increase them very gradually" in the second half of 2010.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury bond prices are flat on Thursday, after prices of Treasurys maturing in 10 years or less fell Wednesday as investors adjusted bets ahead of an announcement about next week's government debt sales and Friday's non-farm payrolls data.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's latest economic survey, known as the beige book, showed economic conditions in the country continued to improve, reducing the appeal of low-risk government debt.\par \par \uc2 It "depicts an economy that is in the early stages of a fragile recovery without any evidence of wage pressures or significant inflation pressures," said Ward McCarthy, chief financial economist within the fixed-income group at Jefferies & Co.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond bucked the selling trend, becoming the only maturity in the Treasury market whose price was up for the session.\par \par \uc2 "The long end of the market is seeing some interest from big accounts selling 10-year notes and buying 30-year bonds. Away from that, the market is plodding along, waiting for Friday's jobs number," said Kevin Giddis, head of fixed-income sales, trading and research in Memphis, Tenn., at Morgan Keegan Inc.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department is scheduled to announce Thursday the sizes for next week's sales of three-year, 10-year and 30-year securities. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke is also set to appear at a confirmation hearing in the Senate on his nomination for a second term.\par \par \uc2 In Japan on Thursday, prices of government bonds turned lower as stocks rallied, but some buying interest remained following the Bank of Japan's additional monetary easing measures.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Oil is higher Thursday, bouncing back from prior falls as investors digested key U.S. inventory data, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for January delivery, rose 20 cents to $76.80 a barrel. Brent North Sea crude for January delivery added 42 cents to $78.30.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices fell sharply in U.S. trade Wednesday after news of a larger-than-expected rise in petrol supplies in the country, the world's biggest energy-consuming nation. [ 03-12-09 0600GMT ]\par \par \uc2 However, investors in Asia focused Thursday on a bigger-than-expected 1.2-million-barrel drawdown in U.S. distillate stocks, including diesel and heating oil, indicating steady demand ahead of the winter, an analyst said. The market was expecting a 300,000-barrel decline.\par \par \uc2 "One optimistic thing was the distillate stocks, which were down," said Benjamin Westmore, a Melbourne-based minerals and energy economist with the \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Spot gold continued its rally and tapped a fresh record high of $1,225.05 a troy ounce, up $10.25 from its New York close.\par \par \uc2 Commodities were the best asset class to invest in, as China and India's development drives demand growth and governments' money-printing pushes investors to seek exposure to alternative assets, said investment strategist Jim Rogers.\par \par \uc2 "If the world economy gets better, commodities are a great place to invest because of supply shortages," Rogers said in a conference call hosted by ETF Securities.\par \par \uc2 LME copper is at $7,130 a ton, up $10 from the PM kerb. "There's a lot of money coming into commodities because people are looking at the sector from a long-term point of view," said a trader in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 03-12-09 0600GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10452.68  -18.90  -0.18% \par Nasdaq     2185.03   +9.22  +0.42% \par S&P 500    1109.24   +0.38  +0.03% \par FTSE 100   5327.39  +15.22  +0.29% \par Xetra DAX  5781.68   +5.07  +0.09% \par CAC40      3795.92  +20.18  +0.53% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9889.81  280.87  2.92% \par Hang Seng    22427.02 137.45  0.62% \par S&P/ASX 200   4767.90   5.54  0.12% \par Taiwan Index  7675.59  -2.03 -0.03% \par S.Korea Kospi 1605.26  13.63  0.86% \par Shanghai Comp 3255.61 -14.14 -0.43% \par Bombay SenSex 17292.59 122.68 0.7% \par Dow Future    10468.00 +18.00  +0.2% \par NASDAQ Future 1798.38  +6.88   +0.4% \par S&P Future    1112.20  +4.30   +0.4% \par Above are as of 0515 GMT \par  \par  \par USD/JPY      87.75-80     +0.38% \par Range        87.92   87.34 \par EUR/USD      1.5088-90    +0.29% \par Range        1.5097  1.5043