\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2009\par 01:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied versus the yen for a second straight session Wednesday as speculation mounted that Japan officials will take measures to arrest the recent appreciation of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A U.S. Treasury spokesman confirmed that Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat met with Treasury officials this week, a move that could signal \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as it seeks to tame the yen. Late Wednesday afternoon in New York, the dollar was at Y87.40 from Y86.68 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The euro was at $1.5045 from $1.5086. The dollar was at CHF1.0017 from CHF0.9994, while the U.K. pound was at $1.6638 from $1.6617.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD to consolidate as markets await 1245 GMT ECB announcement (expected to keep rates at record low 1.0%) and Trichet's 1330 GMT news conference (expected to outline first steps of exit strategy from extraordinary stimulus measures). EUR/USD supported by divergent ECB-Fed monetary policies, sovereign rebalancing of reserves in favor of EUR. But EUR/USD topside limited by official concern over EUR strength, caution before tomorrow''''s U.S. jobs data. Other data focus: 0855 GMT German November services PMI, 0900 GMT euro-zone November services and composite PMI, 1000 GMT euro-zone October retail trade, 1000 GMT euro-zone 3Q GDP (revised). EUR/USD daily chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, though inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.5030 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.4969 (Tuesday's low), then 1.4863 (base of ascending channel formed with July 1 high of 1.4201 and Aug. 17 low of 1.4044), 1.4826 (Friday's reaction low) and 1.4797 (Nov. 20 reaction low). Resistance at 1.5117 (Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.5144 (Nov. 25 high), then 1.5163 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement of decline from July 15, 2008 top of 1.6039 to Oct. 28, 2008 bottom of 1.2328), 1.5284 (previous base set May 8, 2008) and 1.5374 (top of channel).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY to trade with risks skewed higher amid broadly stronger USD undertone (ICE dollar index up 0.4% at 74.66 last ) as market participants cover USD-shorts ahead of today's ECB rate decision and tomorrow's U.S. employment data. USD/JPY supported by growing speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  preparing for USD/JPY-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  pointman, Vice Minister Tamaki, met his U.S. Treasury counterpart in Washington on heels of BOJ's emergency liquidity injection. 'The Japanese authorities are ticking all the boxes to show they are doing all the things they need to do before they get involved in an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign,' said Simon Derrick, head of Bank of New York-Mellon's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy team. USD/JPY also buoyed by USD demand for import settlements, higher U.S. Treasury yields. But USD/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales, loose U.S. monetary policy. No strong cue for yen carry trades from Wall Street overnight as U.S. stocks little changed (DJIA off 0.18%, Nasdaq up 0.42%), CBOE volatility index VIX dropped 3.65% to 21.12. Data focus: 2315 GMT Japan November services PMI, 1330 GMT U.S. 3Q productivity & costs (revised), 1330 GMT U.S. Nov 28 weekly jobless claims, 1500 GMT U.S. November ISM nonmanufacturing composite index, 1730 GMT Fed's Rosengren speaks. USD/JPY daily chart positive-biased as stochastics rising from oversold, negative MACD histogram bars contracting, pointing to increasing upside risks. Resistance at 88.00 (previous base set Oct. 7); breach would expose upside to 88.65 (Nov. 25 high), then 89.19 (Nov. 23 high), 89.53 (Nov. 17 high) and 89.74 (55-day moving average). Support at 86.97 (hourly chart), then at 86.57 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 86.11 (Tuesday's low), then 85.83 (Monday's low) and 84.81 (14-year low hit Friday).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD to consolidate with risks skewed lower, undermined by broadly stronger USD undertone, position adjustments before ECB, U.S. jobs data, as well as concerns over high UK public debt, official nod for weaker GBP. Data focus: 0930 GMT UK November CIPS services PMI. GBP/USD daily chart mixed as stochastics bullish, but MACD still in bearish mode. Support at 1.6548 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.6390 (Tuesday's low), then 1.6379 (Monday's low), 1.6270 (Friday's low) and 1.6260 (Nov. 3 reaction low). Resistance at 1.6695 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.6726 (Nov. 26 high), then 1.6745 (Nov. 25 high), 1.6845 (Nov. 18 high) and 1.6877 (Nov. 16 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF to consolidate, supported by broadly stronger USD undertone, expectations of more SNB CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But USD/CHF gains limited by caution before ECB, U.S. data. Daily chart still negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, though inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 1.0026 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0075 (highs off Monday & Tuesday), then 1.0175 (Friday's high, near 55-day moving average), 1.0198 (Nov. 23 high) and 1.0222 (Nov. 20 high). Support at 0.9974 (yesterday's low), then at 0.9965 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9914 (19-month low hit Nov. 26), then 0.9868 (March 31, 2008 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD to trade with risks skewed higher, underpinned by broadly stronger USD undertone, softer oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $1.77 yesterday at $79.60/barrel), dovish BOC monetary stance, official concern over CAD strength. Data focus: 1315 GMT Canada November monetary reserves. USD/CAD daily chart still negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode; though inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 1.0532 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.0578 (Tuesday's high), then 1.0618 (Monday's high), 1.0720 (100-day moving average) and 1.0750 (Friday's reaction high). Support at 1.0429 (yesterday's low), then at 1.0404 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0376 (Oct. 21 low), then 1.0264 (Oct. 20 reaction low) and 1.0204 (16-month low hit Oct. 15).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD to consolidate. AUD/USD underpinned by Aussie-USD yield gap; but gains tempered by caution before today's ECB, U.S. jobs data. AUD/USD daily chart mixed as stochastics bullish, bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.9278 yesterday, but MACD still in bearish mode. Support at 0.9228 (yesterday's low), then at 0.9202 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 0.9103 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9070 (55-day moving average), 0.8944 (Friday's low) and 0.8905 (Nov. 2 reaction low). Resistance at 0.9295 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9322 (Nov. 25 reaction high), then 0.9405 (16-month high hit Nov. 16).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD to range-trade. NZD/USD underpinned by Kiwi-USD yield gap, but topside limited by caution before ECB, U.S. data, as well as official concern about strong Kiwi, dovish RBNZ monetary stance. NZD/USD daily chart mixed as stochastics bullish, but MACD still in bearish mode. Support at 0.7200 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7138 (Tuesday's low), then 0.7116 (Monday's low), 0.7021 (Friday's low) and 0.6959 (Sept. 14 low). Resistance at 0.7295 (highs off Tuesday & yesterday), then at 0.7307 (55-day moving average); breach would expose upside to 0.7331 (highs off Nov. 25 & 26), then 0.7367 (Nov. 23 high) and 0.7463 (Nov. 19 high).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar's strength against the yen during Asian trade pushed China's yuan lower against the U.S. unit late Wednesday. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.8263 around 0930 GMT, up from Tuesday's close of CNY6.8260. It traded between CNY6.8261 and CNY6.8274. Traders traded the onshore dollar up as they expect the central parity to be set higher following the dollar's strong performance today. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the dollar-yuan central parity rate at 6.8268, largely unchanged from 6.8270 Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2009 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020091203e5c30002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
