\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ FOCUS:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Could Spark Rush Of  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2009\par 06:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  (This story was oroginally published Tuesday.) \par  \par    By Katie Martin \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--If Japan buckles under the pressure to devalue the yen, it could be open season for a global round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 Such a move became much more likely Tuesday, after the country's authorities pumped Y10 trillion ($115 billion) in cheap liquidity into the country's financial system--a surprise move aimed at weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tackling deflation.\par \par \uc2 It was, however, a flop, at least in terms of the yen's strength against the dollar. The greenback ticked up by just over 1% against the yen, to hit the day's high at Y87.54. Traders quickly bought the yen back once again, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within reach of its highest levels against the dollar since 1995.\par \par \uc2 Many market watchers now think a more straightforward, heavy-hitting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involving selling yen to buy dollars, is edging closer. That could rumble not only the yen, but a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  all around the world. The reason: if it is OK for Japan, then what is to prevent other countries from stepping up their efforts to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from soaring against the sliding dollar?\par \par \uc2 "That's the problem," said Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk taker at hedge fund BlueGold Capital in London. "It would validate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that's the reason \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not done it yet. Japan sees itself as always doing the right thing for the good of the region and the world. But it doesn't have a choice at this point."\par \par \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far from certain. For one thing, it's tough to describe the current bout of yen strength as disorderly--a trigger that has been used by authorities around the world for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. Instead, the yen's climb has been quite gradual.\par \par \uc2 In addition, while the yen didn't weaken much against the dollar Tuesday, it did fall markedly against the euro, sterling and the Australian and New Zealand dollars.\par \par \uc2 Still, if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  less of a dirty word for major economies, it could tempt others in. "It's imaginable that New Zealand could join the party," said Ray Farris, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in London. "You could maybe also think about Sweden and Norway," he added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become a useful tool for any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is keen to raise interest rates but reluctant to generate export-crimping \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, Farris said.\par \par \uc2 Like other major nations, Japan has repeatedly signed up to the consensus among the Group of Seven major economies that competitive devaluations are undesirable. Still, it has been tempted of late, because the yen has soared by some 10% since April when measured against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Against the dollar, which has slumped against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the move is more severe, with the yen climbing by 14% since April to mark a 14-year high.\par \par \uc2 Japan now has two serious problems. One is sinking consumer prices, which fell by 2.2% on the year in October, following a 2.3% decline the month before. Yen strength only adds to the problem by making it ever cheaper for Japan to import foreign goods.\par \par \uc2 The other major concern is investor sentiment. "Foreign investors don't like yen strength," said Simon Derrick, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon in London. Flow data at his bank show that "foreign investors have been pulling away from Japan since the start of the year," Derrick said, noting that a strong yen makes life hard for Japanese exporters, the backbone of the economy. "It's all been out, out, out," he added.\par \par \uc2 To some, the BOJ move Tuesday was designed chiefly to lay the groundwork for a full-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , proving that it has exhausted its monetary policy options.\par \par \uc2 Other, less developed nations have already gone down this route. Most famously, Brazil imposed capital controls in October, seeking to weaken the real against the dollar. Taiwan also toughened rules on foreign investors' deposits in November.\par \par \uc2 Authorities across Asian emerging markets have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  for several months. Vietnam shoved its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the dong--5% lower last week, while Russia has made no secret of its desire to cool the ruble. China has effectively pegged its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the sliding dollar since the middle of last year.\par \par \uc2 "In the early 1990s, it was absolutely accepted that central banks managed their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  within well-defined ranges," said \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2 's Derrick. "I think we might go back to that kind of world."\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0) 207 842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 02, 2009 00:30 ET (05:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091202e5c2000to\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
