\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Official Meets Treasury; Eye On Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2009\par 21:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with details on yen and Japanese economy; investors comments; previous \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Meena Thiruvengadam and Fabio Alves \par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat met with U.S. Treasury officials this week, a move that could signal \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as it seeks to tame the yen.\par \par \uc2 A Treasury spokesman confirmed that Rintaro Tamaki, Japan's influential vice finance minister for international affairs, met with Lael Brainard, the Obama administration's nominee for Treasury under secretary for international affairs, on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials declined to provide details on the discussions between the two, but it is likely the pair discussed the yen's recent surge against the dollar, market watchers said.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the yen hit a 14-year high against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Rapid or disorderly movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have always been the official reason for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese authorities are ticking all the boxes to show they are doing all the things they need to do before they get involved in an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign," said Simon Derrick, who heads the Bank of New York-Mellon's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy team.\par \par \uc2 The yen's recent rise has increased pressure on the new government. Japan has long fought a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it hurts the competitiveness of Japan's exports, threatening to choke off an export-led economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "A strong yen is a big problem for Japan because 70% of the revenue from three of Japan's largest companies Toyota, \uc2 Sony\uc2  and Canon, are generated outside of Japan," said Kathy Lien, a director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Further gains in the yen will also hinder the emergence of the world's second biggest economy from deflation. Japanese authorities last month declared that the country fell into deflation--a persistent decline in consumer prices--for the first time in three years.\par \par \uc2 The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased after the dollar plunged to 84.82 yen last week in the wake of Dubai's debt crisis. With doubts mounting about the speed at which the U.S. economy will recover and the Federal Reserve will raise rates from ultra-low levels, investors have favored the yen over the greenback. Japanese officials have stressed the yen's gains reflect dollar weakness, not fundamental yen strength.\par \par \uc2 However, analysts believe Japanese authorities won't allow the yen to hit its post-Second World War record high, when the dollar sank to 79.75 in April 1995.\par \par \uc2 Tamaki's position has been very high-profile in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the days of the original "Mr. Yen," Eisuke Sakakibara, in the 1990s. Sakakibara was a master of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and his comments alone were often enough to move the yen's exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "Given that Tamaki has taken the post as the top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat of Japan, calling him the new Mr. Yen is certainly appropriate because Sakakibara was given this name simply because of his influence on the market," Lien said.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ, acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  numerous times through the years in attempts to slow the yen's advance. Beginning in 2003, the BOJ switched tactics from open sales of yen to covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through agent banks. The BOJ hasn't been active in markets with any sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have already altered their language in discussing the yen, with the country's finance minister recently describing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements as "abnormal." They also have continued to ease monetary policy, revealing a new Y10 trillion lending facility this week.\par \par \uc2 "Prior to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you have to take monetary policy action," Derrick said. "Introducing another funding mechanism presumably should discourage investors from buying the yen."\par \par \uc2 Noting that Japanese officials can "clearly demonstrate that the strength of the yen is going against economic fundamentals," Derrick said they are taking a "proactive" approach when it comes to managing the value of the yen.\par \par \uc2 -By Meena Thiruvengadam, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-6629; meena.thiruvengadam@dowjones.com; and Fabio Alves, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2204; fabio.alves@dowjones.com [ 12-02-09 1501ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091202e5c2000d7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
