\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Flat; Support from Resources\b0\par\par\uc2 1,886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2009\par 23:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10452.68  -18.90  -0.18% \par Nasdaq     2185.03   +9.22  +0.42% \par S&P 500    1109.24   +0.38  +0.03% \par FTSE 100   5327.39  +15.22  +0.29% \par Xetra DAX  5781.68   +5.07  +0.09% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100  2/32  -3/32 0.72% +5.1 \par 5Y Tsy  100  6/32  -8/32 2.08% +5.3 \par 10Y Tsy 100 16/32 -11/32 3.32% +4.0 \par  \par USD/JPY       87.36-41     +0.71% \par Range         87.49-86.60 \par EUR/USD       1.5043-45    -0.27% \par Range         1.5110-1.5033 \par AUD/USD       0.9244-49    -0.08% \par Range         0.9295-0.9232 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   12.55/12.95% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   10.00/10.40% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   13.40/13.75% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1213.00  +$12.80 \par Comex Gold Range    $1196.50-$1218.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)      $76.60   -$1.77 \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 Analysts' bearish comments weighed on \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and other financials, though another round of buying in \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  and other materials stocks kept any broad market declines muted.\par \par \uc2 After closing at another 2009 high on Tuesday, the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed Wednesday down 18.90, or 0.18%, at 10452.68. Within the index, decliners were led by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , down 1.5% to $15.65. \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  was also weak, off 1.7% to $62.49, after \uc2 President and Chief Operating Officer Ralph Alvarez\uc2  said he will retire at the end of the year for health-related reasons. Alvarez had been widely regarded as the likely successor to \uc2 chief executive Jim Skinner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the positive side of the index, \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  rose 6.6% to $13.64. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500 rose 0.38, or 0.03%, to 1109.24, thanks to a gain for \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  and other basic materials firms.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite closed up 9.22 points, or 0.42%, to 2185.03, marking its third straight day in the green.\par \par \uc2 Shares of the basic-materials sector climbed, boosted by rising metals prices and as portfolio managers continued to pad their funds with stocks exposed to commodities. Copper hit its highest point since September 2008, aluminum since October 2008 and zinc and lead since May last year. Gold's hitting a new high above $1,200 an ounce also contributed to base-metal gains.\par \par \uc2 Weighing against materials firms, however, one commodity--oil--was under pressure for much of the session. Oil futures slid as government data showed a larger-than-expected rise in U.S. oil inventories and renewed worries about slumping demand. \uc2 Chesapeake Energy\uc2  was one of the energy sector's weakest components, off 2.9% at $23.40.\par \par \uc2 Also weighing on the market, analysts with \uc2 UBS\uc2  predicted the capital market's seasonal slowing could come earlier this year, cutting its fourth-quarter earnings target for \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  and others. In addition, Sanford C. Bernstein said \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  could face a revenue decline of as much as $3 billion under a "worst case" scenario for derivatives legislation.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  closed down 0.7% at $41.93, while \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  lost 2.9% to $30.60.\par \par \uc2 "Financials have been the weak link of the market of late," said Howard Ward, chief investment officer for GAMCO Investors. "I've lightened up in financials of late. If a crack shows in this recovery, it's going to be seen in financial stocks first."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied versus the yen for a second straight session as speculation mounted that Japanese officials will take measures to arrest the recent appreciation of the yen.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. Treasury spokesman confirmed that Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat met with Treasury officials this week, a move that could signal \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as it seeks to tame the yen.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is getting serious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kathy Lien, a director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday afternoon in New York, the dollar was at Y87.40 from Y86.68 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The euro was at $1.5045 from $1.5086, as well as at Y131.48 from Y130.76. The dollar was at CHF1.0017 from CHF0.9994, while the U.K. pound was at $1.6638 from $1.6617.\par \par \uc2 The Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 74.635 from 74.426 Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have stepped up rhetoric on the yen after the dollar plunged to a 14-year low, at 84.82 yen last week, in the wake of Dubai's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The greenback's gains began yesterday after comments by Japanese Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama, who was quoted by the Nikkei as saying the yen's sharp rise against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "cannot be left as is," during a speech Wednesday in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials worry that a stronger yen will hurt exports, derailing an incipient economic recovery, as well as making it more difficult for the world's second biggest economy to emerge from deflation, or a persistent decline in consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 A Treasury spokesman confirmed that Rintaro Tamaki, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, met with Lael Brainard, the Obama administration's nominee for Treasury under secretary for international affairs, on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials declined to provide details on the discussions between the two, but it is likely the pair discussed the yen's recent surge against the dollar, market watchers said.\par \par \uc2 "The signals that we've heard from Japan have been somewhat stronger in the past 24 hours," said Vassili Serebriakov, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk is particularly high unless" the dollar trades consistently under 85 yen, he said. "The risk is that they will step up their response going forward, although it may not be necessarily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Speculation arose that the meeting between Tamaki and Brainard may be part of a process through which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Some analysts reckon that the Japanese government could still attempt unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower.\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese authorities are ticking all the boxes to show they are doing all the things they need to do before they get involved in an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign," said Simon Derrick, who heads the Bank of New York-Mellon's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy team.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also rose against most of its major rivals, although the gain on the euro was modest.\par \par \uc2 Investors were in a waiting mode ahead of Friday's U.S. nonfarm payrolls data for November to gauge the pace of the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The market saw little reaction after the Federal Reserve said in its Beige Book that conditions across many of its 12 districts have "improved modestly." Consumer spending has risen and home sales picked up. But inflation remains tame, as the report said upward wage pressures were subdued.\par \par \uc2 Investors will also be eyeing the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on its benchmark interest rate scheduled for today, as well as comments by its president, Jean-Claude Trichet, following the rate-setting meeting.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the ECB to signal a definite intention to head for the exit," James Nixon, an ECB Watcher at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in London, wrote in a note to clients. "Market stands to be surprised by the ECB's determination to reduce excess liquidity."\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 Prices of Treasurys maturing in 10 years or less were lower as investors adjusted bets ahead of an announcement about next week's government debt sales and Friday's nonfarm payrolls data.\par \par \uc2 Bond prices were also lower following the release of the Federal Reserve's latest economic survey, known as the beige book, which showed economic conditions in the country continued to improve, reducing the appeal of low-risk government debt.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond bucked the selling trend, becoming the only maturity in the Treasury market whose price was up for the session.\par \par \uc2 "The long end of the market is seeing some interest from big accounts selling 10-year notes and buying 30-year bonds. Away from that, the market is plodding along, waiting for Friday's jobs number," said Kevin Giddis, head of fixed-income sales, trading and research in Memphis, Tenn., at Morgan Keegan Inc.\par \par \uc2 As of 2050 GMT, the two-year note was 3/32 lower at 100 2/32 to yield 0.72%, the 10-year note was down 11/32 to 100 16/32 to yield 3.32% while the 30-year bond was 9/32 higher at 101 31/32 to yield 4.26%.\par \par \uc2 Supply pressure mounts, with the Treasury Department scheduled to announce tonight the sizes for next week's sales of three-year, 10-year and 30-year securities. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke is also set to appear at a confirmation hearing in the Senate on his nomination for a second term.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Beige Book Says Economic Conditions Continue To Improve \par Geithner Says TARP Winding Down \par Dubai World Creditors Poised To Appoint KPMG \par Frank Sees Reform Bill On House Floor Dec. 9 \par US Credit-card Delinquencies Near Record Highs \par Goldman Sees Continued Economic Rebound \par Fed Bullard: We're On Track For Recovery -CNBC \par US Private Sector Jobs Drop 169K \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Avtovaz\f28 \uc2 \f2  Foreshadows 65,000 Job Cuts \par EU Agrees Financial Supervision Plan \par UK Banks May Not Meet Lending Promises \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  Denies Departure From Switzerland \par Pakistan Seeks Cooperation With US On New Afghan War Strategy \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 1601  Hong Kong    Nov Composite PMI \par 2230  Australia    Nov PSI \par 2315  Japan        Nov Services PMI \par 2350  Japan        3Q Financial Statements Statistics of Corporations \par 0030  Australia    Oct Retail Trade \par 0100  Australia    Nov VFACTS Vehicle Sales \par 0200  New Zealand  Auction of Treasury bonds \par 0230  China        Nov Services PMI \par 0500  India        Nov Services PMI \par N/A   Australia    Auction of March 2010 Treasury Notes worth A$600M \par N/A   Indonesia    Nov International Reserves \par N/A   Philippines  Bangko Sentral Monetary Policy meeting highlights \par 0630  Indonesia    Bank Indonesia Board of Governors meeting & decision \par 0630  India        Weekly WPI (fuel products and primary articles) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-12-09 2209GMT ]\f28 \par \pa