\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Stronger Yen Will Keep Bank Of Japan Pressured To Act\b0\par\par\uc2 By Fabio Alves   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2009\par 21:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The yen looks set to remain strong in the short term despite the Bank of Japan's efforts, hindering the economy's recovery and its emergence from deflation.\par \par \uc2 That keeps the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive, particularly as ultra-low rates in the U.S. mean the dollar will remain under pressure against its major rivals. Confusing comments from Japanese authorities in recent days on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely only add to investors' willingness to test the government's resolve.\par \par \uc2 "The higher yen is a problem for the Japanese economy. If this situation continues, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to do something," said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 The tension over the yen came to a boil last week when the dollar fell to a 14-year low of Y84.82 amid uncertainty over the fallout from Dubai's debt crisis, prompting warnings about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials. Deflation recently took hold again in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for the first time in three years, which will hurt the domestic economy at a time when a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hurting Japan's big exporters.\par \par \uc2 The greenback has since recovered from those lows as worries over Dubai's impact eased. It even rallied briefly, rising as high as Y87.54 after the BOJ announced its surprise meeting Tuesday, but has since eased back to Y86.59 as investors decided the outcome--additional injections of three-month funds into the economy--won't be enough to drive yen rates lower and arrest the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 "The markets are likely to test the resolve of the Japanese authorities, so I wouldn't be surprised if we did see some further move to the downside" in the dollar, said Derek Halpenny, European head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ in London.\par \par \uc2 Just how far the dollar could slide is a matter of debate: Halpenny expects a slow decline to the year-end, though he wouldn't rule out a sharper drop given the market's current volatility.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen certain if the dollar approaches its post-war record low of Y79.75 from April 1995. Few think that's likely barring a significant shock that would result in another rush for safe investments such as the yen.\par \par \uc2 Adam Boyton, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York, forecasts the dollar at Y85 by year's end.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think what the Bank of Japan has done is really sufficient to change the underlying dynamics" of the dollar against the yen," he said.\par \par \uc2 BOJ board members on Tuesday voted unanimously to leave the unsecured overnight call loan rate at 0.1%, where it has been since December 2008, but they agreed to lend up to Y10 trillion in three-month funds at the 0.1% policy rate.\par \par \uc2 The compression between the U.S. and Japanese interest rates, combined with weakness in the dollar, should continue to put downward pressure on" the dollar versus the yen, he added.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa could face further political pressure when he meets Wednesday with Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama.\par \par \uc2 "It's clear that the government will pressure the Bank of Japan to take more action to fend off deflation," said Jane Foley, a research director at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in London. To do so, the BoJ has to take action to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said, adding that the greenback may drop as far as Y82 in the next three months.\par \par \uc2 Reversing the trend may lie more in the Fed's hands than in the BOJ's.\par \par \uc2 "Until we get a clear change in message from the Federal Reserve, the yield differential is very favorable for the dollar remaining at these extreme low levels" against the yen, said \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2 's Halpenny.\par \par \uc2 (Fabio Alves writes about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Dow Jones Newswires. He can be reached at 212-416-2204, or by email at fabio.alves@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 12-01-09 1514ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091201e5c1000ds\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
