\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Fin Min Fujii: Haven't Heard About BOJ's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Rate Check\b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 10:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii said Friday that he doesn't know if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted what market players consider to be a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar against the yen.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know. I haven't heard such a thing," Fujii told a group of reporters when asked about whether the BOJ carried out a so-called "rate check" on commercial banks.\par \par \uc2 A rate check involves a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  asking commercial banks about the details of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction plans. The practice is seen by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players as a form of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and is sometimes followed up by actual \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying.\par \par \uc2 A senior Japanese banker told Dow Jones Newswires that the BOJ carried out such a check on many Japanese and non-Japanese commercial banks Friday. He also said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the news to be passed on to other banks.\par \par \uc2 -By Tomoyuki Tachikawa and Takeshi Takeuchi; 813-6895-7550; Tomoyuki.Tachikawa@dowjones.com, Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com [ 11-27-09 0426ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020091127e5br0001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
