\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Threatens  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Yen At 14-Year High\b0\par\par\uc2 858 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 06:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (Adds comments from other government officials, background.) \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's finance minister Friday escalated his warnings against a soaring yen, calling the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise to a fresh 14-year high "one-sided" and threatening to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the rapid ascent continues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "There's no doubt that (the yen's recent appreciation) is one-sided," Hirohisa Fujii told a news conference. "If this kind of situation is sustained, I think that it would be something abnormal ... it would be possible for us to take" steps under such conditions.\par \par \uc2 Fujii made the remarks after the dollar fell early Friday to Y84.82, its lowest level since July 7, 1995, as investors piled into the yen on the view it offers the safest haven amid falling Asian share prices and concerns over global banks\u8217\'20\'19 exposure to Dubai debt.\par \par \uc2 Other high-ranking government officials on Friday echoed Fujii's concern about the dangers that out of control rises in the yen pose for the economy.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Prime Minister Naoto Kan said at a press conference the government stands ready to implement additional stimulus steps to limit economic damage from the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 Kan, who only a week ago formally declared the return of deflation to the country, quickly toed the line with Fujii about the threat a strong yen poses.\par \par \uc2 "I will closely monitor its impact, and take necessary steps to prevent this economy" from reentering a recession, Kan said.\par \par \uc2 He added that "there was talk that the recent strong yen will put downward pressure on the economy" during a regular Cabinet meeting held earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 The tougher stance among Japanese officials reflects growing worries in the Democratic Party of Japan-led Cabinet that the ongoing rise in the yen could foil the country's still-flimsy economic recovery by exacerbating deflation and further hurting hard-hit exporters.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen about 7% versus the yen since Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama formed his government in the middle of September amid speculation that the DPJ may be less worried about the damage of yen strength than the previous governments led by the Liberal Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Faced with the yen's bull run, however, DPJ members including Fujii have seemingly abandoned the view they held before they rose to power: that yen rises are more advantageous than yen falls, making oil, food and other key imports cheaper for Japanese consumers.\par \par \uc2 The negative effects on Japan's economy of the recently soaring yen "are in no doubt far larger" than its positive impact, Fujii said.\par \par \uc2 A stronger yen makes Japanese goods more expensive abroad and reduces the value of exporters' profits in the yen term, while it makes imported products cheaper, worsening Japan's near-chronic problem of deflation.\par \par \uc2 Underscoring how a rising yen is becoming a major pain in the neck for the government, Chief Cabinet Secretary Hirofumi Hirano also called any excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves "undesirable."\par \par \uc2 Despite the warnings from Japanese officials, speculation persists that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be still several steps away from conducting a yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a course of action it has avoided for five and a half years. Some traders even see the dollar falling below Y80.00 in the weeks ahead, assuming Tokyo will stay out of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think Japan will step into the market anytime soon because what matters is speed, not the level," said Yuji Saito, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  group at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "And (the yen\u8217\'20\'19s ascent) isn't fast enough to be called abnormal. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be justified (only) if the yen rises by Y5 or so within a day."\par \par \uc2 Adding to such speculation, Fujii said: "Let me look at the situation for a bit more to figure out whether the current situation is what (the Group of Seven industrialized economies) consider to be 'disorderly'" and worth acting against.\par \par \uc2 There's also doubt that Tokyo could do much about the dollar's broad downtrend because factors driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower are mostly beyond Japan's control.\par \par \uc2 A wide variety of external factors contributed to the U.S. unit's plunge earlier in the day such as concerns over global banks' exposure to the debt problems of a real-estate subsidiary of Dubai's state-run Dubai World. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness is also seen as being partly due to expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve will keep its interest rates near zero for some time to support the country's economic improvement.\par \par \uc2 Fujii admitted that the reasons behind the yen's climb are "very complicated," and said he will contact U.S. and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers when necessary to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. He hinted that if needed, he may even consider calling on other G-7 leading nations to publish a statement against rapid yen rises, as the group did in October 2008.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7558; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com (Takeshi Takeuchi, Takashi Mochizuki contributed to this article.) [ 11-27-09 0046ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020091127e5br0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
