\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,081 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE CONFIDENCE, CAPACITY USE RISES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business and consumer confidence in the 16 countries that use the euro continued to improve in November, while capacity use in the manufacturing sector rose for the first time since the onset of the financial crisis.(published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par COST OF INSURING DUBAI'S DEBT CONTINUES TO ROCKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost of insuring Dubai's sovereign debt against default surged for the third day in a row as the fall-out from Dubai World's restructuring announcement continues to fuel a flight from risk. (published 1038 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREATENS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS YEN AT 14-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister Friday escalated his warnings against a soaring yen, calling the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise to a fresh 14-year high "one-sided" and threatening to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the rapid ascent continues. (published 0531 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN BANKS HAVE TOTAL UAE LOAN EXPOSURE OF $83.7B -RBS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European banks have a total of $83.7 billion of loan exposure to the United Arab Emirates, Jaques Callioux, Chief euro-zone Economist at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a report released Friday. (published 0853 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN ECONOMY STRUGGLES; THREAT FROM YEN RISE LOOMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese consumer spending sputtered and deflation remained entrenched in Japan last month, underscoring weakness in the economy as Tokyo grapples with a surge in the yen to 14-year highs that could hinder support from its export industries. (published 0253 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EURO-ZONE ECONOMIC RECOVERY PICKED UP IN NOVEMBER: EUROCOIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone's economic recovery gathered pace in November as consumer and business confidence has rallied, according to a measure of activity compiled by the Centre for Economic Policy Research and the Bank of Italy. (published 0845 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FIN MIN FUJII: HAVEN'T HEARD ABOUT BOJ'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATE CHECK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii said Friday that he doesn't know if the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conducted what market players consider to be a verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the dollar against the yen. (published 0911 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB NOWOTNY SEES INFLATION OF AROUND 1% IN 2010 - NEWSPAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro zone inflation will remain around 1% in 2010, subdued by the expiry of liquidity-boosting measures, low capacity utilitization in industry and raw materials production, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Ewald Nowotny said in an interview published Friday. (published 0835 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DIW SEES GERMAN 4Q GDP RISING 0.8% ON QTR VS +0.7% IN 3Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's economy will likely grow by a quarterly 0.8% in the fourth quarter but the fundamental state of the economy is still "very fragile," economic think-tank DIW said Friday. (published 1006 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE BEAN: WOULDN'T BE SURPRISED IF GDP GROWS IN 4Q -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of England Deputy Governor for Monetary Policy Charlie Bean has said that he wouldn't be surprised if the U.K. economy grows in the current quarter, despite a slightly disappointing performance in the third quarter.(published 0747 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA POLITBURO'S FOCUS STILL ON ECONOMIC GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's top leaders pledged Friday to continue focusing on economic recovery next year and to maintain a basically accommodative policy stance, a statement that will likely reassure domestic businesses and financial markets that it supports continued growth. (published 1013 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH NOV CONSUMER CONFIDENCE SURGES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French consumer confidence surged in November from the previous month as households grew more optimistic about their personal financial prospects, even though they remain wary about rising unemployment, data showed Friday..(published 0754 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ADB OFFICIAL: AUTHORITIES MUST ENSURE ASIAN RECOVERY REMAINS STRONG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian economies appear to be on the mend, but it may be too soon to begin unwinding the stimulus measures that helped cushion the blow from the global financial crisis, a senior official at the Asian Development Bank said Friday. (published 0529 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    === DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY === \par  \par  \par =WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR, YEN EXTEND GAINS ON DUBAI RIPPLES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar and the yen are still roaring higher in Europe Friday as the fallout from the Dubai debacle continues to resonate through global financial markets. (published 1030 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ABU DHABI WOULD NOT LET DUBAI GO BANKRUPT - ANALYSTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dubai's request for a debt moratorium for its Dubai World conglomerate worried markets Thursday, but the Gulf city state can count on Abu Dhabi, its deep-pocketed rich brother, to avoid default, analysts said. (published 0132 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJA(11/27) HEARD ON THE STREET: DUBAI'S CAUSTIC LESSON FOR BANKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government-controlled Dubai World shocked investors Wednesday by seeking a standstill agreement on its debt to give itself time to be restructured. Most had expected that the government would stand behind the company. (published 2211 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: THE SWISS FRANC WILL LIKELY REMAIN IN FAVOR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has its work cut out. (published 0815 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =CHINA SELLS MAIDEN 50-YEAR BOND AMID SOLID DEMAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China sold its first 50-year government bond Friday, a move that expands its domestic debt market and places the country in a small league of nations that have issued ultra-long sovereign debt. (published 0614 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: GILTS POWER TOWARDS 120.82 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Maintains the recent strong bull tone following the push into fresh contract highs above 123.04/123.12. This confirms a breakout of a 4-5 month rectangle on the continuation chart, promoting room for further gains above 124.06 towards projected targets at 124.57 and 124.75, just shy of the 125.00 psychological level. (published 0844 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: USD, JPY STRENGTHENING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Weakness extends below Wednesday's low at 1.4956 to leave Wednesday's high at 1.5145 stranded. The failure to hold onto gains after setting fresh '09 highs above 1.5064 suggests a significant rejection at these lofty levels. (published 0825 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INTERVIEW: GOLDMAN SACHS: GOLD STILL RELATIVELY FAIR VALUE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold and other commodities are justified at current pricing levels and aren't exhibiting bubble-like characteristics when macro variables are taken into account, a senior analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  told Dow Jones Newswires. (published 0734 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: ASIA'S CENTRAL BANKS GROW UP ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actions of Asia's central banks suggest prudence is the order of the day as officials look to manage the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market rather than fight it. (published 0116 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Katrina Bishop, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 207 842 9376; katrina.bishop@dowjones.com [ 27-11-09 1200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091127e5br000t4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
