\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREATENS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS YEN AT 14-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister Friday escalated his warnings against a soaring yen, calling the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise to a fresh 14-year high "one-sided" and threatening to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the rapid ascent continues. (published 0531 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:JAPAN ECONOMY STRUGGLES; THREAT FROM YEN RISE LOOMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese consumer spending sputtered and deflation remained entrenched in Japan last month, underscoring weakness in the economy as Tokyo grapples with a surge in the yen to 14-year highs that could hinder support from its export industries. (published 0253 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: CHINA SELLS MAIDEN 50-YEAR BOND AMID SOLID DEMAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China sold its first 50-year government bond Friday, a move that expands its domestic debt market and places the country in a small league of nations that have issued ultra-long sovereign debt. (published 0614 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: S KOREA CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS RISES IN OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's current-account surplus rose to a four-month high in October, driven by stronger exports and smaller service-account deficits as travelers spent less overseas. (published 0309 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:NZ GOODS TRADE DEFICIT CONTINUED TO NARROW IN OCT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's annual goods trade deficit narrowed in October to its lowest level in seven years, Statistics New Zealand said Friday. (published 2354 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM STATE-OWNED BANKS AGREE TO CAP DEPOSIT RATES AT 10.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's state-owned banks agreed to cap their interest rate on deposits at 10.5%, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday. (published 0338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: THAI FINANCE MINISTER: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAY NOT HIKE RATES NEXT WEEK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said Friday that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is unlikely to raise the policy rate at its upcoming rate-setting meeting. (published 0450 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: INDIA OCTOBER INFRASTRUCTURE OUTPUT UP 3.5% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's infrastructure output growth slowed to 3.5% on year in October from 4.1% in September, the government said in a statement Friday. (published 0532 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA REDDY: EXITING STIMULUS STANCE HAS STARTED GLOBALLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some countries across the world have started exiting the easy policy stance that was needed to cushion the global economy form a deep financial crisis, the previous governor of India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday. (published 0533 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA RBI: CAPITAL INFLOWS SHOULD BE EQUAL TO CURRENT ACCOUNT DEFICIT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said Friday that capital inflows into the country should be equivalent or slightly in excess to the country's current account deficit. (published 0615 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA VICE MINISTER: WATCHING FALLOUT OF DUBAI WOES - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Vice Minister of Strategy and Finance Hur Kyung-wook said Friday that the government will closely watch the impact of Dubai World's debt risk and how it could impact the international financial market, Yonhap Infomax reported. (published 0525 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ADB OFFICIAL: AUTHORITIES MUST ENSURE ASIAN RECOVERY REMAINS STRONG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian economies appear to be on the mend, but it may be too soon to begin unwinding the stimulus measures that helped cushion the blow from the global financial crisis, a senior official at the Asian Development Bank said Friday. (published 0529 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NATIONAL HOUSING BANK AIMS TO RAISE UP TO INR5 BLN VIA BONDS - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's National Housing Bank aims to raise up to INR5 billion through an issue of bonds, a person familiar with the matter said Friday. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE:ECB WEBER:DOESN'T SEE MARKETS RETURNING TO 06 SPREAD LEVELS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is unrealistic to expect that markets will return to spread levels seen three years ago, before the global financial crisis, a member of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Governing Council said Thursday. (published 2048 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ABU DHABI WOULD NOT LET DUBAI GO BANKRUPT - ANALYSTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dubai's request for a debt moratorium for its Dubai World conglomerate worried markets Thursday, but the Gulf city state can count on Abu Dhabi, its deep-pocketed rich brother, to avoid default, analysts said. (published 0132 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: ASIA'S CENTRAL BANKS GROW UP ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actions of Asia's central banks suggest prudence is the order of the day as officials look to manage the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market rather than fight it. (published 0116 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: EXTERNAL DEMAND MAY BE A BUGBEAR FOR ASIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia might find itself in a spot of bother in 2010 given the risk that external demand fails to kick in. (published 2340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS HIT 7-WEEK HIGH ON SPOT FALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen options rose in Tokyo on Friday as the greenback's brief plunge below Y85 to a fresh 14-year low fueled demand for hedges against further falls. (published 0230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJA(11/27) HEARD ON THE STREET: DUBAI'S CAUSTIC LESSON FOR BANKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government-controlled Dubai World shocked investors Wednesday by seeking a standstill agreement on its debt to give itself time to be restructured. Most had expected that the government would stand behind the company. (published 2211 GMT) [ 27-11-09 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091127e5br000dd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
