\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYST VIEW 2-Japan raises prospect of G7 joint  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  statement\b0\par\par\uc2 1,241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 04:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 27 (Reuters) - Japan's finance minister raised the prospect of a Group of Seven joint statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said a government response to extreme moves was possible, as the dollar tumbled to a 14-year low against the yen.\par \par \uc2 The greenback slumped to a low of 84.82 yen <JPY=> earlier on Friday as investors shunned riskier assets due to concerns about Dubai's debt problems, but it pared its losses after Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii's comments. [ID:nGEE5AO2FN] ***************************************************************\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY\par \par \uc2 MITUL KOTECHA, GLOBAL HEAD OF \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGY AT CALYON IN HONG KONG:\par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to believe the U.S. would support \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the dollar. You hear a lot of talk about the strong dollar policy but the dollar's not collapsing. Unless the dollar collapses, it's very difficult to see the U.S. become too involved in this. Europe would also have to be actively involved and euro/dollar is now back below $1.50 and I don't think it's at a level where Europe are also going to want to be seen to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There are arguments for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we may have moved closer towards it but it's unlikely that we're going to see concerted support. But certainly Japan is going to be pushing for it.\par \par \uc2 "It's helping the U.S. economy and there's no inflation risk at the moment of a weaker dollar and therefore the U.S. is disinclined to want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the dollar up. Unless it turns into a dollar rout in which case you have to worry about U.S. assets -- that becomes a dollar problem, a U.S. problem. But at the moment I don't think that's the case."\par \par \uc2 GERRARD KATZ, HEAD OF \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\uc2  TRADING, STANDARD CHARTERED, HONG KONG\par \par \uc2 "I don't think there's any chance of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 80 yen. I found it very interesting that the Japanese finance minister said a strong yen could harm Japan's economy, because that's what it really is all about. But the way the market is going, we're not close to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come in alone on another break below 85 yen, and that could be enough to calm the market down.\par \par \uc2 "There are options and a huge amount of structured products down there that could add a lot of momentum. I'm sure the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Japan are on top of that and wouldn't want to see things build up momentum."\par \par \uc2 KOJI FUKAYA, SENIOR \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, DEUTSCHE SECURITIES, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "I would not be surprised if the G7 or G20 issues a statement to prevent the dollar from weakening further, although it is unclear if they will act now or wait till a G7 meeting in February.\par \par \uc2 "Such joint action was unthinkable a year ago as countries feared it would lead to trade protectionism in the wake of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Now the world economy is recovering and the G20 is keeping an eye on the dollar weakness, so it would be easier for countries to act together.\par \par \uc2 "But the statement would unlikely be the kind issued in October last year when the G7 warned against a rapid yen move, because the current situation stems from a weak dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Japan could act alone. Although it may be difficult, Japan could justify the move if the yen further appreciates, given that the country is facing much deeper deflation than other countries and that a stronger yen would exacerbate the problem."\par \par \uc2 MASAFUMI YAMAMOTO, CHIEF \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, BARCLAYS BANK PLC, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "Traders moved to unwind yen-long positions because Fujii signalled joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and his remarks today are stronger than ones made before. Many market players had believed there would be no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  based of what Fujii had said before, and it will take more remarks from policymakers to wipe out this belief completely.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the latest rebound, the dollar's downtrend is unlikely to be changed fundamentally as long as U.S. interest rates stay at a low level."\par \par \uc2 NAOMI FINK, JAPAN STRATEGIST, BANK OF TOKYO-MITSUBISHI UFJ, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "The rhetoric that we've heard seems to be trying to quell speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't ever \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it seems to indicate that they'd prefer not to do so."\par \par \uc2 "It's not really exporters and a strong yen that they are worried about ... it's speculation. They don't want speculators to start pushing dollar/yen towards 70."\par \par \uc2 "They are going to take steps to prevent a double-dip. Is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen going to prevent a Japanese double-dip? Probably not. Even with the yen's relatively strong level, the economy is recovering. And actually thanks to the yen's strong level, the terms of trade were positive.\par \par \uc2 "When Fujii said that a strong yen is not universally negative, he was referring to positive terms of trade."\par \par \uc2 "They know that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has costs. You have to offset three-month bill rates against a pool of sinking assets, namely U.S Treasuries. They only need to look at China and say accumulating twice as much as we have now would not really support U.S. demand."\par \par \uc2 "The dollar is weakening because the main imbalance in the U.S. economy is driven by the renminbi peg to the greenback. So joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not going to do a whole lot on that.\par \par \uc2 "I think there will be a growing argument for it and I would not discount joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it's wrong to say there is a line in the sand below which the Japanese government thinks it might actually prevent a double-dip in Japan."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 -- For yen updates click <FXNEWS>; for prices click <JPY=>\par \par \uc2 -- For JGB updates, click <MMNEWS>, for prices click <2JGBv1>\par \par \uc2 -- For stocks click [.T], for the Nikkei share average click <.N225>\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND -- The dollar has been falling broadly recently as expectations that interest rates in the United States will remain low, speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and a bout of risk aversion all snowballed into a sell-off.\par \par \uc2 -- The last time the Group of Seven nations issued a joint emergency statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in October last year, which warned against excessive volatility in the yen's exchange rates after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared to a 13-year high. No market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed. [ID:nT78987]\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily earlier in the decade to stop a rising yen from harming exports, but they quit in March 2004 at the end of a 15-month long spree in which they sold 35 trillion yen to try to shield a struggling economy. [ID:nT353201]\par \par \uc2 -- Pressure is mounting on Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama's administration to keep the economy from slipping into another recession as public support for his cabinet is slipping and his ruling party faces an upper house election next year. (Reporting by Leika Kihara) ((leika.kihara@thomsonreuters.com; +81-3-6441-1828; Reuters Messaging: leika.kihara.reuters.com@reuters.net)) ((If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com))\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/YEN (ANALYST VIEW 2)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091130e5br0007y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
