\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen surges as Dubai woes put investors off risk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,166 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 07:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Yen hits another 14-year high on dollar, yen crosses fall\par \par \uc2 * Pro-risk trades unwound on concerns about Dubai debt\par \par \uc2 * Japan finmin raises prospect of G7 joint statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 27 (Reuters) - The yen hit its highest level in 14 years on the dollar on Friday and jumped against higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors cut risk trades on concerns about debt problems in Dubai, while Japan signalled growing discomfort with the surge.\par \par \uc2 After plummeting on Thursday, the dollar plunged to a new 14-year trough below 85 yen as investors unwound yen-funded carry trades in the likes of the Australian and New Zealand dollars.\par \par \uc2 In a market made thinner by a U.S. holiday on Thursday, Japanese exporters fuelled the drop by buying yen ahead of the month-end, while traders also dumped dollars due to option triggers at 85.00 yen, and sell orders kicked in below that level.\par \par \uc2 Japan's finance minister raised the prospect of a Group of Seven joint statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cool the yen's rally, but some traders and analysts were sceptical the move was disorderly enough to draw a response from the U.S. or Europe just yet. [ID:nT304288]\par \par \uc2 The comments gave a bit of a lift to the dollar and the other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen, but traders said investors who had gone short were already buying back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after some fell 2 and 3 percent, and the impact of the comments was limited.\par \par \uc2 "It is all about unwinding of carry trades with the euro falling and the Aussie being the most vulnerable," said Hideaki Inoue, deputy general manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Trust Bank.\par \par \uc2 "The yen is drawing funds because of its relative safety appeal in comparison to the euro and the dollar after the Dubai debt scare raised caution over potential loan problems among European banks and a credit crunch in emerging markets."\par \par \uc2 Dubai struggled to ease fears of debt default on Thursday after its move to delay repayments at two flagship firms shook confidence in the Middle East as a centre for investment and a source of capital. [ID:nGEE5AO2FN]\par \par \uc2 European share markets fell on Thursday and shares in Asia also dropped, adding to the nervous climate. [.T]\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell as far as 84.82 yen <JPY=>, its weakest since 1995 and ever closer to its record low of 79.75, before pulling back up to 86.05 yen, still down 0.6 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 Stop-loss selling in the dollar by Japanese margin traders likely gained steam after the greenback's drop below 86.00 yen, according to major margin broker GAITAME.com.\par \par \uc2 The broker also said initial signs suggested that there was a surge in loss-cut selling by margin traders earlier on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The market was driven by stop losses in yen crosses in thin trade," said Kazuyuki Kato, treasury department manager at Mizuho Trust & Banking.\par \par \uc2 "The market bias is for risk-avoiding trade," he said.\par \par \uc2 The euro dropped 1.3 percent to 128.36 yen <EURJPY=R> after falling as far as 126.95 yen, its lowest since late April. The Australian dollar shed 1.6 percent to 77.88 yen <AUDJPY=R>, after tumbling below 76.60 yen.\par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.7 percent at $1.4922 <EUR=> and the dollar index <.DXY> was up 0.5 percent at 75.199.\par \par \uc2 JAPAN OFFICIALS ALERT OVER DOLLAR FALL\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii raised the prospect of a Group of Seven joint statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said a government response to extreme moves was possible. [ID:nT304288]\par \par \uc2 While some analysts said the G7, or the bigger G20, could issue a statement to prevent the dollar from weakening further, or Japan could step in alone, others doubted the chance of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 80 yen.\par \par \uc2 "The rhetoric that we've heard seems to be trying to quell speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't ever \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it seems to indicate that they'd prefer not to do so," said Naomi Fink, Japan strategist at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar is weakening because the main imbalance in the U.S. economy is driven by the (Chinese) renminbi peg to the greenback. So joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not going to do a whole lot on that."\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Friday in Tokyo, Japan's government and the Bank of Japan were checking dollar/yen rates with commercial banks, market sources said, prompting some traders to cover yen long positions. [ID:nTKF106774]\par \par \uc2 Investors will be watching U.S. financial markets when they reopen on Friday after the Thanksgiving holiday on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The market focus will be moves in the U.S. stocks, commodity markets, as well as other asset markets, not only today but in the days ahead," said Jun Kato, senior chief analyst at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 "If those markets start falling, unwinding of the yen and dollar carry trades is expected to accelerate, meaning dollar/yen is then likely stay in a narrow range," he said. (Additional reporting by Satomi Noguchi, Charlotte Cooper; Editing by Joseph Radford) ((kaori.kaneko@thomsonreuters.com; Reuters Messaging: kaori.kaneko.reuters.com@reuters.net; +81-3-6441-1983)) ((If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com))\par \par \uc2 All data taken from Reuters calculated from the levels at 2130 GMT in the previous New York session.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Last    US Close    Pct   YTD Pct   2008\par                                    Change  Change   Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------Euro/dlr   <EUR=>   1.4907   1.5011   -0.69   +6.65   1.3978\par Dlr/yen    <JPY=>   86.210   86.480   -0.31   -4.85   90.600\par Euro/yen <EURJPY=R> 128.50   129.76   -0.97   +1.45   126.66\par Dlr/swiss  <CHF=>   1.0110   1.0028   +0.82   -5.24   1.0669\par Stg/dlr    <GBP=>   1.6393   1.6509   -0.70  +12.08   1.4626\par Dlr/cad    <CAD=>   1.0650   1.0606   +0.41  -12.45   1.2165\par Aus/dlr    <AUD=>   0.9007   0.9128   -1.33  +27.34   0.7073\par Nzd/dlr    <NZD=>   0.7086   0.7159   -1.02  +21.44   0.5835\par Aus/yen <AUDJPY=R>  77.650   78.950   -1.65  +21.27   64.030\par Nzd/yen <NZDJPY=R>  61.090   61.900   -1.31  +15.66   52.820\par Stg/yen <GBPJPY=R>  141.33   142.90   -1.10   +6.84   132.28\par Aus/Nzd <AUDNZD=R>  1.2697   1.2746   -0.38   +4.86   1.2109\par All spots <\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 => Tokyo spots <AFX=> Europe spots <EFX=>\par  Volatilities <FXVOL=> Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par  <TKYFX> World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news [CEN] Economic\par  Forecasts...<ECON>   Official rates...[INT/RATE] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  Diary.......[MI/DIARY]   Top events........[M/DIARY]\par  Diaries...........[DIARY] Diaries Index........[IND/DIARY]\par  Press Digests.....[PRESS] Polls on G7 economies..[SURVEY/]\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 LANGEN|T