\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  WEEK AHEAD: Dubai Debt Worries Could Revive Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 21:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Dubai World's debt standstill could darken prospects for the euro and other risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week, thereby underpinning a rebound for the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about the broader impact of the Dubai government holding company's request for a standstill on its roughly $60 billion in outstanding debt will continue to weigh on financial markets.\par \par \uc2 These worries, which calmed somewhat Friday, may prompt an extension of the rebound in haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the dollar and the yen when trading volumes return to normal after a holiday-thinned week.\par \par \uc2 The overall effect could be to derail, perhaps only temporarily, the rally that recently drove the euro to 15-month highs and carried aloft such risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars.\par \par \uc2 "It was a bit of a game-changer, at least in the short term," said Vassili Serebriakov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Wells Fargo Bank in New York. "I think it's too early to say how long this correction will last and how far it will go."\par \par \uc2 Longer term, pressures on the dollar are likely to remain in place, analysts said. However, the evolving situation in Dubai could provide a pretext for investors to book profits on the euro and other risk-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as year end approaches. That would also tend to support the dollar in coming sessions.\par \par \uc2 "I think this Dubai story becomes a catalyst for investors to lock in some of the really healthy profits that we've seen in the second half of this year," said Omer Esiner, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst at Travelex Global Business Payments in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam's decision to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 5% Wednesday, its third devaluation since June 2008, also amplified investors' awareness of risk, particularly in emerging markets. The fear of financial contagion to other countries is perhaps the biggest threat the Dubai crisis will pose to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Against this background, analysts expect the euro to range between its recent high for 2009 around $1.5150 to as low as $1.4700. The dollar is seen moving between Y84.00 and Y89.00 against the yen.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday afternoon, the euro was trading at $1.4959, down from $1.5009 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y86.70, up from Y86.49, while the euro was at Y129.73, little changed from Y129.74. The U.K. pound was at $1.6487 from $1.6499. The dollar was at CHF1.0065, up from CHF1.0038.\par \par \uc2 The Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 75.023, up from 74.799.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the dollar had at one point dropped trading to a 14-year low against the yen at Y84.82, fostering increased speculation about the possibility of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed a quelling strength in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think the Y85.00 level is important psychological support for [the dollar versus the yen]," said Serebriakov, of \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . "I think the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise materially if we do manage to stay below Y85.00," he added.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities haven't sent clear signals about what level would prompt them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Complicating an already volatile environment for the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there are several significant U.S. data releases next week, including the Institute for Supply Management's November index for the manufacturing sector Tuesday and the ISM index for the services sector Thursday. The most significant economic report, the U.S. nonfarm payrolls data for November, comes Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Obviously [the U.S. jobs report] is going to be a big driver for overall market sentiment, and it's going to play right into sentiment [on the euro versus the dollar]," said Tyson Wright, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Custom House, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  services firm in British Columbia.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect that the U.S. lost about 120,000 jobs in November, better than the 190,000 decline in the previous month, and that the unemployment rate held steady at 10.2%.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, encouraging economic indicators have supported risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a recovery scenario lessens the dollar's safe-haven appeal.\par \par \uc2 Outside of the U.S., two important \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings loom.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  is scheduled to hold a policy meeting. Another interest-rate increase from Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would encourage more confidence about global economic prospects, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold a policy meeting, although it is expected to keep rates steady. Investors will be watching closely for clues from the ECB on future changes in monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 -By Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020; don.curren@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 11-27-09 1513ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091127e5br000bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
