\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei Off 1.8% Midday; Yen, Dubai Fears Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 1,636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 03:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Tokyo. 813 6895 7550; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei ends morning session down 1.8% at 9213.51; intraday low so far 9180.47 and further decline today may be limited as USD/JPY seems to have temporarily stabilized after brief drop below 85.00 earlier this morning, says Tachibana Securities market analyst Kenichi Hirano. Still, Nikkei may fall more next week and test technical support at July 13 intraday low of 9050.33, he says; "In addition to worries about further yen strengthening, the unfolding debt crisis in Dubai is bringing back fears over European financial markets." General contractors sharply lower on Nikkei report they have taken on major contracts tendered by Dubai World's property development unit; Shimizu (1803.TO) down 8.3% at Y277, Taisei (1803.TO) down 7.7% at Y144. 28/33 Topix subindexes lower, with iron & steel (down 3.3%), nonferrous metals (down 3.1%), construction (down 3.0%) pacing decline. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) down 3.3% at Y2,675. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 3.0% at Y2,300. Canon (7751.TO) down 1.8% at Y3,230. (JUO)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares set to follow regional markets lower after European stocks sold off heavily on concerns over Dubai World debt. "While we believe the fundamental impact to Asia and Thailand's economy is limited, risk aversion will likely dominate the market," says KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul; but adds doesn't expect free-fall, with some bargain hunting around technical support levels of 678, then 670. Any upside capped at 690. Market closed down 1.4% at 685.73 yesterday. (LHM)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY rises mildly to 6.8272 vs 6.8265 last close following overnight USD rebound vs EUR, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; but PBOC sets central parity at 6.8269, nearly unchanged vs 6.8268 yesterday, well below 6.8275 some traders anticipated. "This shows that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep the central parity steady no matter how volatile the dollar is against global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. USD/CNY tipped at 6.8268-6.8278 for session.(JSX)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to extend loss after state media reports \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposing government to end first stimulus package, which helps offset four percentage points off interest rates on loans enterprises take from commercial lenders, by end-December, instead of end-March 2010. "Sentiment continues to be weak as more bad news is coming, after the devaluation of the dong yesterday and the hike of benchmark policy rate to 8% from next month from current 7%, and many players are hesitant to enter to buy now on concerns companies will face higher \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs soon," says trader with Agribank Securities. Tips key index to range in 460-462 after falling 4.1% to 482.6 yesterday.(HAA)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] JGB yield curve likely to stay under downward pressure, with medium zone expected to outperform, as market expected to start pricing in chances of BOJ additional easing steps even before BOJ releases interim review of outlook report in January, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  strategist Kenro Kawano; 10-year cash JGB yield now down 2.0 bps at 1.260%, 5-year yield also down 2.0 bps at 0.550%. Adds liquidity provision in short-term market, such as increases in treasury discount bill buying, more likely than increases in JGB outright purchases. Lead December JGB futures stabilizing; now +0.22 at 139.72.(MEF)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered says USD/JPY declines have come amid thin liquidity, with short-term momentum indicators mixed, weekly charts still bearish. Notes that, looking at JPY real effective exchange rate and JPY nominal effective exchange rate, yen is nowhere near its highs of January and February 2009; "at that time, the BOJ chose not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market even though the yen REER and NEER were higher than in 2003-04, when the BOJ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The speed of the appreciation may be the key cause of concern." Fundamentally, house is neutral on JPY; "investor repatriation is likely to remain relatively strong ahead of the fiscal year-end in March 2010, but growth prospects and valuation are medium-term concerns."(RXM)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2  says JPY to reach short-term peak, JPY looks "increasingly overbought." Notes technicals may demonstrate near-term peak of yen appreciation vs dollar. "With a two-month cycle in place, the yen has recently tended to peak out in odd months and experience a correction in even months." Says \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate's 5-week moving average spread is approaching level seen when yen hit short-term peak in Oct. Technically, yen looks increasingly overbought from Y87.50 level, says house. USD/JPY hit 84.82 in early trading, its lowest level since 1995 (pair now 86.00/86.02). Falling USD/JPY widely attributed as single most compelling factor behind Japan stock market fall, as exporters suffer directly from higher yen vis-a-vis major trading partners' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially USD and EUR. (BMF)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY's earlier drop to 14-year low at 84.82 was not related to fundamentals - it was due to "the pair breaching levels where there were lots of stop-loss selling orders," says Daisuke Karakama, market economist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 ; "we saw this from earlier in the week at 88, then 87 and today at 86." While FOMC minutes released Tuesday weighed on USD by suggesting Fed not unduly concerned with weak USD, likely to maintain ultra-low rates for foreseeable future, pace of USD/JPY falls thereafter stemmed from technical factors; "for that reason, the kind of bounces like we're seeing this morning (with the pair now at 86.04) could persist." But adds while some concern over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  partially behind pair's rebound, comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  finance minister Fujii, other officials "have maintained a relatively even tone from yesterday"; that's leading some to speculate any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only come with much sharper USD/JPY fall.(ATM)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Dubai debt fears are a storm in a teacup for AUD as fundamentals haven't changed a bit, says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Richard Grace. Says relative economic strength continues unbounded, while U.S. yields remain in doldrums. "It is difficult to know to what extent confidence will be affected, but it is unlikely the AUD will fall too far." Also, expects RBA to hike by 25 bps next week. "We would look to buy AUD on dips, but wait until Friday and assessing the open of the US stock market before entering large long AUD positions." AUD/USD last 0.9059 vs low of 0.9017 earlier in day. (EGC)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] November HSI futures down 1.8% at 21,777, indicating cash market expected to open lower as Dubai crisis likely to weigh on financial counters. November contract ended yesterday at 33-point discount to cash, December contract at 50-point discount to cash, suggests bearish sentiment expected to persist near-term. November futures going to expire today, with open interest down 13,202 lots to 28,454, as contracts rolling over to December, with open interest for December at 74,090 lots.(SUT)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher amid broad risk aversion, Kospi's 2.0% fall, traders say; 3-year yield down 7 bps at 4.13%, 5-year yield down 6 bps at 4.65%, December futures +23 ticks at 110.17. Government says late yesterday it will sell KRW4.413 trillion in treasurys at auctions in December vs KRW5.548 trillion treasurys sold in November. "The overseas environment as well as the demand/supply conditions all seem favorable for local bonds," says local securities firm trader. Notes "the conditions seem set now for the 3-year bond yields to target the key 4.00% level next, which looks possible after the industrial output data is out" later this month; note, Dow Jones poll tips October industrial production up 3.2% on year, slower than 11.0% growth recorded in September. Bond gains to be capped on profit taking, but expects overall bullish sentiment to stay. Puts 3-year yield support at 4.10% for day.(MJL)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 2.5% at 4590.9, remains weak after hitting 3-week low of 4564.1 as Dubai World debt restructuring hit European financial stocks. Index finding support from lower Bollinger band at 4575.0, but could test Nov. 5 low at 4502.5 after breaking uptrend line support today at 4650.0. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) the biggest drag, down 2.9% at A$40.61, though former resistance near A$40.00 offering support. Financial sector is weakest, with major banks down more than 3.0%, \uc2 Macquarie Group\uc2  (MQG.AU) down 4.5%. ANZ (ANZ.AU) says it has no material exposure to Dubai World, while CBA (CBA.AU) declines comment. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU), NAB (NAB.AU) not immediately available for comment. U.S. stock index futures also keeping Australian market under pressure, with S&P 500 futures down about 2.1%, DJIA futures down 1.7%. "The implications for Australian banks are probably small, but Dubai has put some risk back into the financial sector," says RBS head of sales Justin Gallagher. "This may be a buying opportunity in some stocks, but we expect to see the U.S. react negatively tonight. Our market is still fairly expensive on 16X (2010 earnings consensus)". (DWR) [ 11-26-09 2111ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091127e5br0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
