\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-GLOBAL MARKETS-Dlr hits 14-yr low vs yen, gold at new high\b0\par\par\uc2 891 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2009\par 05:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dlr hits 14-yr low vs yen as Tokyo says won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Gold punches new high on dollar weakness\par \par \uc2 * Asian shares weak as investors digest mixed data\par \par \uc2 * European shares set to open lower (Repeats to more subscribers)\par \par \uc2 By Susan Fenton\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Nov 26 (Reuters) - The dollar slumped to a 14-year low against the yen on Thursday, helping gold scale another record high, while Asian stocks faltered as investors digested a mixed batch of economic data.\par \par \uc2 Financial bookmakers expected European stockmarkets to open lower while U.S. markets will be closed for the Thanksgiving holiday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar skidded below 87 yen <JPY=> to its lowest level since July 1995 as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was not planning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. It also hit a 15-month low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <.DXY> as investors betted on prolonged weakness in the world's reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gold punched a new record above $1,192.60 an ounce on expectations more central banks would buy the precious metal to diversify away from the dollar, which has lost more than 8 percent against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year and is heading for its biggest yearly slide in six years.\par \par \uc2 Investors also sought out the Swiss franc <CHF=> as a safe way to diversify, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to an 18-month high although analysts expected the Swiss authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  once European markets opened.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Japanese finance minister Yoshihiko Noda told Reuters that Tokyo was not planning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to rein in the yen. [ID:nT371315] That helped depress shares of Japanese exporters and pushed Tokyo's Nikkei stock index <.N225> down 0.6 percent to a four-month low.\par \par \uc2 "This will contribute still further to the current investors' shunning of Japan. If there were signs that the major problems were being dealt with things might get better, but as they are now it's pretty hard," said Noritsugu Hirakawa, a strategist at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Japan is back in deflation and the steel industry federation said the current yen rate could push Japan back into recession and urged the authorities to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nTKB006568]\par \par \uc2 DISMAL START FOR MINSHENG IPO\par \par \uc2 Equity markets across Asia were subdued ahead of Thanksgiving holiday and as investors digested mixed economic data.\par \par \uc2 U.S. jobless claims fell to the lowest in more than a year last week and consumer spending and home sales rose more than expected in October but durable goods orders unexpectedly fell last month.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI index of Asia Pacific stocks traded outside Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> was down 0.7 percent and the Thomson Reuters index of regional shares <.TRXFLDAXPU> was 1 percent lower.\par \par \uc2 Shares in China slumped more than 3 percent as banking stocks fell on concerns about shrinking lending and possible needs to raise capital.\par \par \uc2 That dampened the Hong Kong trading debut of China's Minsheng Bank , the country's seventh-largest lender. The $3.9 billion issue, the world's fifth-biggest IPO so far this year, saw Minsheng shares fall to as low as HK$8.95 from an offer price of HK$9.08.\par \par \uc2 Friday marks the start of the U.S. Christmas holiday shopping season and will provide a key indicator of demand for Asian exports in the final months of the year.\par \par \uc2 Asia-Pacific stock markets outside Japan have already rallied nearly 70 percent this year, making investors cautious as the year-end nears. However, analysts say the region still looks a good bet compared with other parts of the world as Western consumer demand remains fragile.\par \par \uc2 The Middle East has also lost some of its shine with Dubai on Wednesday reporting that two of its flagship firms planned to delay repayment on billions of dollars of debt [ID:nGEE5A02L1].\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar <AUD=> held firm as a rate rise still looked to be on the cards next week after data showed a sharp upgrade in Australian business spending plans even though investment unexpectedly slipped last quarter. [ID:nSYD53704]\par \par \uc2 "For the Reserve Bank (of Australia), rates at these levels are too low and we anticipate a move upwards in December of 25 basis points," said Andrew Hanlan, senior economist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Australia.\par \par \uc2 In Vietnam, the dong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted near its new low point against the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by more than 5 percent on Wednesday and narrowed its trading band. [VN-FRX-CEN-RTRS]\par \par \uc2 The oil price dipped 0.8 percent but held above $77 a barrel. (Additional reporting by Aiko Hayashi in TOKYO and the SYDNEY newsroom; editing by Kazunori Takada) (susan.fenton@thomsonreuters.com; +852 2843 6367; Reuters Messaging: susan.fenton.thomsonreuters.com@reuters.net)\par \par \uc2 ((For the state of play of Asian stock markets please click on: <0#.INDEXA>))\par \par \uc2 * For Reuters Global Investing Blog, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.reuters.com/globalinvesting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.reuters.com/globalinvesting}}}\par \par \uc2 * For the MacroScope Blog, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.reuters.com/macroscope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.reuters.com/macroscope}}}\par \par \uc2 * For Hedge Fund Blog, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.reuters.com/hedgehub"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.reuters.com/hedgehub}}}\uc2  ((If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com))\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL (WRAPUP 2)|LANGEN|SI|AUF|Z|IF|SNS|J|ABN|E|U|ND|PHD|SNI|GNN|DNP|PCO   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091126e5bq0006y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
