\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says no plans to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to curb yen\b0\par\par\uc2 984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2009\par 01:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Yen jumps to 14-year high against U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 * Deputy finmin Noda: not mulling \b \highlight22\uc2 fx intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now\par \par \uc2 * Finmin Fujii: now the time to watch \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets\par \par \uc2 * Market not expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in face of broad dlr fall\par \par \uc2 * In trade-weighted terms yen rising modestly (Adds graphic, company and analyst comments)\par \par \uc2 By Stanley White\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 26 (Reuters) - The Japanese finance minister and one of his deputies said they had no plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to stop the yen's rise as it jumped to a 14-year high on Thursday, saying current moves were led by the dollar's fall.\par \par \uc2 "We are not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now," Yoshihiko Noda, a deputy finance minister, told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The dollar slumped to a 14-year low of 86.29 yen on trading platform EBS, with Noda's comment reinforcing a view that Japan would not step into the markets to try to combat persistent dollar weakness.\par \par \uc2 "This is at the stage where we should watch," Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii added.\par \par \uc2 The weak dollar is a dilemma for Japan's new government, in power for just two months, as it threatens export earnings and could slow an economic recovery. A major Japanese exporter decried the yen's "extraordinary" level.\par \par \uc2 Analysts, however, played down the chances of a Japanese move against a broad fall in the dollar, which hit a 15-month low on Thursday against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Would I expect to see physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? No. Physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being frowned upon so we need to see a much weaker dollar first," said \uc2 Robert Rennie\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Both Fujii and Noda have stressed the yen's gains were due to a broad fall by the dollar, although Fujii also used the standard line that Japan would act if it felt it was needed.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether current exchange rate moves were abnormal, Fujii told reporters: "We're trying to find that out now."\par \par \uc2 NODA PUSHES YEN UP\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said Noda's rejection of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped foreign players push the yen higher, saying option-linked buying and importer bids emerged below 87 yen per dollar but was overwhelmed by dollar selling.\par \par \uc2 The yen has jumped around 17 percent against the dollar in less than eight months but has risen less than 5 percent since the end of 2008.\par \par \uc2 In trade-weighted terms, its rise is also modest with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  well below highs hit in January, as this graphic shows: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/zus63g"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/zus63g}}}\par \par \uc2 The yen's real effective exchange rate -- a trade-weighted, inflation-adjusted measure of its broad value calculated once a month by the Bank of Japan -- was 117.8 in October, roughly 9 percent below an eight-year high of 128.9 hit in January.\par \par \uc2 Investors are increasingly on edge about Japan, where the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are bickering over how to combat deflation. The government is also juggling a need for stimulus to prevent a second recession this year with investor unease about its huge debt pile.\par \par \uc2 Those woes, along with the sinking dollar, have sent the Nikkei stock average to a four-month low and pushed 10-year Japanese government bond futures to a seven-week high.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily earlier in the decade to stop a rising yen from harming exports but they quit in March 2004 at the end of a 15-month long spree in which they sold 35 trillion yen to try to shield a struggling economy.\par \par \uc2 EXPORTER PAIN\par \par \uc2 Japan's Democratic Party-led government, which voters swept to power in August, has criticised the previous administration for coddling the country's exporters by keeping the yen weak.\par \par \uc2 However the big companies are major drivers of growth for Japan and Shoji Muneoka, president of the world's second-biggest steelmaker \uc2 Nippon Steel Corp\uc2 , said on Thursday said the yen was reaching "extraordinary" levels against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "That will cause a big problem to steelmakers and our customers like carmakers, electronics manufacturers and shipbuilders because their export levels are now quite high," Muneoka told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Fujii has previously made comments suggesting a stronger yen would benefit Japan as that could help boost domestic demand. While Fujii later softened his tone, economists say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to maintain its policy of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that it has held since 2004.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has changed considerably since the late 1990s when countries were willing to support the dollar as a key global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Hideki Hayashi, global strategist at Mizuho Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "Now the world is letting the dollar fall as U.S. rates stay at low levels."\par \par \uc2 Japan's new government is shifting fiscal spending to measures that will support households from large-scale public works projects that were a common feature of the previous administration's economic policy.\par \par \uc2 Some economists say cutting public works spending could cause Japan's growth to slow temporarily early next year and that it will take time for support of the household sector to make a positive contribution to growth.\par \par \uc2 Japan's exports have showed strong signs of recovery due to stimulus measures in overseas economies. Investors are becoming worried that a strengthening yen will harm future export growth and snuff out Japan's recovery from its worst recession in decades before the government's policies take hold. (additional reporting by Sumio Ito and Yuko Inoue; Editing by Neil Fullick and Rodney Joyce) ((stanley.white@thomsonreuters.com; +81 3 6441 1984; Reuters Messaging: stanley.white.reuters.com@reuters.net)) ((If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com))\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/YEN (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091126e5bq00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
