\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/CZK Higher; Likely To Stay Above 26.00\b0\par\par\uc2 1,269 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2009\par 12:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1156 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades higher at 26.080-26.110 from 26.01 late Wednesday but down from 26.180 earlier in the day, in part tracking the region. "Trading volumes are low and the koruna is currently where many traders, including myself, feel it should," Raiffeisenbank trader Ivo Prokop says, adding the EUR/CZK cross is likely to stay around its current levels for today. "We'll see what US markets will do with it when they reopen tomorrow after today's holidays," he says. Earlier in the day CZK hit support at levels just under 26.200 against EUR. "Just bellow 26.200 people started selling euros because the koruna got too cheap," Prokop says, adding that he still expects EUR/CZK to stay above 26.000 for now. (LEO)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1145 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's economy should begin to post decent annual growth by 4Q, modest growth of 4.1% in 2010, vs government forecast of +4.39%, as exports recovery gains momentum, aided by global manufacturing inventory restocking, strong final demand from China, says KGI Securities economist Wenyen Fang. Pickup in domestic private consumption is likely to extend into next year, but growth is limited by real wage decline, Fang says, adding private investment is likely up 10%-15% in 2010, as more companies announced to significantly increase capital expansion. But says despite brighter economic outlook, Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep rates unchanged through 1H10. (CHY)\par \par \uc2 1131 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro-zone overnight money market rates Thursday are quoted slightly higher between 0.29% and 0.34% from 0.28% and 0.33% Wednesday, a trader says. Notes business is very quiet. Dubai World's debt issue is not thought of as relevant for the euro zone money market. Wednesday's bank reserves with the ECB rose to EUR231.642B from EUR221.897B Tuesday. Autonomous liquidity factors decreased to EUR394.015B from EUR403.206B. Average holdings decreased to EUR232.308B, still above the ECB's minimum reserve requirement of EUR210.234B. (MPT/NAD)\par \par \uc2 1121 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY has come under severe bear pressure, trading within a long-term bear channel originating from the 124.14 reaction high set in June 2007. The push into new 2009 lows below January's 87.10 low threatens room for further weakness towards 85.00 and 82.30. See Thursday's Charting Europe column by Francis Bray. (BFR)\par \par \uc2 1119 GMT [Dow Jones] Signs of wholesale profit taking in JPY crosses combined with general USD weakness are a toxic combination for USD/JPY, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Now at 86.80, the bank says the risk is that resistance between 88.00-20 caps any rallies and a Friday close below 87.15 provides additional confirmation that a return to the 1995 lows of 79.90 is underway. USD/JPY now at 86.77. (GST)\par \par \uc2 1115 GMT [Dow Jones] The Middle-East region is unlikely to constitute more than 1-2% of European banks' exposures [loan books] and Dubai would be a small portion of this, according to analysis by \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says European banks in its coverage could have exposure of about EUR13B. For \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (HSBA.LN) its Middle East loan book is about $25B, or 3% of the group, and the group discloses that the UAE accounts for $16B or around 2% of group loans. For Standard Chartered (STAN.LN), MESA - Middle East and South Asia - is a loan book of about $18B or 10% of group loans but with exposure to several countries in the region. (ANT)\par \par \uc2 1104 GMT [Dow Jones] UK banking stocks decline sharply Thursday, suffering on exposure to Dubai World's debt issues, says a trader. "Dubai's stalling repayment request has lead to fears that banks with direct exposure may suffer with more bad debt, therefore the sector as a whole is taking a defensive posture this morning," the trader says. However, another trader notes that Dubai has GBP48B of debt, while the UK government has spent GBP37B bailing out three banks, so it is not that bad for Dubai yet. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (HBC) trades -4.3% at 709p, Standard Chartered (STAN.LN) -3.7% at 1547p, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  (BARC.LN) -3.8% at 304p, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  (RBS.LN) -4.2% at 34p, \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2  (LLOY.LN) -3.8% at 91p. (ISD)\par \par \uc2 1102 GMT [Dow Jones] Spreads on 10-year Greek Government Bonds (GGB) widened to 200 basis points over German bunds, compared with 185bp Wednesday, as investors continue to sell off Greek bonds. "The market has been hit by a raft of bad news," says a trader at a Greek bank. "That includes the fears over a debt default by Dubai World, and the sell-off in the stock market." Next resistance level seen at 215-220 bp. (AKG)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT [Dow Jones] After USD/CHF closed below parity Wednesday, the risks of returning to the 2008 low of 0.9640 are increasing, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . A weekly close below parity would provide additional ammunition, as would the USD index closing below 74.75. Topside, BarCap says resistance comes in at 1.0040 and 1.0075 and ideally this should cap any rallies. USD/CHF now at 1.0003, the USD index trades at 74.53. (GST)\par \par \uc2 1057 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds hover in range as high weekly uptick in primary prices fails to faze market reveling in dwindling government bond supply; 6.90% 2019 bond at 98.16 vs 98.00 last close. "There were good volumes today, showing decent buying appetite," says dealer with foreign bank. Tips bonds to continue to inch up as markets reap benefits on thin supply. Tips benchmark 10-year bond yield to hit 7.10% over next few sessions. Inflation shocks, next year's likely heavy government borrowing still far away, notes dealer. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 1053 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY's surge to 14-year high today has Japan worried, but government unlikely to join many of its Asia counterparts and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets yet. There's good reason for Tokyo's reticence, says \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA. The dollar's plunge looks stomach-wrenching on a chart but given deflation has gripped Japan for much of past decade while prices have risen elsewhere in world, yen is significantly weaker now in real terms than when it was last at current levels against dollar. What's more, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options market continues to signal only modest turbulence for dollar/yen exchange rate--something authorities watch as much as they do spot levels. See \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Has Good Reason Not To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Yet (KAO)\par \par \uc2 1046 GMT [Dow Jones] "Parasite singles" is rather unflattering name given to key Japanese demographic: unmarried women and men in 20s and early 30s living with parents and able to blow large portions of paychecks on themselves. Nearly half of Japan's single men and women between age of 20 and 34 live at home. This group is losing yen to spend -- and sales of European-made handbags, shoes and watches tumbling. Insecurity over jobs plus falling wages and sluggish economic growth all account for decline in spending. Japan key market for European luxury brands. Country accounted for 14% of revenue in 2008. Include Japanese tourists and figure almost doubles for some, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. In 2014, Japanese will account for only 9%. See HEARD ON THE STREET: Japan's Demographics Claim Fashion Victims (JCS) [ 11-26-09 0656ET ]\p