\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Has Good Reason Not To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Yet- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2009\par 11:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1053 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY's surge to 14-year high today has Japan worried, but government unlikely to join many of its Asia counterparts and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets yet. There's good reason for Tokyo's reticence, says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA. The dollar's plunge looks stomach-wrenching on a chart but given deflation has gripped Japan for much of past decade while prices have risen elsewhere in world, yen is significantly weaker now in real terms than when it was last at current levels against dollar. What's more, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options market continues to signal only modest turbulence for dollar/yen exchange rate--something authorities watch as much as they do spot levels. See \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Has Good Reason Not To \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Yet (KAO)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1046 GMT [Dow Jones] "Parasite singles" is rather unflattering name given to key Japanese demographic: unmarried women and men in 20s and early 30s living with parents and able to blow large portions of paychecks on themselves. Nearly half of Japan's single men and women between age of 20 and 34 live at home. This group is losing yen to spend -- and sales of European-made handbags, shoes and watches tumbling. Insecurity over jobs plus falling wages and sluggish economic growth all account for decline in spending. Japan key market for European luxury brands. Country accounted for 14% of revenue in 2008. Include Japanese tourists and figure almost doubles for some, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. In 2014, Japanese will account for only 9%. See HEARD ON THE STREET: Japan's Demographics Claim Fashion Victims (JCS)\par \par \uc2 1042 GMT [Dow Jones] Panmure Gordon lifts \uc2 Daily Mail & General Trust\uc2  (DMGT.LN) target price to 500p from 400p after the company's FY results. Panmure says the results are well ahead of expectations at the headline profits level. Panmure adds FY'09 pretax profit is around 7% better than expected. Panmure says the tax charge guidance is encouraging and notes there is scope for material EPS upgrades. The brokerage keeps a buy rating. Shares -1.6% at 430p. (MMA)\par \par \uc2 1036 GMT [Dow Jones] SNB has in all probability \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep CHF from rising further, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. "There's market talk the SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  today through the BIS, selling Swiss francs against the dollar, this time," \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kasper Kirkegaard says, pegging the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level at 0.9950. Afterwards, SNB likely also moved on EUR/CHF when the cross was approaching 1.50, he adds. SNB doesn't comment, BIS not immediately reachable. USD/CHF at 0.998, EUR/CHF at 1.5083. (MGE)\par \par \uc2 1035 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India July-September quarter GDP likely +6.3% on year vs +6.1% on year in April-June, +7.7% same period last year, indicates Dow Jones poll; performance likely buoyed by strong growth in manufacturing, offsetting possible decline in farm production. "We see industry as the front runner in terms of sectoral growth prospects and see it as a crucial support to headline growth in the remainder of the year," says \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2 . Growth forecasts for calendar 3Q GDP in poll range from 5.1%-6.6%. Data due at 0630 GMT Monday. (NEC)\par \par \uc2 1034 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF's strong intraday rebound seen since it dropped to the major 1.5010 support area Thursday implies this level will continue to hold although the cross will likely find resistance at 1.5090, 1.5125 and 1.5137 says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones Jones. The support held, as it did in April, May and June. For a strategy Jones favors buying any further dips to 1.5025-10 with a target if 1.5125, leaving a stop and reverse below 1.50 as this would open risk toward 1.49 and 1.4570. EUR/CHF now at 1.5082. (GST)\par \par \uc2 1033 GMT [Dow Jones] The SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/CHF Thursday via the electronic broking systems rather than dealing direct with banks, says a London based trader. The SNB didn't comment. USD/CHF spiked from around 0.9965 to 1.0030 in late Asian trade Thursday, it now trades at 1.00. (GST)\par \par \uc2 1032 GMT [Dow Jones] The SNB surprised the markets Thursday by buying USD/CHF after the spot traded below parity, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . The bank notes in previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , namely March 12, June 25 and Sept. 30, the SNB primarily bought EUR/CHF although it also showed its hand in USD/CHF. The SNB didn't comment. This morning the cross fell to a five-month low of 1.5012 before the SNB acted, but this was a function of the overnight USD moves rather than specific EUR/CHF flows and that's why the SNB chose to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/CHF to curb CHF strength. USD/CHF now at 1.00, EUR/CHF at 1.5083. (GST)\par \par \uc2 1028 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely hike rates in 1Q, reiterates \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Frederic Neumann, after Taiwan government says 3Q GDP down 1.29%, smallest decline in 12 months. Notes inflation isn't concern, given 2010 CPI forecast of +0.92%. "With liquidity flush, and growth intact, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may well wish to signal to markets that it is on top of things," says Neumann. Notes 3Q private consumption +2.21% on-year, first positive reading in 3 quarters, strongest since 2005, while consumption likely to improve further as jobless rate has started to fall. Adds it may further raise Taiwan's 2010 GDP forecast, now at 4.4% vs government's forecast of 4.39%. "We wouldn't be surprised if we will be talking about figures of above 5% in due course." (JEH)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR at intraday high as Sensex posts heavy losses in last few hours, dollar rebounds some globally after worries of debt repayment default by Dubai send investors back to relatively safer dollar; pair at intraday high of 46.47 vs 46.21 late Wednesday, dealer tips could rise to 46.50 today. "The rapid losses for the Sensex in the last few hours have pulled the rupee down," says dealer with foreign bank. Sensex closed down provisional 2.0% at 16,854.93. (AVK)\par \par \uc2 1019 GMT [Dow Jones] India interest rate swaps bounce off early lows as primary prices continue steady upmove in weekly data print earlier; 1-year OIS at 4.53%/4.57% vs 4.54%/4.57% last close, 5-year OIS at 6.43%/6.47% vs 6.45%.6.48% last close. "There was some pretty strong receiving in the morning as people become increasingly skeptical of the recovery, but the primary price print turned that around," says foreign bank dealer. Notes, flattening still main trend on curve with longer end of curve showing softness. India's primary articles index rose 1.2% to 279.7 in week to Nov. 14 from 276.4 previous week. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 1013 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to 33.16-33.17 vs 33.18 late yesterday, but buying by importers, short-covering by interbank players helps lift pair off 33.10, fresh 17-month low. Suspected mild BOT \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also slows pace of THB rise, say dealers. "Players' short-covering limits the downside. They expect USD will rebound in short term. However, expectations of USD weakness in the long-term are still intact." 33.10-33.20 band expected. (OPW) [ 11-26-09 0553ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091126e5bq00086\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
