\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Sinks To 15-Mo Lows; Euro Close To $1.51\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2009\par 16:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar was near 15-month lows against its major rivals Wednesday, with the euro piercing $1.50, as renewed expectations that interest rates in the U.S. will remain low for longer than in other large economies exacerbated bets against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar was sharply higher after a report that the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is planning to invest part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Broad U.S. dollar weakness also underpinned the Canadian dollar's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Trading strategy Wednesday has been "anything but the dollar," said Andrew Wilkinson, senior market analyst at Interactive Brokers in Greenwich, Conn.\par \par \uc2 The euro smashed through the psychologically key $1.50 level to touch a 2009 high just below $1.51. The dollar sank to a 10-month low below Y88, also a key psychological level for investors, said John McCarthy, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at ING Capital Markets in New York. He said the onslaught on the dollar was likely exacerbated by thin trading conditions ahead of the Thanksgiving holiday in the U.S. on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "In these thinner markets, you get to these extreme [technical] levels, and when they break, it can get quite volatile," said McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 The euro was recently trading at $1.5057 from $1.4965 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y87.74 from Y88.55, while the euro was at Y132.12 from Y132.49. The U.K. pound was at $1.6665 from $1.6586. The dollar was at CHF1.0024 from CHF1.0089.\par \par \uc2 The Dollar Index, which tracks the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a trade-weighted basket of six others, was at 74.628 from 75.116. In earlier trading, the Dollar Index hit a 15-month low.\par \par \uc2 The broad dollar selloff may have been sparked overnight by rising speculation that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  would raise interest rates by another 25 basis points when it meets next week. Stocks and commodities also recovered in Asian trading, helping to boost appetite for risk-sensitive investments at the expense of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Diverging interest rate expectations between the U.S. and other key global economies have been a major force behind the dollar's slide and rush of investment into assets with more exposure to risk.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve had set the stage Tuesday afternoon for a fresh bout of dollar weakness. The minutes of its most recent policy meeting revealed that the Fed isn't yet close to withdrawing economic stimulus. The minutes also said the recent fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  value of the dollar had been orderly, a sign the Fed isn't unduly concerned with the current pace of dollar weakness.\par \par \uc2 "By showing a laissez faire attitude in the case of orderly decline, the Fed might be ushering in the next wave of dollar losses," Wilkinson said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also hit a 19-month low against the Swiss franc, tumbling at one point below parity with the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though it was trading just above that level in the New York session.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii said the dollar's fall to a 10-month low against the yen was symptomatic of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness, not the yen's strength, the Kyodo news service reported.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Fujii attributed the dollar's recent weakness to market expectations of the U.S. continuing with a low interest rate policy. On Wednesday, Fujii again suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates "move abnormally," Kyodo reported.\par \par \uc2 After breaking sharply from their recent well-worn ranges, many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have established new ranges, McCarthy said, with the euro to trade between $1.49 and $1.52 and investors watching closely to determine whether the dollar will break below Y87.00, a key support level.\par \par \uc2 The report about the diversification of Russia's reserves wasn't totally unexpected. In late September, a top official with the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had told reporters that Russia might, in the future, add \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Canadian and Australian dollars to its reserves.\par \par \uc2 "The Canadian dollar was already stronger ahead of those comments on the back of the U.S. dollar being sold generally," said Adam Cole, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in London. "It's partly (Canadian dollar) specific and it's partly just the dollar looking very sad [against] all the majors," he said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.0484 from C$1.0592 late Tuesday. Its session low of C$1.0452 is its lowest level since Nov. 18, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Don Curren in Toronto and Katie Martin in London contributed to this\par \par \uc2 article.) [ 11-25-09 1046ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091125e5bp000br\par }\