\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2009\par 13:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA ECONOMIC RECOVERY CONTINUES TO STRENGTHEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's industrial output growth in October accelerated to a 19-month high and the decline in exports shrank, bolstering the country's economic rebound as demand at home and abroad improved. (published 0556 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC TWEAKS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY WORDING AMID YUAN-RISE PRESSURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  made a rare change Wednesday to the official language of its exchange-rate policy, giving a nod to concerns about the declining dollar and surging capital inflows just as the country comes under increasing pressure to appreciate its tightly-controlled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 1117 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DOLLAR SINKS TO 15-MONTH LOW ON FED CARRY-TRADE COMMENTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a speech late Tuesday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Richard Fisher\f28 \uc2 \f2  said he was aware that the Fed's current stance of keeping interest rates low for an "extended period" was denting the dollar. (published 0918 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN SEPTEMBER CORE MACHINERY ORDERS +10.5% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese core machinery orders rose at the surprisingly fast pace of 10.5% from the previous month in September due to strong demand from non-manufacturers and overseas customers, the Cabinet Office said Wednesday. It was the latest sign of improvement for Japan's economy. (published 0258 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE SEES SLOW RECOVERY, SIGNALS STEADY POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England is likely to keep policy steady for an extended period amid what is likely to be a "slow" U.K. economic recovery, policymakers said Wednesday. (published 1037 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE KING:WRONG TO ASSUME END TO QUANTITATIVE EASING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. policy makers could still extend the Bank of England's quantitative easing program, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governor Mervyn King said Wednesday. (published 1137 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par STERLING DIVES AS BOE WELCOMES WEAKNESS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling has lurched sharply lower after Bank of England Governor Mervyn King said in a press conference accompanying the BOE's quarterly inflation report that recent sterling weakness would be supportive to an export-led recovery. (published 1123 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FITCH: FURTHER STIMULUS COULD HURT UK RATING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K.'s sovereign credit ratings could suffer if the government introduces another sizeable stimulus package, a senior official at Fitch Ratings said Wednesday. (published 0549 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SLOWER UK JOBLESS RISE POINTS TO RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. unemployment continued to rise in October but at the slowest pace in 18 months, encouraging hopes that the steep increases reported earlier this year are coming to an end as the economy shows signs of emerging from recession, data showed Wednesday. (published 1133 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GEITHNER TAKES MEASURED STANCE TO DOLLAR'S DECLINE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said Wednesday that maintaining a strong dollar is "very important" for the country's economy, sticking to his mantra on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy as the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues its broad downtrend. (published 0700 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD BANK: DOLLAR IS SECURE; US MUST TACKLE DEBT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar is "relatively secure," but Washington must deal with the huge budget deficits that are piling up, World Bank President Robert Zoellick said Wednesday. (published 0442 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: US NEEDS TO MOVE MORE AGGRESSIVELY ON FREE TRADE IN ASIA -CHAMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. needs to be more proactive in reaching trade deals with Asian nations or risk getting left behind as the region pursues its own free-trade pacts, the president of the American Chamber of Commerce said Wednesday. (published 0928 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par APEC URGED TO FIGHT PROTECTIONISM; LEAD ON DOHA ROUND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia-Pacific economies led by the United States and China opened annual talks Wednesday with calls to fight protectionism or risk reversing the region's "fragile" economic rebound. (published 0747 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA AIMS FOR AUTO, PARTS EXPORTS TO RISE 10% A YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's government said Wednesday it aims to expand its exports of automobiles and automobile parts by an average of 10% a year through 2011, and is targeting US$85 billion worth of automobile and automobile parts exports a year by 2015. (published 1142 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN OFFICIAL: DON'T EXPECT APEC TO FOCUS ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Pacific Rim leaders likely won't focus on exchange rates during their meetings this week and should leave any discussion of the yuan to talks between China and the U.S., a Japanese government official said Wednesday. (published 0606 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EX-MOF KURODA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  UNLIKELY TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO WEAKEN YEN \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to stick to its policy of staying away from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market given the stable yen, and the country's long-term interest rates will likely remain low, former Japanese vice minister for international affairs and now president of the ADB Haruhiko Kuroda said Wednesday. (published 1118 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN IFO: EURO-ZONE ECONOMIC CLIMATE IMPROVES IN 4Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro-zone's economic climate improved in the fourth quarter as people grew more optimistic about the economic outlook, Germany's Ifo Institute said Wednesday. (published 1002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN MIN: MUST GRADUALLY WITHDRAW FISCAL AID \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany must gradually withdraw fiscal policies meant to stimulate its economy in the midst of crisis, the country's new Economics Minister Rainer Bruederle said Wednesday. (published 1007 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RISING INFLATION THREAT SEEN IN SOUTH ASIA - WORLD BANK ECONOMIST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rising inflation is posing a threat to South Asia, with the situation most worrying in the Maldives where a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  black market has emerged, a senior World Bank economist has warned. (published 0151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBI MAY HASTEN TIGHTENING IF INFLATION DOESN'T EASE-ADVISER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  may hasten tightening some policy rates if the wholesale price index-based inflation rate doesn't ease in the November-December period, when prices usually fall, a top policy adviser to the government said Wednesday. (published 0754 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN CONSUMER MOOD SOURS AS RATES NUDGE HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian consumer confidence fell in November for the first time since May, the mood soured by rising interest rates and a higher gasoline prices. (published 0048 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DLR UNDER PRESSURE AS EQUITIES RALLY MORE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An improvement in general market sentiment-- helped by strong data from Japan and China--left the dollar under pressure in Europe Wednesday. (published 1035 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: IS THAT IT FOR EUR/USD? \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro is unlikely to make it to a new 2009 high over $1.5060 now. (published 0818 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: S KOREA MINISTER: GOVT WILL MAINTAIN EXPANSIONARY POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean authorities will continue to maintain an expansionary policy for now as it is too soon just yet to implement an exit strategy, the country's Deputy Finance Minister, Shin Je-yoon, said Wednesday. (published 1106 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: USD LOSES DOWNWARD MOMENTUM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: RSI lacks upward momentum; the pair has struck against resistance and should pull back on its new support ahead of a rebound. Suggest long positions above 1.4915 with 1.502 and 1.5065 in sight. The downside penetration of 1.4915 will call for 1.4875 and 1.4825. (published 0728 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: TO BOUNCE OFF RESISTANCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The contracts should strike against their resistances and face new down moves. (published 0738 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: S&P FLAGS TENSIONS WITH NORTH AS KEY RISK FOR S KOREA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's sovereign ratings could be hurt if tensions with North Korea worsen, a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Service analyst said, hours after a naval clash between the two neighbors. (published 0403 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE: HEARD ON THE STREET: JAPAN:LAND OF THE RISING CDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canary in the coal mine? The cost of insuring Japanese government bonds against default has doubled to 0.75 percentage point in the past three months, as markets fret about debt issuance. Credit risk is assuming ever-greater stature in government-bond markets previously blithely assumed to be risk-free. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Eva Szalay, Dow Jones Newswires; 4420-7842-9449; eva.szalay@dowjones.com [ 11-11-09 1207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091111e5bb001bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
