\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Japan Finance Official Noda Wants Flexible Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2009\par 21:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment from Japanese Senior Vice Finance Minister Noda, more background) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 ST. ANDREWS, Scotland (Dow Jones)--Japanese Senior Vice Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Friday called for China to let the yuan trade more freely, aligning himself with the U.S. and Europe in seeking rises in the tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a Group of 20 meeting.\par \par \uc2 Noda's comments come as investors wonder how advanced economies may use the two-day gathering of finance officials from the G-20 industrial and developing countries to press China to accept export-damaging yuan gains to help fix global trade imbalances.\par \par \uc2 "Basically, it's desirable if [the yuan] is flexible," Noda told reporters.\par \par \uc2 One of the two senior vice finance ministers, Noda will attend the G-20 talks on behalf of Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii, who has skipped the event to focus on domestic government efforts to win Parliamentary approval of legislation such as one to support cash-strapped small companies.\par \par \uc2 Although \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't on the official G-20 agenda, Noda said he will stress the need for nations to "avoid engaging in a competition to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " if there are \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debates during the gathering.\par \par \uc2 Appreciation in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar is generally believed to help fix imbalances in the global economy--a key goal of the G-20 leaders-- by reducing trade surpluses in the region while cutting the U.S. trade deficit.\par \par \uc2 But China has kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  virtually pegged to the dollar to defend its export competitiveness, inviting complaints from the U.S. and Europe that the yuan is unfairly undervalued. Other Asian nations from Taiwan to South Korea to the Philippines have been also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to arrest rises in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the greenback. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to use "verbal" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to discourage investors from pushing up the yen too aggressively, but it hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell or buy the yen since spring 2004.\par \par \uc2 Noda's comments were the first indication from the new government led by the Democratic Party of Japan on how it may handle the yuan issue. Its stance appears similar to that of the previous government led by the Liberal Democratic Party, which has urged China to let the yuan appreciate on worry that too tight control over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could eventually hurt China's economy and its major trade partners, including \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Calls from Tokyo for less Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are often interpreted by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders as a sign that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may tolerate rises in the yen to set an example for Beijing. Noda, however, reminded markets that the DPJ government isn't necessarily happy to see the yen strengthen, threatening an export-led economy that has only recently begun recovering from its worst postwar recession.\par \par \uc2 "There's no such fact that we're calling for yen rises," Noda said. "Excessive fluctuations [in exchange rates] are not desirable. Stability is desirable."\par \par \uc2 A Japanese government official has also told Dow Jones Newswires that Tokyo continues to oppose to the idea of relying on changes in exchange rates to achieve the G-20 goal of rebalancing the global economy.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7558; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 06, 2009 15:06 ET (20:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091106e5b6005sc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
