\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Five political risks to watch in Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Sieg   \par \uc2 1,078 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2009\par 08:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 2 (Reuters) - Japan's new government faces a dilemma over how to keep its campaign promises while reining in huge debt, a fragile economy that could slip back into recession, and strains in ties with close security ally the United States.\par \par \uc2 Investors watching Japan must also factor in policy gaps among Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama's Democratic Party and two tiny but vocal coalition partners.\par \par \uc2 Following is a summary of key Japan political risks to watch:\par \par \uc2 * FISCAL DILEMMA\par \par \uc2 The new government is trying to put politicians instead of bureaucrats in charge of drafting budgets in order to reduce waste and reallocate funds from companies to consumers.\par \par \uc2 But cabinet ministers have presented budget requests totalling a record 95 trillion yen for the next fiscal year, starting April 1, 2010, up from about 92 trillion proposed by the defeated Liberal Democratic Party-led government.\par \par \uc2 Who will take the lead in forcing cuts is unclear.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii has said he wants to limit new bond issuance in 2010/11 to below the 44 trillion yen earmarked for this year.\par \par \uc2 But the goal looks in doubt given the size of the budget requests and falling tax revenues in an economy just emerging from a deep recession. One of Fujii's deputies has also said new JGB issuance may have to rise to 50 trillion yen anyway this year to cover the tax shortfall.\par \par \uc2 That uncertainty over issuance is weighing on government debt, helping push the yield on benchmark 10-year bonds to a 2-\u189\'00\'BD month high of 1.425 percent on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Longer term, Japan faces burgeoning welfare costs of the world's fastest-ageing population. Hatoyama has promised to keep Japan's broad sales tax at 5 percent for the next four years.\par \par \uc2 The government says spending more on consumers will boost domestic demand but critics say it lacks a broad growth strategy.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- The budget for 2010/11 is due by the end of this year and the focus is on whether the government can implement key campaign promises without issuing heaps of new debt, or postpone keeping pledges without upsetting voters.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  INDEPENDENCE?\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan began withdrawing from credit markets on Friday but extended a key loan scheme.\par \par \uc2 Reaction from Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had prevented a rift with the government from getting worse, as policymakers grapple with an economy that has just emerged from recession but faces prolonged deflation.\par \par \uc2 What could now prove a bone of contention with the government is the BOJ's economic forecasts. Fujii had called into question the BOJ's economic outlook before Friday's policy board meeting, and after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued its half-yearly economic outlook report, the prime minister suggested it was too rosy.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Persistent deflation could bring the BOJ under pressure to buy more government bonds or revert to quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 -- A big output gap, falls in planned capital spending and the probability of prolonged deflation mean the BOJ will probably keep interest rates ultra-low for several years.\par \par \uc2 * SECURITY TIES\par \par \uc2 Hatoyama has vowed to deepen ties with Asia and steer a diplomatic course more independent of Washington, causing uncertainty about the U.S.-Japan alliance, pivotal in a region home to a rising China and an unpredictable North Korea, whose Rodong missiles can hit most of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- A feud over the repositioning of the U.S. Marines' Futenma air base on Japan's southern island of Okinawa is straining ties ahead of President Barack Obama's Nov. 12-13 visit. Foreign Minister Katsuya Okada will meet U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton to discuss the matter in Washington on Nov. 6.\par \par \uc2 -- North Korean leader Kim Jong-il has said he is ready to resume nuclear talks, but with preconditions. Whether or when negotiations will resume remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 -- Ties with China have improved but bitter memories of Japan's wartime aggression persist, and Tokyo is wary of Beijing's military build-up. Bilateral feuds over marine resources and territorial sovereignty could flare up.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  - HOW STRONG IS TOO STRONG?\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should not be manipulated out of line with economic fundamentals, and his comments favouring a strong yen echo the government's desire to wean Japan from dependence on exports for growth.\par \par \uc2 Still, the new government, which took office on Sept. 16, appears reluctant to see the yen soar too quickly and endanger \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fragile, export-led recovery. Fujii has said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an option if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves too far, too fast.\par \par \uc2 In the past couple of weeks the yen has retreated, making its strength less of an immediate issue. But it remains within range of highs hit against the dollar during the global financial crisis and could quickly return to those levels if the dollar weakens.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Attention will be on comments by government officials regarding possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Picking a level that would trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tricky. The market is unlikely to get too worried about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless it becomes more volatile and nears its all-time high of 79.75 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 * GOVERNANCE HEADACHES\par \par \uc2 Politicians' efforts to exert leadership instead of relying on bureaucrats have sown confusion over where control lies.\par \par \uc2 Added to this, Hatoyama's Democrats must put up with two tiny partners whose backing is needed in parliament's upper house to enact bills smoothly.\par \par \uc2 People's New Party chief Shizuka Kamei, a harsh critic of bare-knuckles capitalism, has been particularly outspoken on economic and financial matters, while gaps on security policies with the pacifist Social Democrats are another Achilles' heel.\par \par \uc2 Political funding scandals also haunt Hatoyama and his predecessor Ichiro Ozawa, now Democratic Party secretary-general.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- The Democrats won two upper house by-elections on Oct. 25, but Hatoyama will probably have to wait to see if his party wins a majority in a mid-2010 upper house poll before dumping the duo.\par \par \uc2 The cabinet's ratings have fallen to around 60 percent, still high by Japan's standards, from about 70 percent earlier.\par \par \uc2 -- Hatoyama will face questions on the scandals in the current parliament session and prosecutors might make new moves.\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091102e5b2000m0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
