\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Down; Improved Risk Appetite In Spot\b0\par\par\uc2 1,241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2009\par 03:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options fall to 12.90%/13.60% vs 13.30%/14.00% in NY overnight as spot rises on improved risk appetite, thanks to better-than-expected U.S. GDP data, says options dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Adds demand for hedges decreasing, as sentiment suggests players now speculating that U.S. economic data later in global day, such as personal spending, may log better than expected results, easing USD downside risk ahead. But vols won't fall much further as players wait for next week's FOMC, payrolls data, which considered more significant events.(TMO)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ bond market in equilibrium with solid underlying support after well bid NZ$300 million government bond tender, says Wellington trader. Adds appears that one bidder took most of NZ$150 million April 2013 bonds on offer which went at 5.16% vs 5.165% before tender. "We're speculating another bank liquidity book wanted to buy some reserve assets but it could have been offshore also." Adds 3.2 bid-to-cover ratio in NZ$75 million on offer for both April 2015, May 2021 maturities still showed good demand. "We've still got a healthy market where there's good demand, we've got a positive yield curve that supports that and Kiwi looks possibly a fraction cheap to offshore investors so that will keep them interested." Adds swap market dead. April 2015 yields unchanged from tender at 5.53%, May 2021 yields unchanged from tender at 6.07%.(SML)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving lower on renewed risk appetite; pair last at 47.52 in moderate volume after settling at 7-week high 47.73 late yesterday. Support pegged at 47.40, resistance 47.80. "We're getting a boost from U.S. GDP," says trader.(SFL)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] Weakness in Australian business credit now chronic. Annette Beacher, senior analyst at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 , says business credit has shrunk more or less in each month since December last year, to be 4.6% lower than a year earlier. Adds these weak annual figures have not been seen by business sector since wake of recession Australia had in early 1990s; it appears banking sector is not supporting smaller businesses via restrictive lending practices. Beacher says policy makers need to take greater notice of weakness in business credit.(JEG)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely higher, boosted by firmer IDR, gains across Asia after stocks in U.S. end higher on strong GDP. Main index likely in 2330-2380 range after ending down 0.5% yesterday at 2344.033. "I think the market is trying to recover from recent sluggish performance," says trader with Lautandhana Securities; expects higher oil prices to also inspire bargain buying in commodity-related stocks. Coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK) may gain after reporting 9-month net profit rose 69%, while rival Bumi (BUMI.JK) may gain on bargain buying. (EDH)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY now at 91.38, slightly above recent week's range around 90.00. Senior dealer at major U.S. bank says "the bias remains downward due to virtually no risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as U.S. low interest rates, but the dollar's downside against the yen looks to be supported. Something's strange here." Adds in past when market develops in similar manner, USD/JPY has suddenly spiked, sometimes by more than Y10; there could be a spike again but doesn't expect any sudden rise to be as steep as Y10. "Over the longer term, the yen may become the weakest due to a slow economic recovery as well as BOJ keeping rates low for the foreseeable future due to deflationary concerns." Tips today's range at 90.50-92.00.(TMO)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil bulls get space to run thanks to U.S. government spending; price breaches psychological $80/bbl level after 3Q GDP data shows 3.5% annualized growth, but Mike Sanders with Sander Capital Advisors points out: "GDP growth this past quarter was positive only because of all the government stimulus programs...at some point the government has to slow down spending and raise interest rates...until then I would expect positive GDP reports and high oil prices." Nymex December crude oil futures up 18 cents at $80.05/bbl.(MAX)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures up 1.4% at 10,020, moving in range of 9980-10,050 helped by gains in Wall Street on strong U.S. GDP data, says Mamoru Nakajo, manager at Phoenix Securities. "There may be end-of-month window dressing buying, so the index may end above 10,000 today," he says. Market analysts say amid peak of Japanese corporate earnings, investors buying carefully today. (AYT)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares tipped to rise, tracking gains on Wall Street, also rising hopes that government will soon approve new financial measures to continue to support an economic recovery. "Players are likely to return after seeing the key index lose 6.9% over the past five days and wiping out all the month-to-date gains," says trader with Agribank Securities. Tips thin trading volume for today because few people want to sell on expectations of higher prices next week. Predicts key index in 594-598 range after ending down 3.1% at 581.49 yesterday.(HAA)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls to 32.507 vs 32.580 yesterday on gains in local shares (Taiex up 0.4%), weaker USD against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in tight 32.470-32.530 band rest of morning. "The pair is unlikely to fall a lot as Taiwan shares haven't rebounded that much from yesterday's sell off." Notes current level still acceptable to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it may buy USD later in session if pair falls further to help exporters meet month-end settlement needs. (CMK)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] November HSI futures +1.8% at 21,641, as cash market expected to rebound on Wall Street cue. Contract ended yesterday at only 1-point discount to cash, making de-facto premium given \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK), Hang Seng Bank's (0011.HK) ex-dividend effect in November, suggesting players already anticipating market rebound after 3-day, 5.9% selloff. November open interest at 78,979 lots.(RLI)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds fall tracking falls in U.S. Treasurys overnight, amid short selling by securities houses, lack of buying interest in reopened NT$30 billion 20-year bonds during when-issued trade, says local trader. Notes players believe "the days of loose monetary policy may come to an end soon", adding to bearish sentiment, as local newspaper reports \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  trying to curb rising real estate prices. Key 10-year last at 1.4235% vs 1.4180% last close, tipped in 1.4100%-1.4400% band rest of session; 5-year yield at 0.9050% vs 0.9032% last close, tipped in 0.9000%-0.9300% band rest of session; 20-year yield at 2.1685% vs 2.0205% last close, tipped in 2.1600%-2.2000% band.(CMK) [ 10-29-09 2217ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091030e5au00023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
