\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Five political risks to watch in Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 986 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2009\par 07:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Japan's new government faces a dilemma over how to keep campaign promises while reining in huge debt, a strong yen that could hurt a fragile recovery and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looking to exit emergency corporate funding support.\par \par \uc2 Investors watching Japan must also factor in policy gaps among Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama's Democratic Party and two tiny but vocal coalition partners.\par \par \uc2 Following is a summary of key Japan political risks to watch:\par \par \uc2 * FISCAL DILEMMA\par \par \uc2 The new government is trying to put politicians instead of bureaucrats in charge of drafting budgets in order to reduce waste and reallocate funds to consumers from companies.\par \par \uc2 But cabinet ministers have presented budget requests totalling a record 95 trillion yen ($1 trillion) for the next fiscal year, starting April 1, 2010, up from about 92 trillion proposed by the defeated Liberal Democratic Party-led government.\par \par \uc2 Who will take the lead in forcing cuts is unclear.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii has said he wants to limit new bond issuance in 2010/11 to below the 44 trillion yen earmarked for this year, but the goal looks in doubt given the size of the budget requests and falling tax revenues in an economy just emerging from a deep recession.\par \par \uc2 That uncertainty over issuance is weighing on government debt, helping push the yield on benchmark 10-year bonds to a one-month high of 1.335 percent on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Longer term, Japan faces burgeoning welfare costs of the world's fastest-ageing population, although Hatoyama has promised to keep Japan's broad sales tax at 5 percent for the next four years.\par \par \uc2 The government says spending more on consumers will boost domestic demand but critics say it lacks a broad growth strategy.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- The budget for 2010/11 is due by the end of this year and the focus is on whether the government can implement key campaign promises without issuing heaps of additional debt.\par \par \uc2 * EXIT STRATEGY DELAY?\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan is under pressure from outspoken cabinet ministers who worry that a quick exit from emergency steps to support corporate funding could hurt the economy, and especially smaller firms -- pushing up unemployment.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ's current plans are unlikely to worry investors as corporate finance conditions have eased since the financial crisis, but analysts want to see how the new government handles its relationship with the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Past government jawboning has been known to backfire by prompting the BOJ to act just to demonstrate its independence.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Analysts expect the BOJ to unwind some emergency steps by the end of the year. BOJ policy board meetings are set for Oct. 30, Nov. 19-20 and Dec. 17-18.\par \par \uc2 -- A big output gap, falls in planned capital spending and the probability of prolonged deflation mean the BOJ will probably keep interest rates ultra-low for several years.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  - HOW STRONG IS TOO STRONG?\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should not be manipulated out of line with economic fundamentals, and his comments favouring a strong yen echo the government's desire to wean Japan from dependence on exports for growth.\par \par \uc2 Still, the new government, which took office on Sept. 16, appears reluctant to see the yen soar too quickly and endanger \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fragile, export-led recovery. Fujii has said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an option if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves too far, too fast.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Attention will be on comments by Fujii and other government officials regarding possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -- Picking a level that would trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tricky as the dollar is weakening broadly and there is a global push to rebalance the world economy. The market is unlikely to get seriously worried about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless it becomes more volatile and approaches its all-time high of 79.75 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 * GOVERNANCE HEADACHES\par \par \uc2 Politicians' efforts to exert leadership instead of relying on bureaucrats have sown confusion over where control lies.\par \par \uc2 Added to this, Hatoyama's Democrats must put up with two tiny partners whose backing is needed in parliament's upper house to enact bills smoothly.\par \par \uc2 People's New Party chief Shizuka Kamei, a harsh critic of bare-knuckles capitalism, has been particularly outspoken on economic and financial matters, while gaps on security policies with the pacifist Social Democrats are another Achilles' heel.\par \par \uc2 Political funding scandals also haunt Hatoyama and his predecessor Ichiro Ozawa, now Democratic Party secretary-general.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Two upper house by-elections will be held on Oct. 25, but Hatoyama will probably have to wait to see if his party wins a majority in a mid-2010 upper house poll before dumping the duo.\par \par \uc2 -- Hatoyama will face questions on the scandals in parliament due to resume on Oct. 26, and prosecutors might make fresh moves.\par \par \uc2 * SECURITY TIES\par \par \uc2 Hatoyama has vowed to deepen ties with Asia and steer a diplomatic course more independent of Washington, causing uncertainty about the U.S.-Japan alliance, pivotal in a region home to a rising China and an unpredictable North Korea, whose Rodong missiles can hit most of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Key issues to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- U.S. Defense Secretary Robert Gates' visit to Japan from Tuesday and President Barack Obama's Nov. 12-13 visit should provide clues to how the two governments will manage ties.\par \par \uc2 -- North Korean leader Kim Jong-il has said he is ready to resume nuclear talks, but with preconditions. Whether or when negotiations will resume remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 -- Ties with China have improved but bitter memories of Japan's wartime aggression persist, and Tokyo is wary of Beijing's military build-up. Bilateral feuds over marine resources and territorial sovereignty could flare up. ($1=90.87 Yen) (Editing by Rodney Joyce)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-POLITICS/RISKS (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA|ABX|BNX|FUN \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091019e5aj0008z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
