\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Japan may grit teeth and tolerate a stronger yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,059 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2009\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the yen, click on [ID:nYEN])\par \par \uc2 * Japan seen as respecting need to correct global imbalances\par \par \uc2 * New government favours consumers over big corporations\par \par \uc2 * Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk higher near record peak, speed key (Repeats story originally issued late on Friday)\par \par \uc2 By Charlotte Cooper and Rika Otsuka\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 9 (Reuters) - Even after Japan's finance minister reminded market players that the country may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, a number of factors are indicating Tokyo will grit its teeth and tolerate a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 First and foremost, the global crisis has made it harder for a big exporting nation such as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally when policymakers worldwide want big trade imbalances reduced -- which means Japan shifting away from its reliance on exports and doing more to spur consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 Ever since the Group of Seven rich nations put more pressure on China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, as was repeated at last week's meeting, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has found it more difficult to launch unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at relieving its big exporters.\par \par \uc2 In past weeks the bigger G20 forum has pledged to pursue policies that promote more balanced growth. [ID:nN25480100] [ID:nFCC000036]\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would find it hard to justify market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as correction of the global imbalance has been a big issue," said Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2 Japan has other reasons to accept a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 : it brings greater import purchasing power for consumers and corporations, especially if the energy-dependent country is hit by another surge in oil prices as seen last year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also believe Japan does not want the headache of amassing an even bigger pile of dollar reserves, which have climbed above $1 trillion to be the world's second-largest after China's.\par \par \uc2 Adrian Foster, head of financial markets research for Asia-Pacific at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  in Hong Kong, says Japan is prepared to tolerate a stronger yen as part of any global correction.\par \par \uc2 "I think Japan is accepting that they have a role to play in this and they're playing it."\par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> is hovering near a 13-year peak against the dollar of 87.10 set in January during the global financial crisis and not far from the all-time high of 79.75 yen struck in 1995.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the market is unlikely to get seriously worried about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless it becomes more volatile and approaches the 79.75 level -- the highest since it was allowed to trade freely in the early 1970s.\par \par \uc2 Identifying a trigger point is tricky because it also depends on the speed of appreciation. So far the yen climb has been steady, and measures of market volatility remain subdued.\par \par \uc2 "The government is likely to tolerate a rise in the yen as long as it is gradual," said Yasutoshi Nagai, chief economist at Daiwa Securities SMBC.\par \par \uc2 The new Democratic Party-led government wants to shift Japan to a stronger domestic economy and away from reliance on exports.\par \par \uc2 The old administration was long perceived to favour a weaker yen to promote exports crucial for its economy, although it had stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March 2004.\par \par \uc2 New Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii has said not promoting a weak exchange rate is the right policy. He also says the current yen rise stems more from dollar weakness even while warning that Japan could act if the moves become "abnormal". [ID:nT302844]\par \par \uc2 ON THE BRIGHT SIDE\par \par \uc2 Japan depends heavily on imported food, energy and raw materials. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would boost the purchasing power of companies and consumers, even if it worsens deflation in the near-term by cutting the cost of imported goods. [ID:nT338478]\par \par \uc2 A more domestic demand-led economy would make Japanese shares less sensitive to the impact of yen swings on exporters, and make Japanese assets more attractive to investors, said Daisuke Uno, chief strategist at \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So far the yen's rise is mainly against the dollar, which hit a 14-month low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <.DXY> on Thursday, and it has not fared as well against some other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen's real effective exchange rate <JPYEEXR=J> -- a trade-weighted index adjusted for consumer price changes -- was at 118.5 in early October, a slight rise in the past few months but 8 percent below this year's peak hit in March and 28 percent below the 1995 all-time high, according to Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 This weakens a case for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially as economic recovery around the world is still fragile.\par \par \uc2 "At heart, every country wants to weaken its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Tsutomu Soma, senior manager of foreign securities at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 . "At the same time, leaders around the world realise it is most important for all of us to support the U.S. economy."\par \par \uc2 Analysts agree authorities could step in if a yen rise gets out of hand. But so far the move has been a gradual one.\par \par \uc2 Dollar/yen one-month implied volatility stands near 14 percent <JPY1MO=>, below this year's highs above 20 percent and indicating options markets do not expect sharp daily swings.\par \par \uc2 Blocking and turning a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trend is hard, especially if you try to do it solo. In Tokyo alone, daily dollar/yen turnover has risen 25 percent since 2006 to $158.1 billion.\par \par \uc2 Tohru Sasaki, who formerly oversaw \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is now \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Japan, says the country doesn't want to add to reserves because the scale of funding the current amount is enormous.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells short-term bills, which fund the holdings of U.S. Treasuries and other assets, totalling 102.9 trillion yen ($1.2 trillion) -- about 18 percent of the economy's size and creating big interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, Sasaki said.\par \par \uc2 "Japan is not willing to increase \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves any more," he said.\par \par \uc2 For a graphic on the yen's real effective exchange rate and the yen/dollar rate, please click on: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/kaz82f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/kaz82f}}}\uc2  (Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/YEN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|J   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091011e5ab000gr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
