\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2009\par 15:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par OBAMA WINS NOBEL PEACE PRIZE IN SURPRISE PICK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Barack Obama wins the Nobel Peace Prize for "his extraordinary efforts to strengthen international diplomacy and cooperation between peoples," the Nobel Committee says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US AUGUST TRADE DEFICIT NARROWS TO $30.71B \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. trade deficit unexpectedly narrows for the first time in four months in August, decreasing 3.6% to $30.71 billion, with exports rising to their highest level of the year and imports easing despite higher oil prices. Economists had expected the gap to widen to $33.6 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CREDITOR REJECTS FORMER \f28 \uc2 \f2 TXU\f28 \uc2 \f2  DEBT PLAN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Franklin Templeton rejects the plan to restructure the debt of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Energy Future Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2 , The New York Post reports. The newly issued debt would only have a face value of half of the current loan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED READY TO TIGHTEN WHEN ECONOMY IMPROVES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be ready to tighten monetary policy and shrink its bloated balance sheet to prevent inflation once the economy improves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STANDS BY AS DOLLAR FALLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to a 14-month low against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday, intensifying a trend that the Obama administration has publicly suggested it opposes -- but which it appears prepared to tolerate quietly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IN TODAY'S LBO ARENA, A LOT LESS 'L' \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two new deals show private equity is awakening after a miserable two-year period. But they also reveal how hard it is to complete a transaction: Far more banks are needed to stitch together far less leverage for transactions that are taking far longer to get done than during the boom.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CANADA JOBLESS RATE UNEXPECTEDLY FALLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada's unemployment rate falls in September for the first time since the downturn began, to 8.4% from 8.7% in August, and the country adds 30,600 net jobs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par REVIEW OF CITI DRAWS WARY FDIC RESPONSE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Deposit Insurance Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . questions the generally positive conclusions in a government-mandated review of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 's top management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES LITTLE CHANGED AFTER DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures remain lower after trade deficit data. Earlier, U.S. shares pointed to a weaker start as Fed Chairman Bernanke said the unprecedented loose monetary policy will have to come to an end once the economic recovery begins to take hold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HATOYAMA TO PROMOTE IDEA OF ASIA BLOC AT SUMMIT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When the leaders of Japan, China and South Korea meet for a trilateral summit in Beijing on Saturday, Japanese Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama will try to persuade his counterparts to share his vision that Asia needs its own economic bloc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Mark to Market \par BERNANKE STICKS TO DELPHIC PRONOUNCEMENTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's remarks about policy were confined to generalities and sentiments already expressed repeatedly by FOMC members, Jim Murphy writes, and in truth the Fed is no closer to raising rates than it was before his speech.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Money Talks \par HOW I LEARNED TO STOP WORRYING AND LOVE THE $ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Everyone hates the dollar -- which means it'll probably go up. It's easy to see why investors might be a bit nervous about the dollar's outlook, writes Alen Mattich.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Focus \par YEN'S ADVANCE IS FAR FROM OVER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's relentless rise will continue. Despite a new administration in Tokyo, despite renewed threats of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and despite fears about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's growth prospects, there is little reason to expect a reverse, writes Nick Hastings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Extra Credit \par HOW TOP HY FUNDS EXCELLED IN A VOLATILE YEAR \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The last 12 months posed some unique challenges for junk-bond portfolio managers, Andrew Edwards writes, and the managers that performed the best were light on their feet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par TREASURYS RECOVERING; LONG END REBOUNDS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys continue to recover, long end rebounds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES REMAIN LOWER FOLLOWING TRADE DEFICIT DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US stock futures remain lower after trade deficit data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DOLLAR GAINS SLIGHTLY ON US TRADE DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. dollar gets slight bump up after US trade gap narrows unexpectedly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NY GOLD SEEN DOWN $6, SILVER DOWN 15 CENTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold is lower as dollar eases. [ 09-10-09 1303GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091009e5a90010g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
