\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  won't  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  yet, but could change-Nomura\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven C. Johnson \par \uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2009\par 22:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels to weaken the yen, but could act if the economy takes a turn for the worse and deflation fears rise, Nomura Securities' global chief economist said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Since Japan's economy could slow again in the fourth quarter and continue to struggle in 2010, authorities may feel pressured next year to launch new "proactive stimulus measures," Nomura's Paul Sheard said at a meeting with reporters.\par \par \uc2 If the dollar, which hit an 8-1/2-month low Wednesday near 88 yen <JPY=>, were to fall below 85 yen, he said these measures might include \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly through quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 According to a Reuters poll, analysts see just a 23 percent chance that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they expect weak Japanese economic fundamentals to arrest the yen's sharp recent gains. For more, see [ID:nL6321054]\par \par \uc2 But Sheard said the dollar is also likely to remain on a weakening path as the Federal Reserve keeps interest rates near zero until late 2010 or early 2011.\par \par \uc2 Additional yen strength, he said, would not be helpful for Japan, which is dependent on exports and is likely to struggle as existing stimulus measures fade and firms slow inventory rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 Nomura expects Japan's economy to shrink 5.6 percent this year and grow just 0.8 percent in 2010.\par \par \uc2 If authorities were to act, Sheard said they might not simply buy dollars but rather amp up a quantitative easing program by having the Bank of Japan buy government debt from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The money pumped into the system "would probably do the trick," and push the yen down against the dollar, Sheard said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2004. Before that, governments led by the Liberal Democratic Party \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to cap yen gains.\par \par \uc2 The new Democratic Party of Japan-led government, however, initially suggested it favored a more hands-off approach.\par \par \uc2 But Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii has since backpedaled a bit, saying on Wednesday he was quietly watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves though he added that yen gains were more a function of U.S. dollar weakness rather than yen strength. [ID:nT302844]\par \par \uc2 Sheard said that the new government, like LDP-led ones, would prefer not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to achieve maintain a given dollar-yen level but remains wary of quick, violent yen appreciation not justified by fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 "At the end of the day, I still think there won't be a fundamental policy shift," Sheard said. "But conditions in Japan are very challenging."\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|DNP \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091007e5a7001f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
