\par\par \b \uc2 Japan economy not ready for exit strategies-finmin\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara \par \uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2009\par 19:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Japan needs to discuss exit strategies for its expansionary fiscal and monetary policies but implementing them now would be premature given the fragile state of its economy, Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii said.\par \par \uc2 Fujii also told the International Monetary Fund's steering committee on Sunday that Tokyo hopes to expand support for the IMF's effort in helping low-income countries struggling from the aftermath of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 A finance ministry official later told reporters Japan was ready to extend $2.7 billion in loans to the IMF for this purpose.\par \par \uc2 While Japan's economy is showing signs of a pick-up, job losses are rising and exports continue to fall from year-before levels, warranting continued fiscal and monetary support, Fujii said in a statement to the IMF's policy-steering committee.\par \par \uc2 "While it is necessary to discuss exit strategies, at this moment, their implementation is still premature," he said.\par \par \uc2 Central banks around the world have begun debating how and when to phase out their emergency steps to contain the damage wrought by the worst global financial crisis in decades, but most are not expected to do so until well into next year.\par \par \uc2 While the Bank of Japan is seen keeping interest rates near zero at least until 2011, it is considering phasing out its emergency steps aimed at supporting corporate finance when they expire in December.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa signaled an exit to the unconventional steps may be near, telling reporters on Saturday that corporate finance was in less need of policy support with credit market conditions improving significantly.\par \par \uc2 Fujii's remarks, however, show the government is not letting its guard down against downside risks to the economy, with one being the yen's recent rises against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 At a joint news conference with Shirakawa on Saturday, Fujii repeated his warning to markets that Tokyo was ready to take action if one-sided \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves become excessive.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen hurts Japanese exporters by making their products less competitive overseas.\par \par \uc2 Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the Group of Seven -- the United States, Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Italy and Canada -- gathered in Istanbul on Saturday on the sidelines of the semiannual meetings of the World Bank and IMF.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2004 and many market players doubt the new government, led by the Democratic Party which won elections in August, will depart from the old administration's stance. [ID:nT249408]\par \par \uc2 IMF/JAPAN|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|ABX|J|DNP \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091004e5a4000hh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
