\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Japan's Fujii Repeats To Act If Yen Rise Becomes Extreme\b0\par\par\uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2009\par 22:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more comments from Japan's finance minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, and adds details throughout.)\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL -(Dow Jones)- Japan's finance minister warned again Saturday that he will act properly if the yen moves too one-sidedly, in yet another attempt to dispel the market speculation that he is unlikely to ever try to arrest the yen's current bull run.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's governor, meanwhile, signaled that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its interest rate anchored at 0.1% until the nation's economic recovery firmly takes hold. But he voiced confidence in the improvement of Japan's corporate finance market, possibly adding to speculation that the bank may let some of its emergency measures - namely the buying of corporate bonds and commercial paper - expire in December as planned.\par \par \uc2 Their comments came after finance chiefs of the Group of Seven industrialized nations issued a statement that warns against too sharp swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates while promising to keep in place stimulus measures until the world's economy gets out of the woods.\par \par \uc2 "As I have said in Tokyo, we will take appropriate steps if one-sided movements become excessive," Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii said during a post-G-7 joint conference with BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa. "It's desirable for exchange rates to be stable, which is all positive for every nation in the world. At the same time, it's best if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates move in line with the economic strength of individual countries."\par \par \uc2 Since Monday, Fujii has been working to clarify his \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy after a series of his initial remarks decrying the idea of artificially weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster exports encouraged speculators to push the yen higher. Traders took his words to mean that the new Democratic Party of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets even though the yen's strength is threatening to derail a nascent recovery in the nation's export-led economy.\par \par \uc2 The markets pushed the dollar to an eight-month low of Y88.23 on Monday, although the greenback has since recovered to around Y89.80. A strong yen makes Japan's exports more expensive abroad.\par \par \uc2 Fujii is displeased with the market perception that he is little disturbed by the surging Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said he had told his G-7 counterparts that his opposition to completive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation has been "misinterpreted."\par \par \uc2 Yet Fujii declined to say whether he sees the yen's current trend as one-sided, although he had called it "a bit one-sided" on Monday. "Factors that are driving the yen higher recently are very complex," he added at the press conference.\par \par \uc2 Fujii acknowledged how fragile Japan's current recovery is, saying the economy remains in a "serious state" with its exports still falling. Now is "not the time yet to debate how to exit" from expansionary policies introduced to battle the global and domestic economic crisis, he said.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Shirakawa said he had told other G-7 officials that the BOJ will "appropriately manage monetary policy" in line with economic policies agreed upon by the Group of 20 nations. "Specifically, we are going to patiently maintain accommodative monetary conditions to support the economic recovery through our interest rate policy," he said.\par \par \uc2 But "conditions in the commercial paper and corporate bond markets have greatly improved ... and support from the policy side is playing a less important role" now, he said. "Fund-raising conditions for companies are clearly improving, and there's a significant decrease in the risks of financial (problems) pushing down the real economy."\par \par \uc2 During the G-7 "I explained that we will appropriately deal with our emergency steps of buying commercial paper and corporate securities in accordance with the levels of improvements in the financial markets and corporate financing," Shirakawa said. "I believe that on this matter, I have gained understanding of other member states."\par \par \uc2 Fujii refrained from judging Shirakawa's views about the measures to support the corporate finance markets. But possibly in an indication the finance minister isn't too comfortable with Shirakawa's stance, Fujii said that "there's no way you can shift policies based on the assessment that the (Japanese) economy has fully recovered."\par \par \uc2 Fujii added that he made no commitment to other G-7 nations that Japan will compile a fresh additional spending plan to prop up its economy.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7558; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 03, 2009 16:52 ET (20:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091003e5a30008y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
