\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan has no plans to put yen on G7 agenda\b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2009\par 10:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click)\par \par \uc2 * Japan will tell G7 focusing on boosting domestic demand\par \par \uc2 * Finance minister says G7 still has key role to play (Adds details, background)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Japan has no plan to put the yen's recent rises on the agenda when Group of Seven finance leaders meet on Saturday, its finance minister said, a sign it does not see the rally as volatile enough to warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now.\par \par \uc2 The yen's surge to an eight-month high against the greenback on Monday has prompted Hirohisa Fujii, just two weeks in the job, to backtrack on earlier comments that he wanted to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that a strong yen was good for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since pulled back from that high as Fujii softened his rhetoric in favour of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and raised the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under extreme circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would bring up the issue at the gathering in Istanbul, Fujii told reporters on Wednesday: "No, I don't have such a plan."\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell after Fujii's remarks to an intraday low around 89.40 yen, according to Reuters data. That was still above the eight-month low of 88.23 yen hit on Monday.\par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors will meet on Saturday on the sidelines of World Bank and International Monetary Fund meetings in Istanbul to discuss recent financial market developments and signs of recovery in the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Fujii said it would be important to explain to other G7 countries how Japan's new Democratic Party-led government is trying to turn the country into a domestic demand-driven economy by increasing household incomes.\par \par \uc2 Japan should also stress it wants to cooperate with other countries to ensure a global economic recovery, he said.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been steadily rising this month as Fujii, chosen to be finance minister after voters swept the Democrats to power at the end of August, repeatedly said he didn't see the need to weaken the yen for the sake of the country's exporters and that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has benefits.\par \par \uc2 But he back-pedalled this week, saying that he never explicitly said he wanted a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that recent moves were sudden and one-sided.\par \par \uc2 Despite the flip-flop, traders and analysts see little change to Tokyo's stance of focusing more on the speed of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, rather than levels, when determining whether to step into the market.\par \par \uc2 Markets have been speculating that the new Democratic Party government's promise to focus its policies more on the domestic economy will make it less inclined to help exporters by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  agains the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily earlier in the decade to stop a rising yen from harming its all-important exports.\par \par \uc2 But they haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, the end of a 15-month long spree in which they sold 35 trillion yen ($391 billion) to shield a struggling economy, raising suspicions \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has abandoned its old \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  ways.\par \par \uc2 The G7 has repeatedly said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves should reflect economic fundamentals and that excessive exchange-rate moves are undesirable.\par \par \uc2 Fujii said that despite growing views that the G20 would supplant the G7 as the key venue for global debate, he believed the G7, comprised of rich economies, had an important role to play.\par \par \uc2 "The role of the G7 meeting is important even if there is the G20 gathering, especially because G7 countries have a common type of financial markets," he said.\par \par \uc2 The G20, which includes the world's richest nations and fast-growing emerging economies including China, India and Brazil, has become the primary venue for world leaders to meet on the financial crisis after its gathering in Pittsburgh, the third G20 summit in less than a year. (Additional reporting by Stanley White; Editing by Chris Gallagher)\par \par \uc2 G7/JAPAN (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090930e59u000lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
