\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 4- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  finmin hints at  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to limit yen damage\b0\par\par\uc2 967 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2009\par 02:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Fujii scales back rhetoric in support of strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Comments spark brief slide in yen against dollar\par \par \uc2 * Repeats never said Tokyo would accept excessive yen rises\par \par \uc2 * Annual core CPI fall hits record, deflation deepens (Adds comments from Gyohten)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Stanley White\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister raised the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time on Tuesday in a scramble to limit the damage from a yen rally fuelled by earlier comments that he favoured a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen hit an eight-month high of 88.22 to the dollar on Monday after Fujii, who has been on the job for two weeks, repeatedly said that he wanted to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that a strong yen would eventually support domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 It has since pulled back to around 90.00, giving up all of the previous day's gains, as Fujii backpedalled on his remarks.\par \par \uc2 Domestic demand is collapsing in the world's second-largest economy, pushing the country into its deepest deflation on record in August. Governments fighting deflation typically devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Perhaps Fujii didn't realise how much the market would move on his comments after he became finance minister. But now he realises that this isn't such a good thing," said Tokichi Ito, deputy general manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  team at Trust & Custody Services Bank in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "I doubt Fujii has abandoned his view that a strong yen is good for domestic demand, and this view is shared by the government. But the yen buying in reaction to the government's stance has likely run its course."\par \par \uc2 A strong yen puts pressure on import prices in a country where deflation is already at record levels, although policymakers and businesses tend to worry more about the impact it has on exports, the key driver of the country's growth.\par \par \uc2 Fujii reiterated on Tuesday that global competition to devalue \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was wrong, but that this didn't mean Tokyo would leave excessive yen rises unattended.\par \par \uc2 "If moves are irregular, there is a possibility we might take whatever action deemed necessary for the sake of the country," Fujii told a news conference after a cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 He said that his message had been consistent but that it was being misinterpreted by markets.\par \par \uc2 "I never said a word about leaving a strong yen as it is. I'm saying it's wrong, as history shows, for countries to continuously take policies aimed at lowering the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Fujii said.\par \par \uc2 Toyoo Gyohten, an adviser to Fujii on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters, agreed with the finance minister's comments that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a possibility. Gyohten also said that the yen's recent rise wasn't sharp and that it is stabilising now.\par \par \uc2 Gyohten was director general of the MOF's international bureau at the time of the Plaza Accord in 1985, when rich countries agreed to let the dollar depreciate to reduce the United States' trade deficit. He then served as Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official until he retired from the MOF in 1989.\par \par \uc2 This month, the yen has neared January's 13-year high of 87.10 per dollar as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen to its lowest in a year against the likes of the euro and Australian dollar.\par \par \uc2 While the dollar has been pressured in part by ultra-low interest rates and lost about 3 percent on the yen in September, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only gained about 1 percent on the euro and none at all against the Australian dollar as investors have resumed buying of higher-yielding assets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Persistent strength in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has prompted the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to warn about the possible effects a strong Canadian dollar could have on returning inflation to its target range.\par \par \uc2 DOUBTS ON CREDIBILITY?\par \par \uc2 Analysts said market forces had led Fujii to beat a retreat from his previous comments.\par \par \uc2 "Fujii appears to have been jolted by the yen's rapid appreciation and is worried that the strong yen would throw cold water on the economic recovery led by exports," said Takumi Tsunoda, an economist at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 "But it is unlikely the government actually will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market."\par \par \uc2 The yen has strengthened steadily from about 93.00 to the dollar at the start of September as Fujii said he did not see a need to weaken the Japanese unit for the sake of the country's exporters and that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also had benefits.\par \par \uc2 It hit 88.22 to the dollar on Monday according to Reuters data, the highest since January, before easing towards 90.00.\par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed back above 90.00 yen briefly on Tuesday, reflecting Fujii's repeated warnings that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were getting too one-sided towards yen rises.\par \par \uc2 "Until yesterday, he seemed to cheer the strong yen, but now he can't stand it," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, chief investment strategist at SJS Markets Ltd in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "It puts a question mark on the stability of his policies and how well they are thought out for the long term. It might be better to support exports in the short term and allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen later once domestic demand picks up more."\par \par \uc2 In the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen to help its exporters stay competitive.\par \par \uc2 But Tokyo has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004, after a 15-month-long, 35 trillion yen ($390.4 billion) selling spree aimed at preventing the yen's strength from snuffing out an economic recovery. (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano, Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Hugh Lawson & Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 4)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090929e59t0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
