\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-What will spark Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength\b0\par\par\uc2 By Satomi Noguchi and Charlotte Cooper    \par \uc2 859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2009\par 13:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - There is little chance that the new Japanese government will reverse a hands-off stance and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise... for now.\par \par \uc2 The rally in the yen towards a 13-year high set in January would need to become more volatile and damaging to stocks to make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more likely.\par \par \uc2 The yen hit 88.23 per dollar on trading platform EBS on Monday, an eight-month peak and not far from the 13-year peak of 87.10. The dollar later came back to 89.40 yen but has still shed 4 percent this month.\par \par \uc2 The yen also surged to a five-month high against sterling and two-month peak versus the euro.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily earlier in the decade to stop a rising yen from harming its all-important exports.\par \par \uc2 But they haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, the end of a 15-month long spree in which they sold 35 trillion yen ($383 billion) to shield a struggling economy, raising suspicions \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has abandoned its old \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  ways.\par \par \uc2 WOULD THEY \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2  NOW?\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts say this is unlikely and the market won't have to seriously consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the dollar falls below 87.10 yen or even 85 yen -- the lowest since it hit a record 79.75 in 1995.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has changed. The yen surged 27 percent against the dollar from an August low in 2008 to its peak in January, when exports were collapsing because of the global crisis, but authorities did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even a green light to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Group of Seven industrial powers in October 2008 did not tempt Tokyo's hand.\par \par \uc2 At that time, the G7 issued a rare statement between meetings singling out yen volatility, which was interpreted as giving Japan approval to stem the yen's surge if it wanted to.\par \par \uc2 The yen's real effective exchange rate stood at 114.8 at the end of August, below the eight-year high of 128.7 reached in January.\par \par \uc2 WHAT DO THE OFFICIALS SAY?\par \par \uc2 Japan's new Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii said on Monday recent \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were not "abnormal" and it would be a mistake to artificially influence exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 He later toned down those comments, saying moves were becoming one-sided in favour of a yen rise and warned he had never said he would leave a yen rise "as it is".\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the month, Fujii said a strong yen was generally good because it boosted the purchasing power of Japanese. But he later backed away from the comments, saying the G20 had said in April that competing to drive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower was not good for the world economy.\par \par \uc2 DOES THE CHANGE IN GOVERNMENT MAKE A DIFFERENCE?\par \par \uc2 Ahead of its election win last month, Japan's new ruling party was critical of the previous administration for pandering to big business by trying to weaken the yen and said Japan should change its economic model of relying on exports.\par \par \uc2 In reality though, many in the market expect the new government to stick to the previous administration's approach, which is to follow G7's stance.\par \par \uc2 The G7 says excess volatility and disorderly moves have adverse implications for stability and it monitors markets closely and cooperates as appropriate.\par \par \uc2 Traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do anything more than smooth volatility would in any case be difficult for any major economy these days, as trying to weaken a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be seen as protectionist, something authorities want to avoid.\par \par \uc2 So far the yen's rise against the dollar has not been a volatile one. While daily swings have picked up in the last week, actual day-to-day volatility on a one-month basis has risen from the lowest level since July 2007 -- the onset of the financial crisis. One-month implied vol ticked up to 14.4 percent, only a six-week high.\par \par \uc2 SO IS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  OUT OF THE QUESTION?\par \par \uc2 A drop in the dollar is not necessarily enough to spark \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be likely to watch the yen's exchange rate against other trading partners as well and keep its eye on the Nikkei share average as a barometer of the impact of a stronger yen on Japan Inc.\par \par \uc2 Many major exporters have planned for a dollar rate of 90-95 yen for the business year ending in March, so current levels are seen testing their pain threshold. The Nikkei fell 2.5 percent on Monday, in part in a reaction to the stronger yen.\par \par \uc2 "What market participants would need to watch is whether comments from authorities change when the pair falls below 85 yen, and if the Nikkei falls as much as 1,000 points from the current level," said Mitsuru Sahara, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives trading at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. (Additional reporting by Rika Otsuka; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Q&A)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091001e59s000v4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
