\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF Gyohten: No Need To Rule Out  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2009\par 13:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The incoming \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister said Thursday that domestic authorities should retain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a policy option to counter rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements that could destabilize economies.\par \par \uc2 Toyoo Gyohten, a 78-year-old former top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, also said that while market confidence in the dollar is waning, the U.S. unit will remain the world's most widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for trade for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 Japan should also work with other major economies to help the U.S. reduce its current-account deficit to shore up investor confidence in the dollar, he told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Gyohten's comments follow an announcement last week by new Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii appointing him as special adviser on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues.\par \par \uc2 It remains to be seen how much Fujii will rely on Gyohten in setting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. But Fujii's respect for Gyohten - touting him as the most trusted Japanese person in the arena of international finance policy - suggests the former \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat could have considerable influence in policy-making.\par \par \uc2 Gyohten's appointment also signals that despite years of absence from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Tokyo is unlikely to take \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  off the table.\par \par \uc2 "For businesspeople, the most important concern is that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are stable from a long-term perspective, giving them predictability on market trends," said Gyohten, who served as vice finance minister for international affairs - a top post for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrats - from 1986 to 1989.\par \par \uc2 "The problem is that there are short-term factors such as speculation, talks over political or military matters, or even an earthquake that could affect exchange rates," he said.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  move on various factors over the short term, and that can't be helped. But it isn't desirable if short-term fluctuations deprive (longer-term) stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or predictability."\par \par \uc2 "There is no need at all" to rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. "In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, you should take the best countermeasure at a given time against such situations while watching actual market movements."\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a seven-month low of Y90.12 on Sept. 16, partly on views that Fujii, who recently spoke out against market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and signaled his preference for a strong yen, isn't too worried about a rising domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gains in the yen can hurt Japan's economy by making its products more expensive abroad, hindering the country's recovery from its worst recession since World War II.\par \par \uc2 Gyohten's position, however, doesn't differ much from that of Fujii, who has also said that authorities may need to mull counteraction if there are abnormal movements in exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 A popular view in market is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , under the guidance of the two, will likely stay the course on the nation's recent policy of refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 The MOF has stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March 2004, when it ended a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign that sold an unprecedented Y35 trillion to fend off a rise in the yen.\par \par \uc2 Gyohten, now president of the Tokyo-based Institute for International Monetary Affairs, has yet to formally take office as adviser. Once Fujii returns from Pittsburgh where the Group of 20 leaders are attending a summit on Thursday and Friday, he and MOF officials will work on the personnel decision, according to the ministry.\par \par \uc2 Dollar To Keep Its Status\par \par \uc2 Commenting on the dollar, Gyohten said he sees market confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  waning due to the U.S.' foreign indebtedness. But he said that the U.S. unit will remain the world's most widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for trade and government reserves for at least 30 years because of a lack of a replacement unit as well as the U.S.' economic, financial and military might.\par \par \uc2 For now, Japan should cooperate with the U.S. and other key nations such as China and Germany to maintain investors' confidence in the dollar, such as by helping Washington reduce its current account deficit, Gyohten said.\par \par \uc2 Major countries probably have no choice but to cooperate with one another to make the dollar a trustworthy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Gyohten said.\par \par \uc2 Gyohten said he has yet to hear from Fujii about his exact tasks as adviser, but added that he has no intention to replace Rintaro Tamaki, current vice finance minister for international affairs, in international conferences such as Group of Seven gatherings.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7558; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 24-09-09 1113GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090924e59o000ie\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
