\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,045 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2009\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE AUG ANNUAL CPI -0.2% AS EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in the 16 countries that use the euro fell 0.2% in August from the year-earlier period and were unrevised from the preliminary estimate, the European Union's statistics arm Eurostat reported Wednesday. (published 0900 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EU ALMUNIA: UNSURE EU ECON STRONG ENOUGH TO WITHDRAW STIMULUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union economy likely still needs help from central banks and government stimulus spending, European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Joaquin Almunia said Wednesday. (published 0942 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OECD:JOB CRISIS NOT OVER YET,UNEMPLOYMENT RISE IN '10 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development warned Wednesday that the unemployment crisis isn't over yet, despite an improvement in the economic outlook. (published 1021 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UK JOBLESS HITS HIGHEST LEVEL FOR 14 YEARS IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. unemployment jumped to its highest level for 14 years in the three months to July, while the number claiming benefits topped expectations, reducing hopes a speedy economic recovery, official data showed Wednesday. (published 0920 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM HATOYAMA: JAPAN ECON OUTLOOK STILL UNCLEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's new prime minister said Wednesday that the outlook for the Japanese economy remains murky, and the government will take steps to stimulate household spending while reexamining the previous administration's spending priorities. (published 1008 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN RETAIL SECTOR EXPECTS WORST IS STILL TO COME - SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most of the German retail sector thinks the worst of the recession still lies ahead, according to a survey published Wednesday. (published 1037 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE SENTANCE: UK ECON MAY HAVE PASSED TURNING POINT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. economy may already have passed the turning point in the downturn, and improvements in confidence suggest a more sustained recovery over the coming year, Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee member Andrew Sentance says. (published 1018 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 'S INCOMING FIN MIN: AGAINST \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Incoming Japanese Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii said that the recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market "is not fluctuating wildly" and therefore does not call for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Nikkei and other media reported Wednesday. (published 0649 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE HALDANE:BKS TO TAKE SEVERAL YRS TO REBUILD BALANCE SHEETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banks are likely to take several years to rebuild their balance sheets, according to Andrew Haldane, the Bank of England's executive director for financial stability. (published 0707 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RIKSBANK'S SVENSSON: ECONOMY OVERHEATING QUITE IMPROBABLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation is under control and it's a greater risk to have monetary policy too tight than too loose, said the Riksbank's Lars E.O. Svensson, according to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary policy meeting minutes released Wednesday. (published 0816 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: ITALY AUG FINAL CPI REVISED DOWN TO +0.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian consumer prices increased slightly in August as the economy's weakness kept inflation contained, final data from statistics office Istat showed Wednesday. (published 0811 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AUSTRALIAN LEADING ECONOMIC INDEX IMPROVES IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian economy continues to shows signs of a solid recovery with a leading index of economic activity Wednesday showing strong improvement in July. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: MAY RAISE S KOREA SOVEREIGN RTGS IF ECONOMY IMPROVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service may raise its sovereign ratings for South Korea if the country's economy continues to improve, but any upgrade is unlikely over the next 12 months, an executive at the ratings firm said Wednesday. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    === DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY === \par  \par  \par WSJ(9/16) BERNANKE SEES END TO US RECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke said Tuesday that the recession was "very likely over," as consumers showed some of the first tangible signs of spending again. (published 0037 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR INDEX AT NEW LOW; EURO CLIMBS OVER $1.47 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further optimism over the global economic recovery pushed the dollar lower in Europe Wednesday, with its index falling to a new low of 2009 and the euro briefly rising over $1.4700. (published 0928 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE(9/16): IMF OUTLINES HOW TO END GOVT AID TO BANKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund, reinforcing its role in remaking the global economy, laid out detailed princ iples for scaling back government support of the financial industry over coming years. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =GEITHNER, REGULATORS TO TESTIFY ON FINCL MKT REGULATION REFORM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner and federal financial regulators will head to Capitol Hill in the coming weeks as lawmakers step up work on legislation to revamp regulation of the financial markets. (published 2202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS: NEW JAPAN FIN MIN FUJII MAY FOCUS ON ECON OVER DEBT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's just-appointed finance minister, Hirohisa Fujii, will likely emphasize in his new role taking steps to fix the country's economy, even if that means it will take longer to reduce Japan's massive public debt and possibly prompt even more new debt issuance. (published 0800 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INTERVIEW: RBA'S BROADBENT: CHINA'S RECOVERY SUSTAINABLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's rapid economic recovery is sustainable and bodes well for Australia, but a healing of the global economy is going to be a slow and mixed process, a board member of Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Wednesday. (published 0921 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: PULLING THE CARPET FROM UNDER THE POUND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling supporters must be losing faith fast. (published 0722 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: USD/JPY CONTINUES ITS DESCENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following are forecasts for Wednesday's trading session. They may, at times, be at odds with the three-day and weekly trends in the table above. (published 0646 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BUNDS, EURIBOR CAPPED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December bund and March euribor futures are considered to be capped now, following Tuesday's falls. Gilt futures may stay capped as well with only March short sterling futures continuing to make new all-time highs and remaining well bid. (published 0610 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CREDIT MARKETS: IT'S AS THOUGH LEHMAN NEVER HAPPENED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit markets were buoyed further Tuesday after retail sales and manufacturing data boosted some hopes that the end of the recession could be near. (published 2021 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: THE UK'S FISCAL OVERSTRETCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gordon Brown has finally acknowledged the need for U.K. public spending cuts. The debate now is not just over the details of those cuts but their timing. (published 2052 GMT) [ 16-09-09 1104GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090916e59g000h7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
