\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET SIGNALS-Asia and implications of an ugly dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs, Asia Financial Markets Editor   \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2009\par 10:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Sept 14 (Reuters) - The dollar's slide in the past week has prompted the usual chorus of reasons used to explain every such decline: the inflationary impact of the Federal Reserve's quantitative easing, which has been belatedly tagged to a rise in gold, and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves diversification.\par \par \uc2 Then there's the persistent equity rally and money still chasing risk. Funds leaving safe-haven money markets -- and thus dollar deposits -- have swelled to $285 billion so far this year. That's well over half the money committed to money market funds last year.\par \par \uc2 But what stands out is how steady the dollar drop has been. It slid broadly to a one-year low last week but there were no remarkable milestones in terms of the dollar's percentage drop against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's hard to see a case for G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless it turns more volatile. That said, Asia may soon face questions about how aggressively to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . South Korea is in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 GOLD GAINS ACROSS BOARD\par \par \uc2 For a chart on gold's rise in terms of the pound and Swiss franc, click on: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/sat46d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/sat46d}}}\par \par \uc2 This is not strictly a dollar story, so it's hard to hit the dollar alone with the inflation/quantitative easing stick.\par \par \uc2 The precious metal's latest push above $1,000 <XAU=> has unleashed a wave of commentary pinning big-picture reasons to the move. Beyond the technical nature of the move -- the break of a sideways triangle pattern in place for months -- other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen in tandem versus bullion.\par \par \uc2 So there is a shift towards gold generally (Asian central banks are rumoured to be behind the move, as always), which has coincidentally happened at a time when the dollar is vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 DOGGED BY DIVERSIFICATION\par \par \uc2 For a chart on the Hong Kong dollar's smaller dips during each Hang Seng pull-back: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/ner46d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/ner46d}}}\par \par \uc2 Diversification: It's always happening, but for some reason reserve diversification only makes the headlines when the dollar is falling.\par \par \uc2 If trade surpluses are starting to rise in Asia and hot money is coming in, then that would mean more reserves in need of rebalancing.\par \par \uc2 Looking at Hong Kong's Hang Seng <.HSI> and the HK dollar <HKD=D4> as a proxy of how inflows/outflows are unfolding, it does seem that any drop in stocks is seen as an opportunity to shovel some hot money back into the Hang Seng and Hong Kong dollar, with each bout of outflows weakening the Hong Kong dollar less.\par \par \uc2 And some of that money is probably spilling over into the Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> from the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  AND \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 For a graphic on the yen's trade-weighted rise and daily historical volatility: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/tyq46d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/tyq46d}}}\par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness has also raised the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a fair bit of focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the change of guard in government. Will the Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) live up to its stated intentions to have a hands-off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy even as the yen reaches a seven-month high?\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy is very weak and very dependent on trade at this point because any capital spending recovery is going to take time.\par \par \uc2 Yet Japan has many other considerations. A desire to have the yen play an even greater role in Asian trade is creeping up the agenda just as China promotes the yuan. The yen has always suffered because Japan never really pushed the yen as an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and long \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it.\par \par \uc2 But since 2004, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gone quiet -- even during last year's record yearly surge, market volatility and a green light from the G7 to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yen volatility is now falling even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens, making it hard to build a case for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight volatility.\par \par \uc2 So given recent history, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep surprising on this front by staying out of the market. In the past few years, there was even debate within \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on how it could reduce its reserves stockpile.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  AND KOREA\par \par \uc2 For a graphic on how the South Korean won's advance has stalled: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/ter46d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/ter46d}}}\par \par \uc2 But what of the rest of Asia? There has clearly been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, not a surprise as authorities rebuild reserves depleted last year to fight against the capital outflows. The big question is how aggressive they will get.\par \par \uc2 On that front, South Korea bears watching. The Bank of Korea signalled last week it is poised to raise interest rates in coming months, a move that would certainly lure more foreign funds.\par \par \uc2 Yet the won <KRW=> has had a tough time breaking beyond 1,220 to the dollar. A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Hong Kong described the Bank of Korea's efforts to stem the won's rise as, so far, "passive".\par \par \uc2 But local traders have not spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Will they get more aggressive? Keep an eye out. It could set the tone for how other countries approach \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARKET-SIGNALS/ASIA|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|J|Z|SI|IF|PHD|AUF|ND|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090914e59e000ig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
