\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Yen eyes 90 per dollar and stronger as flows shift\b0\par\par\uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2009\par 00:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Eyes on 90 yen near term, then longer term on 87 level\par \par \uc2  * Japan overseas investment may curb yen rise\par \par \uc2  * But capital outflows unlikely to be driver for yen fall  (Repeats item carried late on Friday)\par \par \uc2  By Charlotte Cooper and Masayuki Kitano\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 11 (Reuters) - Contrary to many expectations, the dollar is set to fall against the yen, not rise, and could revisit its 13-year low at 87 in the next half year.\par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a seven-month low near 90.65 yen <JPY=> on Friday and according to a Reuters poll of more than 60 strategists last week is set to reverse recent losses and climb against the yen to an average of 95 in a month, 98 in six-months and more than 100 in a year.\par \par \uc2   But they may have it wrong, some analysts say.\par \par \uc2   To begin with, the flow of overseas dividend payments being repatriated to Japan is set to pick up following tax changes. Importantly, interest in the yen as a funding vehicle has waned and is switching into the dollar instead.\par \par \uc2   Foreign investors are piling into Japan and a significant portion of overseas portfolio investments from Japan is going unhedged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, analysts say.\par \par \uc2   That all plays into the hands of yen bulls.\par \par \uc2   "There is a risk that we see it breaking 90, maybe in the coming month or so," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Japan at \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  He argues dividends repatriated by Japanese companies from overseas subsidiaries will pick up ahead of the fiscal half-year at the end of September following tax changes.\par \par \uc2  After that, broad dollar weakness, which has already seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slip to a one-year low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <.DXY>, could see it grind lower still.\par \par \uc2  While a rise in the yen to its 13-year peak around 87.10 reached in January would stir market jitters of official yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say the move will be slow and gradual, which would mitigate against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Indeed, the new ruling party, the Democratic Party of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prefers a strong yen anyhow.\par \par \uc2   Hirohisa Fujii, tipped to be named as the country's finance minister, told Reuters in an interview on Sept. 3 that "a strong yen is good for Japan" and in a July 16 interview said "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should not be moved artificially."\par \par \uc2  Analysts say it would be hard for the new administration to do more than smooth excessive volatility, as weakening the yen would cause diplomatic tensions when other economies are trying to recover from the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2  "I don't think there would be any reaction from the government or \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Sasaki said. "I do think Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has changed a lot in the past few years."\par \par \uc2  TO HEDGE, OR NOT TO HEDGE\par \par \uc2  Strategists at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  see the dollar falling to 88 yen by the end of the year as the dollar is increasingly used as a source of funds for investments elsewhere.\par \par \uc2  The yen has already received a leg up from foreign investors, who turned net buyers of Japanese shares in April this year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures show their net purchases were 2.45 trillion yen between April and August and analysts say they are probably not hedging against a falling yen as much as they did in the past, partly because the yen's yield disadvantage against the dollar and euro has decreased, making such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging less attractive.\par \par \uc2  Japanese institutional investors, though, are more wary after suffering at the hands of the dollar's slide by as much as 21 percent against the yen in the last five months of 2008. They prefer to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, thus limiting the selling pressure on the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data shows that categories of investors known for taking \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, including investors such as life insurers and investment trusts, bought a net 3.96 trillion yen in overseas securities between April and August, down from 4.37 trillion yen in the same year-earlier period.\par \par \uc2  (For graphics on overseas securities investment by Japanese investors and the dollar/yen rate, see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://graphics.thomsonreuters.com/099/JP_YEN20909.gif"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://graphics.thomsonreuters.com/099/JP_YEN20909.gif}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2  Such overseas investment is probably not generating as much yen-selling pressure as the numbers would suggest, said Yunosuke Ikeda, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Nomura Securities.\par \par \uc2  "Overseas securities investment has been led by life insurers and investment trusts recently, but life insurers are probably staying close to being fully hedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks," Ikeda said in a recent research note.\par \par \uc2  "So at this point, overseas investment by life insurers probably has very little impact to weaken the yen," he said.\par \par \uc2  To be sure, not everyone sees dollar/yen as a one-way bet. Longer term, concerns about a heavy public debt of some 170 percent of GDP could weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Citi analysts said in a note last week the recent dollar sell-off was a buying opportunity as markets normalised and fundamentals began to drive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more.  (Additional reporting by Satomi Noguchi: Editing by Neil Fullick)    \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN (ANALYSIS, REPEAT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|J|UKI|NAT|U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090916e59d001og\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
