\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  COLUMN-Dollar's summer holiday well and truly over\b0\par\par\uc2 866 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2009\par 16:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 -- Neal Kimberley is an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst for Reuters. The opinions expressed are his own --\par \par \uc2 By Neal Kimberley\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 9 (Reuters) - Labor Day is the traditional end of the U.S. driving season, and has also often been the signal for renewed volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The question is whether September 2009 will follow that trend. The early signs are promising. Gold bugs have led the way but the foreign exchanges have embraced the move and Uncle Sam's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is firmly on the back foot.\par \par \uc2 A couple of days' activity does not make a trend but the break-out from the recent relatively calm ranges is arguably a wake-up call.\par \par \uc2 Dollar bears feel empowered and the growing feeling that the markets are normalising is prompting risk-takers to take a fresh look at what this may mean. This may prove problematic for the value of the greenback.\par \par \uc2 For example, in the aftermath of the fall of Lehman, dollar demand was strong as the financial markets fled to the safe haven of the government bonds of the world's dominant reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those abnormal market conditions prompted dollar strength. Consequently, some investors are now wondering if normalising conditions presage renewed dollar weakness.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, someone has to pay the cost of the use of the printing press as central banks and governments around the world have acted decisively to address the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The United States was at the forefront of the policy responses, but with an unemployment rate still heading towards 10 percent, it is hard to see how it can be at the forefront of an exit policy anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke is well versed in the history of the Great Depression and will not wish to repeat the error of 1937 of premature Fed tightening.\par \par \uc2 At the same time European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Axel Weber has shown some cautious optimism, indicating on Tuesday that while it may be too early to call the end of the financial crisis, he feels the situation has improved.\par \par \uc2 If the market tunes into this vibe from the ECB, the euro could gain renewed traction on the topside with $1.4700 <EUR=>, or even $1.4900, as realistic short term targets. Looking more widely, if the U.S. comes to be perceived as the laggard in the G20 exit policy, then the dollar is a strong candidate to become the world's new funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some might argue that this phenomenon is already apparent as part of the reason behind the rise of the gold price <XAU=> in recent weeks.Also, three-month dollar Libor is now below yen Libor for the first time in 16 years.\par \par \uc2 In Japan itself, the incoming DPJ government has so far seemed somewhat indifferent to the prospect of a firming yen. The markets are usually good at reading signals and they will deliver a stronger yen given the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 With reports in the Japanese media that some exporters are lowering the semi-annual exchange rates on which they are budgeting, from 95 to 90 yen, any speculatively-led move through 91.50 yen <JPY=> could trigger a spiral of dollar sell orders.\par \par \uc2 At the same time with the yen currently relatively stable on the crosses, it would be much more palatable to Tokyo to paint any slide in dollar/yen as part of a broader dollar malaise rather than yen strength.\par \par \uc2 This would also help to fend off any internal pressure for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to authorise the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to initiate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Japanese have avoided \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a number of years and should choose to keep to that stance.\par \par \uc2 These dual scenarios do not even take into account what the United States might need itself as it too recognises that the near-death experience of the global financial system is coming to an end.\par \par \uc2 There is a cynical argument that the perfect way for U.S. President Barack Obama's team to systematically reduce the real burden of the immense issuance of U.S. paper is via a depreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The purchasing power of the dollar was systematically undermined throughout the 20th century even as it achieved global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominance.\par \par \uc2 In particular, at times of great economic distress, U.S. administrations have stood by, or even encouraged, a falling dollar. The examples of the Roosevelt, Nixon and Carter Presidencies spring to mind, while in the first term of the Clinton administration, Treasury Secretary Lloyd Bentsen used the dollar as a negotiating chip in his candid negotiations with the Hashimoto government in Japan.\par \par \uc2 The summer has been characterised by exchange rates staying in ranges, but as autumn approaches the markets are looking for the next big move. They may not get it, but there is a strong argument that normalising global markets may mean a generally lower dollar.\par \par \uc2 Dollar bears are not yet going into hibernation.\par \par \uc2 (Neal Kimberley, +44 207 542 4441)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-DOLLAR (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  COLUMN)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|RN