\par\par \b \uc2 SNAP ANALYSIS-Japan opposition DPJ win to break policy deadlock\b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2009\par 00:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (For more on Japan's election, click [ID:nPOLJP]\par \par \uc2  By Linda Sieg and Stanley White\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 31 (Reuters) - Japanese voters swept the opposition to a historic victory in an election on Sunday, ousting the long-ruling conservative party and handing the novice Democrats the job of reviving a struggling economy.  The Democrats have promised to revive the economy by putting more money in the hands of consumers and to prise policy away from bureaucrats as a way to reduce waste and redirect spending.\par \par \uc2  Here are some implications of the party's victory.\par \par \uc2  END OF LDP RULE, COALITION GOVERNMENT EXPECTED\par \par \uc2  * The Democratic Party's victory ends more than half a century of nearly unbroken rule by the business-friendly Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) and breaks a parliamentary deadlock created when the Democrats and its small allies won control of parliament's upper house in 2007, allowing them to stall bills.\par \par \uc2  * Democratic Party leader Yukio Hatoyama has said he would form a coalition with smaller allies even if his party won a majority because it needs their support in the upper house. But the Democrats' landslide win would limit the influence of the smaller parties.\par \par \uc2  * Hatoyama will be elected prime minister in a special session of parliament, but he is expected to want to have his cabinet up and running by Sept. 20 so he can attend a U.N. General Assembly meeting in New York shortly after.\par \par \uc2  POLICY DEADLOCK BROKEN, SPENDING CONCERNS\par \par \uc2  * Investors in Japan's stock market would generally welcome an end to the policy deadlock, although a Democratic victory may be largely priced into share prices already. [.T]\par \par \uc2  * Democratic Party policies are unlikely to threaten the economy's gradual recovery from its worst recession in 60 years since the party, like the LDP, has put priority on reviving the economy over fixing tattered state finances.\par \par \uc2  * Economists say the party's spending plans will likely give growth a small, but not necessarily sustainable, initial boost.\par \par \uc2  * The Democrats say they can fund spending plans mostly by cutting waste and redirecting spending, but analysts and bond market participants worry that their pledge not to raise sales tax from a current 5 percent for the next four years will inflate an already huge public debt, pushing up government bond yields. [JP/]\par \par \uc2  * The new government may also have to figure out ways to cover sliding tax revenues due to the recent recession.\par \par \uc2  * The Democrats have said they will scrap a record 52.7 trillion yen ($563 billion) ceiling on budget spending set by the government last June for the fiscal year from April 2010, and start the budget drafting process from scratch. They have also said they may halt and/or redirect some planned spending from a record 14 trillion yen extra budget already enacted for 2009/10.\par \par \uc2  MONETARY, \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  POLICIES\par \par \uc2  * The Democrats have said they would respect the independence of the Bank of Japan regarding monetary policy, but analysts say the party might lean on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep interest rates low if it sees a need to support a nascent recovery.\par \par \uc2  * The Democrats are unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's five-year absence from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2  DIPLOMACY, SECURITY\par \par \uc2  * The Democrats have pledged to adopt diplomatic and security policies more independent of Japan's closest ally, the United States, but have recently been signalling a pragmatic stance aimed at avoiding rows with Washington -- at least early on.\par \par \uc2  * Hatoyama and his party have also pledged to increase mutual trust with Asian neighbours such as China, ties with which have often been frayed by memories of Japan's wartime aggression.  ($1=93.64 Yen)  (Editing by Dean Yates and Rodney Joyce)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ELECTION/ (SNAP ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090830e58u000gs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
