\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Japan DPJ May Keep Policy Of Tolerating Yen Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,116 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2009\par 07:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Democratic Party of Japan's drive to jump-start domestic demand means it might tolerate a rising yen if it takes power this month, reflecting a view that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might eventually benefit the ailing economy.\par \par \uc2 DPJ officials, in interviews with Dow Jones Newswires, have indicated that while their party has no intention to guide the yen higher, it is more attuned to the benefits than to the drawbacks of a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That means that if the leading opposition party wins the Aug. 30 general election, as is widely expected, it will likely stay the course on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's five-year-old policy of refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market except in extreme circumstances.\par \par \uc2 While a strong yen would spell more pain for hard-hit exporters, holding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to help them would risk keeping Japan as reliant on overseas customers as ever, impeding the party's efforts to build a domestic-demand-led economy.\par \par \uc2 An appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could benefit Japan's resource-poor economy by making foreign goods and raw materials cheaper, giving consumers windfall savings and lowering costs for manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has tumbled more than 20% against the yen since June 2007 as the global financial crisis intensified, and while it's not clear if the yen will grind higher, a DPJ-led government likely wouldn't stand in the way if it did.\par \par \uc2 "Whether the yen is weak or strong, I think it's wrong to brush aside market trends and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's industries by moving exchange rates," said Hirohisa Fujii, a top adviser for the DPJ, who was finance minister from 1993 to 1994. "Only if speculation emerges, causing abnormal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, might there be exceptional cases where we would need to counter such developments."\par \par \uc2 Tsutomu Okubo, a former \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  managing director who is now a DPJ Upper House member, said there's a "consensus" among the party's financial-affairs specialists that market forces should largely set exchange rates and that only extreme situations would warrant government action.\par \par \uc2 The DPJ's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is a key issue for investors as it becomes more likely that the Japanese electorate, tired of the scandal- and gaffe-prone Liberal Democratic Party and the country's long economic malaise, will vote the DPJ into power. The LDP has governed Japan for all but 10 months since its establishment in 1955, and the DPJ already controls the less-powerful Upper House.\par \par \uc2 Support for the DPJ in the proportional-representation part of the ballot is 34.1%, nearly triple that of the LDP at just 13.3%, according to a Kyodo News Service poll conducted last weekend. The proportional representation vote picks 180 of the Lower House's 480 seats, while the rest of the MPs are directly elected.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Japan Catches Pneumonia When U.S. Sneezes \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A strong yen isn't painless for Japan. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., two of Japan's flagship exporters, cited a strong yen as a major factor behind big losses they posted in the quarter ended June 30.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the past, an export-damaging yen rise would have sparked heavy yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , whose powerful bureaucrats play a key role in setting Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. Tokyo sold an unprecedented Y35 trillion in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market during the 15 months through March 2004 in a bid to fend off a yen rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the ministry's view on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has changed over the years. Officials now believe that trying to drive down the yen to boost exports could hurt Japan's economy even more if demand for Japanese goods overseas falls - exactly what happened during the global slump that caused the economy to contract at a double-digit annualized rate in the two quarters to March 31.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That attitude was partly behind a decision to cease \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the spring of 2004. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't entered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since then, even when the dollar plunged to a 13-year low of Y87.10 Jan. 21, according to MOF data. The dollar was at Y95.81 around 0200 GMT Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That contrasts with several other Asian economies, such as South Korea, Thailand and Taiwan, where the authorities have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent months to slow the appreciation of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Finance Ministry's stance will likely remain a critical variable in Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy under a DPJ administration, given bureaucrats' knowledge and experience as well as their close relations with foreign authorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kan Suzuki, an Upper House lawmaker and senior member of the DPJ's policy research committee, said an increased emphasis on generating domestic demand rather than overseas sales is needed to make Japan's economy stronger.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Right now, we tend to catch a cold or even contract pneumonia if the U.S. or China just sneezes," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Naoki Minezaki, an Upper House member who has served several times as the DPJ's "shadow cabinet" finance minister, even thinks a gradual, market-driven rise in the yen is needed now. "That would enable people to buy more goods (from foreign nations) with the same amount of income. If consumers gain greater purchasing power, that could work positively for consumption," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Eisuke Sakakibara, a former top finance ministry \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bureaucrat who is expected by some observers to be given a government position if the DPJ takes power, also favors a strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "From now on, the issue of how to obtain raw materials will become very important" for Japan, as demand from China and other emerging nations is expected to boost prices of food, oil and commodities on world markets, Sakakibara said. "We are no longer in an era where a cheaper yen is always best."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even if the DPJ unexpectedly decides on a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy that aims to support exporters by weakening the yen, it wouldn't be easy for Tokyo to engineer a sustained drop in the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You can \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but before that, you better get a go-ahead from your (foreign government) counterparts," a senior government official told Dow Jones. "Unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is possible. However, if your counterparts in foreign governments openly complain about your action, that could spoil everything."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the U.S. and Europe appearing unenthusiastic about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at holding exchange rates down to help exporters, solo action by Japan could backfire.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7558; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-08-09 0521GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5008 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090812e58c00060\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
